// Teacher lecture scripts from the DKA_Teacher_Lectures_All180 document (days 13-25 written separately; Ashelyn’s 8th-grade questions are lesson-specific)
var DKA_SCRIPTS = {"1": "Who Is Jehovah? His Personal Name\nScripture: Ps 83:18; Ex 3:14-15; Is 42:8\nSong: #2 — Jehovah Is Your Name  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n[Hold up a flower, leaf, or piece of fruit. Hold it where every child can see it. Wait 10 full seconds of silence.]]\n“I want everyone to look at this. Really look at it. Look at the color. Look at the detail. Now I have a question — did this make itself?”\n[Let all children answer — even Mykah shakes his head no.]]\n“Of course not. Something this beautiful had to be made by someone. Today we are going to learn about the Someone who made everything you have ever seen, touched, tasted, heard, or felt. His name — His personal name — is Jehovah.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Open My Book of Bible Stories to Story 1 for Mykah. Older children open Bibles.]]\n“Jehovah is not a made-up name. It is not a title like God or Lord. It is His actual personal name — the name He chose for Himself. Let's say it together: JE — HO — VAH.”\n[Clap syllables three times.]]\n“Why does a name matter? Because when someone has a name, it means they are a real person. You can know them. You can talk to them. You can love them. Jehovah isn't a vague force somewhere in the universe. He is a Person — the greatest Person who has ever existed — and He wants us to know His name.”\n[Ask an older child to read Psalm 83:18 aloud slowly.]]\n“Most High over all the earth. There is no one more powerful, more wise, or more loving than Jehovah. And He knows your name just as surely as you know His.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Ask Kayla and Ryan to follow in Bibles. Mykah looks at pictures.]]\n“Jehovah didn't just give Himself a name — He explained what that name MEANS. Turn to Exodus 3:14.”\n[Read Exodus 3:14-15 aloud.]]\n“I Will Become What I Need to Become. When Israel needed food He became their Provider. When they needed protection He became their Warrior. When they were heartbroken He became their Comfort. He becomes exactly what each person needs, exactly when they need it.”\n[Look at Mykah:]]\n“Mykah, here is how I explain it for you: Jehovah is like the best parent in the whole universe, times a thousand. He always knows what you need before you even ask. And He always has enough to give.”\n[Ask Ashelyn to read Isaiah 42:8.]]\n“I am Jehovah. That is my name. He takes His name very seriously. His name is Jehovah, and He alone deserves our worship.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n[Directed at Ryan and Kayla — Ashelyn listens — Mykah absorbs.]]\n“In today's world, most people don't use Jehovah's name. Many Bible translations replaced it with LORD or GOD in capital letters. Billions of people have read the Bible their entire lives and never once seen the name Jehovah. Why? A Jewish tradition of not pronouncing the divine name was adopted by scholars, and over centuries translators followed it. By some estimates, Jehovah's name was removed over 6,000 times from the Christian Greek Scriptures alone. Now ask yourself: why would Jehovah include His name 7,000 times in the original scriptures — and be okay with people removing it? He wouldn't. This is one of the things that makes our faith distinctive. We know Jehovah personally. We use His name. We speak it with reverence and with love. And that personal relationship is the foundation of everything we believe.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nCan you say Jehovah's name for me? Good! Who made the flower I brought in today?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhat does the name Jehovah mean? Why does it matter that He has a personal name and not just a title like God or Lord?\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nIf someone said 'I don't think God has a personal name' — what one scripture would you use and what would you say?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nUsing Acts 17:22-31 as a framework, how would you reason with someone who says there is no personal God? What is your strongest logical argument?\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: All oral answers correct — celebrate warmly.\nAshelyn: He Causes to Become — a name makes a relationship personal vs. a title keeping God at a distance.\nKayla: Psalm 83:18 — 'you, whose name is Jehovah' — it is His personal name, not a title. Titles describe roles; names describe persons.\nRyan: Acts 17:24-28 — creation requires an intelligent cause; universal human longing for purpose and transcendence points to a Creator; conscience reflects a moral lawgiver — all three lead logically to a personal God who has a personal name.", "2": "Creation Days 1-3: Light, Sky, Land, Plants\nScripture: Gen 1:1-13; Ps 104:1-9\nSong: #11 — Creation Praises God  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n[Ask the whole room:]]\n“Look around this room. Name one thing you can see.”\n[Let children name things — chair, window, cup, etc.]]\n“Who made that?”\n[For man-made objects: a person made it. Then ask:]]\n'But who made the materials that person used?'\n[Keep tracing back until you reach:]]\n'Jehovah made the raw material of everything. Let's see how He did it — step by step.'\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Open Bibles to Genesis 1. Mykah has his picture book open to Story 2.]]\n“Genesis chapter 1 is one of the most beautiful chapters in the entire Bible — because it shows us a God who creates with purpose, order, and love. And after every single step, He looks at what He made and calls it GOOD. Let's find out what that means.”\n[Read Genesis 1:1-5 aloud together.]]\n“Day 1 — light. Before anything else, there was light. Why do you think light came first?”\n[Let children answer.]]\n“Because life cannot exist without light. Every other step depends on it. Jehovah's order is intentional — He is a God of wisdom, not randomness.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Continue reading Genesis 1:6-13 aloud, pausing after each day.]]\nAFTER DAY 2:\n“The sky and atmosphere — without it nothing can breathe. Jehovah prepared the environment before He put anything in it.' AFTER DAY 3:”\n[Ask an older child to read Psalm 104:1-9.]]\n“This psalm was written thousands of years after Genesis — and it praises Jehovah for exactly what Genesis describes. Creation wasn't an accident. It was worship-worthy from the very beginning.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n[Directed at Ryan and Kayla.]]\n“The Bible says Jehovah created in six creative days. But the Hebrew word for day — yom — can mean a 24-hour period OR a long period of time, an age, an epoch. In fact the Bible itself uses yom that way in other places. What the Bible insists on is the ORDER — and science actually confirms that order: light first, then atmosphere, then dry land, then vegetation. That order is exactly right. Jehovah's Word and the natural world agree. Ryan — your assignment is to research this further using the Creation articles on jw.org.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nWhat did Jehovah make on Day 1? What did He make on Day 3? Can you show me with your hands — grow like a plant!\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhy do you think Jehovah made things in a specific order instead of all at once? What does that tell you about His personality?\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nIf someone said the Bible contradicts science because creation happened in six literal 24-hour days — how would you respond using what we just learned?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nWhat is the Hebrew word for 'day' in Genesis 1? Research on wol.jw.org what Jehovah's organization says about the length of the creative periods. What is their conclusion?\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Day 1: light. Day 3: land, seas, plants. Any movement activity is correct.\nAshelyn: Order shows wisdom — Jehovah is not random or careless; He prepared the environment before filling it; each step served a purpose for the next.\nKayla: The Hebrew yom can mean an age or epoch; the focus of Genesis 1 is the ORDER, not the duration; science confirms the sequence; our position is that Jehovah created over long creative periods.\nRyan: Yom — the organization teaches that the creative days were long epochs, not 24-hour periods; this is consistent with both the Bible text and scientific observation of the natural world.", "3": "Creation Days 4-6: Sun, Stars, Living Creatures\nScripture: Gen 1:14-25; Job 38:4-13,31-33\nSong: #6 — The Heavens Declare Gods Glory  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“Tell me — what is the most amazing animal you have ever seen in real life or in a video?”\n[Let every child answer — celebrate each one.]]\n“Who designed that animal? Where did its instincts come from? Who told the salmon to swim upstream against the current — every time, without fail? Nobody taught it. It just knows.”\n[Pause.]]\n“Today we meet the One who designed every creature on this planet.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 1:14-19 together.]]\n“Day 4 — the sun, moon, and stars were made to serve humanity. For seasons. For years. For signs. Every star you see at night is there to help Jehovah's people mark time. Then look at Day 5 — fish and birds. Day 6 — land animals. And finally, human beings.”\n[Pause.]]\n“After each step, Jehovah says GOOD. Not just that He liked it. It means each thing was perfectly suited to its purpose. Nothing Jehovah made was careless or accidental.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Open to Job 38:4-13.]]\n“Now we are going to read something remarkable. This is Jehovah speaking directly to a man named Job. Listen carefully to the questions He asks.”\n[Read Job 38:4-13 aloud.]]\n“Why is Jehovah asking Job these questions? Is He being harsh?”\n[Let children respond.]]\n“No — He is showing Job how much wisdom and power exist in the creation Job has been taking for granted every single day. Look — Jehovah designed the stars (Job 38:31-33). He set the boundaries of the sea. He controls the weather. He designed animal instincts. One chapter of Job covers what scientists spend entire careers studying.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n[Directed at Ryan and Kayla.]]\n“Job 38-39 covers astronomy, weather patterns, ocean boundaries, and animal instinct. Ryan — choose one of those domains. Research on jw.org how the complexity in that one area alone points to an intelligent Designer. Write about it. Kayla — I want you to look up the word intelligent design in the Research Guide on wol.jw.org and find one article on how creation points to Jehovah.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nPoint to something in the room and say: Jehovah made the materials for THAT! Can you tell me the names of three animals Jehovah made?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nJob 38 asks: 'Where were you when I laid the foundation of the earth?' What is Jehovah communicating through these questions? What should our response be?\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nPick one animal from Day 5 or Day 6. Research its design on jw.org. What is one design feature of that animal that points to an intelligent Creator?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nWrite 5-7 sentences responding to the argument that life could arise from nonliving chemicals without a Creator. Use at least two scriptures and one jw.org source.\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: All oral answers correct.\nAshelyn: Jehovah is communicating that His wisdom and power infinitely exceed human understanding — our response should be humility, not shame or defensiveness, and trust in the One who designed everything.\nKayla: Accept any well-researched animal — key quality: specificity. Mykah did not design his own eyes, so Someone did.\nRyan: The complexity of even one protein or one cell requires more information than random chance can produce; Ps 19:1 says the heavens declare Jehovah's glory; Heb 3:4 — every house is built by someone, and the one who built all things is Jehovah.", "4": "Adam and Eve — The Crown of Creation\nScripture: Gen 1:26-2:25; Ps 8:3-5; Ps 37:29; Is 45:18; Rev 21:3-4\nSong: #5 — Gods Wondrous Works  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n[Ask all children to look at their own hands.]]\n“Look at your hand. Look at the fingerprint on your fingertip. No one in the history of the entire human race has the exact same fingerprint as you. Not one. Not ever. Seven billion people on earth today and not one fingerprint is identical to yours. Who made you that way — and why?”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 1:26-27 aloud.]]\n“In God's image. Not the animals. Not the angels. Human beings are made in the image of Jehovah God Himself. That means we share some of Jehovah's qualities — the ability to love deeply, to think creatively, to make moral choices, to appreciate beauty, to reason, to plan. No animal can do all of those things.”\n[Read Genesis 2:7.]]\n“Jehovah breathed life directly into Adam. That is different from how He made everything else. Adam received the breath of life personally from Jehovah. Every human being is uniquely loved by the God who made them.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Continue with Genesis 2:8-25.]]\n“Jehovah didn't put Adam in a bare empty world. He put him in a beautiful garden — the Garden of Eden. And He gave Adam meaningful work: cultivate and guard it. Work in Jehovah's plan is not a punishment. Even in paradise, humans were meant to have purpose and responsibility.”\n[Read Genesis 2:18-22.]]\n“Jehovah noticed that Adam was alone — and He cared. He made Eve not from dust like the animals, not from Adam's head like his superior or his foot like his inferior, but from his rib — his side. His equal and his partner.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n[Directed at Ryan and Kayla — Ashelyn listens — Mykah absorbs.]]\n“Here is something beautiful. Look at Psalm 37:29, Isaiah 45:18, and Revelation 21:3-4.”\n[Read each.]]\n“What do these three scriptures have in common? Paradise earth. Righteous will inherit the earth. Jehovah formed the earth to be inhabited. Jehovah will dwell with humans. What does this mean? It means paradise earth is not plan B — it was never a backup plan. It was always Jehovah's original and eternal purpose for this planet. Satan's rebellion did not derail the plan. Jehovah simply implemented a rescue operation through Jesus.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\n[Hand Mykah a small mirror.] Look at yourself. Jehovah made YOU and He loves you. Can you say: 'Jehovah made me and I am special to Him'?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhat does 'made in God's image' mean? Name three specific qualities that humans share with Jehovah that animals do not have.\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nConnect Psalm 37:29, Isaiah 45:18, and Revelation 21:3-4 in two or three sentences. What do they tell us together about Jehovah's original and ultimate purpose for the earth?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nJehovah gave Adam meaningful work in paradise even before sin entered. What does this teach us about the purpose of human activity? Write a short essay connecting this to our lives today.\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Any sincere response — celebrate it warmly.\nAshelyn: Ability to love and form deep relationships; ability to make moral choices and understand right from wrong; creative thinking and artistic expression; ability to know and relate to Jehovah personally.\nKayla: Paradise earth is Jehovah's original and ultimate purpose — Ps 37:29 (righteous inherit the earth) + Is 45:18 (earth formed to be inhabited) + Rev 21:3-4 (Jehovah will dwell with humans). It was never plan B.\nRyan: Work gives purpose, satisfaction, and reflection of Jehovah's own creative nature; even in a perfect world humans needed meaningful activity; today our ministry, family responsibilities, and craftsmanship all reflect this design.", "5": "Week 1 Review + Presentations + Friday Song Menu\nScripture: Review: Ps 83:18; Gen 1-2; Job 38\nSong: #0 — Child's Choice — See Friday Song Menu  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“Today belongs to you. You have spent four days learning about the greatest Person in the universe — who He is, what He made, and why He made it. Now it's your turn to tell ME what you learned. We are going to hear from every single person in this room today.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Morning Worship: Let each child choose a song from the Week 1 menu — Songs 2, 11, 6, 5, or any other they love. Sing together.]]\n[Review quiz — ask these questions to the whole room and let any age answer:]]\n'1. What is Jehovah's personal name? 2. What does His name mean in Hebrew? 3. How many creative periods did He use to prepare the earth? 4. What does made in God's image mean? 5. What did Jehovah give Adam and Eve their first day — hint: starts with P.'\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Mykah: Share drawings from the week. Narrate each one.]]\n[Ashelyn: Read your best written response from this week aloud.]]\n[Kayla: Read the first paragraph of your weekly essay.]]\n[Ryan: Present Portrait of Jehovah — all 8 scriptures, what each one teaches. Family may ask 2 questions.]\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n[After presentations, ask the whole family:]]\n“What is the ONE thing about Jehovah that you want to remember from this week — even 10 years from now?”\n[Go around the room. Every person answers. Write them down. Read them back at year-end on Day 180.]\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nShare your drawings! Tell us what Jehovah made in each one.\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nRead your best written sentence from this week. Then tell us: what surprised you most in your research?\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nWhat scripture from this week do you want to memorize? Say it from memory as far as you can.\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nPresent your Portrait of Jehovah. Tell us: which scripture was hardest to explain in your own words — and why?\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: All sharing responses correct and celebrated.\nAshelyn: Accept any genuine reflection.\nKayla: Accept any sincere scripture attempt — encourage them no matter how much they remember.\nRyan: Portfolio check: 8 scriptures, 2 sentences each, entirely in own words, MLA header.", "6": "The Garden of Eden — Jehovah's Perfect Design\nScripture: Gen 2:8-17; Ps 37:29; Is 45:18\nSong: #12 — Great God Jehovah  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“If Jehovah said to you right now — I am going to design your perfect home. What would it have?”\n[Let everyone answer — mention nature, animals, no sickness, good food.]]\n“Jehovah already designed exactly that. And He put it right here on this earth. Today we find out what it looked like.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 2:8-14 together.]]\n“Four rivers flowing from one river. A garden that watered itself. Gold, precious stones, lush trees, every kind of fruit. This was not a simple park — this was a masterpiece. Jehovah designed it specifically for the people He loved.”\n[Ask:]]\n'What does this tell you about what Jehovah thinks of human beings?'\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 2:15-17 together.]]\n“One restriction. Just one. Out of an entire paradise — one tree. And Jehovah explained the reason: eating from it would bring death. This was not a harsh rule. It was a loving warning from a Father who knew what would happen.”\n[Ask:]]\n'Why do good parents set boundaries even in a happy home?'\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n“Jehovah's arrangement from the beginning was that humans would care for and develop the earth — forever. The original purpose was never for humans to go to heaven. Psalm 37:29 and Isaiah 45:18 both confirm: the earth is humanity's home, and righteousness will fill it. Paradise is the plan — not the backup plan.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nWhat are three things that were in the Garden of Eden? Which one would you love most?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhy did Jehovah give Adam and Eve meaningful work in paradise? What does that tell us about what life in the new world will be like?\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nConnect Genesis 2:15 (cultivate and guard) with Revelation 21:3-4 (no more death or pain). What will work look like in paradise?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nResearch on jw.org: What is Jehovah's stated purpose for the earth in Isaiah 45:18? How does the ransom make the fulfillment of that purpose possible?\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Trees, fruit, animals, rivers, gold — any three are correct.\nAshelyn: Meaningful work gives purpose and dignity; paradise will not be idleness but joyful, productive activity without pain or futility.\nKayla: Work in paradise will be purposeful, creative, and fulfilling — maintaining a beautiful planet without curse, pain, or failure.\nRyan: Is 45:18 — formed earth to be inhabited, not in vain; the ransom removes the penalty of sin that was the barrier to paradise; Jesus' death makes it possible for Jehovah's original purpose to be fully realized.", "7": "Angels — Jehovah's Heavenly Family\nScripture: Job 1:6; Heb 1:7,14; Rev 12:7-9; Dan 7:10\nSong: #1 — Jehovahs Attributes  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“Before any human being existed — before Adam, before the Garden — Jehovah was not alone. He had a family. And that family was enormous.”\n[Pause.]]\n“Today we meet the angels — Jehovah's heavenly sons.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Job 1:6 and Hebrews 1:14.]]\n“Angels are spirit creatures — beings made by Jehovah before the physical creation. They are called sons of God in the Hebrew scriptures. They are real persons with names, personalities, and feelings. They are not chubby babies with wings. They are powerful, intelligent beings who love Jehovah and serve Him.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Read Daniel 7:10 — 100 million angels.]]\n“A hundred million angels standing before Jehovah's throne. And Hebrews 1:14 says they are sent out to serve people who will inherit salvation. That means angels are involved in YOUR life right now.”\n[Read Revelation 12:7-9.]]\n“Michael the archangel leads Jehovah's heavenly forces. When war broke out in heaven — Jehovah's side won. That is the world we live in now — a world where Jehovah's sovereignty is the central issue.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n“Here is a deep question: why did Jehovah create beings who have free will and could rebel? Why not just create loyal, obedience-only robots?”\n[Discuss.]]\n“Because love requires freedom. Loyalty that is forced is not loyalty — it is compulsion. Jehovah wants genuine love and service from His creatures. This is why both angels and humans were given the freedom to choose. And it is why our choice to serve Jehovah means something real.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nHow many angels does the Bible say are near Jehovah? Point to a big number! What is one thing angels do to help us?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhy are angels called 'spirits' and what does that mean? What is one way angels interact with human beings according to the Bible?\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nWhy did Jehovah give angels free will if He knew some would rebel? What does this tell us about the kind of love and worship Jehovah wants from us?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nResearch on wol.jw.org: What is the difference between Michael the archangel and other angels? What scriptures identify Michael? Write a paragraph.\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: 100 million — very many; angels protect us and help Jehovah's people.\nAshelyn: Spirit beings have no physical body; angels can take on physical form temporarily (Gen 18); they deliver messages, protect, serve.\nKayla: Love requires genuine freedom; forced loyalty has no value; Jehovah wants love that comes from understanding His qualities and choosing Him — this is why our service is precious to Him.\nRyan: Michael appears in Dan 10:13,21; 12:1; Jude 9; Rev 12:7 — identified as a prominent angel, archangel means chief angel; the organization teaches Michael is the pre-human Jesus.", "8": "Satan — How Rebellion Began in Heaven\nScripture: Rev 12:9; John 8:44; Ezek 28:12-17; Is 14:12-15\nSong: #32 — Take Sides With Jehovah  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“Has anyone ever been lied to by someone you trusted? How did it feel?”\n[Let children respond briefly.]]\n“The very first lie ever told was told by the most intelligent creature Jehovah had created up to that point. Today we find out who that creature was — and how he became Jehovah's enemy.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Ezekiel 28:12-15.]]\n“Ezekiel is describing a spirit creature who was originally perfect — full of wisdom, beautiful, in Jehovah's garden. This was an angelic son of Jehovah who had everything.”\n[Read verse 15-17.]]\n“What happened? He became proud. He began to look at his own beauty and wisdom and began to desire worship for himself instead of giving worship to Jehovah.”\n[Read Isaiah 14:13-14.]]\n“I will make myself like the Most High. He wanted to replace Jehovah.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Read John 8:44.]]\n“Jesus himself identifies this creature as a liar — and says he did not stand in the truth. He walked away from the truth of who Jehovah is and what He deserves. And then he brought his rebellion to earth.”\n[Read Revelation 12:9.]]\n“The one called Satan — a word that means Resister — and Devil — which means Slanderer — is the same one who was once a perfect angel. Pride, covetousness, and deception are the pattern of his fall — and they are the pattern of temptation he still uses today.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n“Why didn't Jehovah immediately destroy Satan? Because a lie had been raised: that Jehovah's rulership was unnecessary and that humans could successfully run their own lives without Him. That was the issue in Eden. Destroying Satan immediately would not prove the lie wrong — it would look like silencing an opponent. Instead, Jehovah allowed time for human history to show whether life outside His guidance leads to blessing or ruin. Look at history. Look at today's world. The answer is clear.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nWho is Satan? He was first an angel who became... what? What did he want?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhat three steps led to Satan's rebellion? (Hint: look at Isaiah 14 and Ezekiel 28.)\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nWhy didn't Jehovah immediately destroy Satan after he rebelled? What important issue had to be settled first?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nResearch on jw.org: What is the 'issue of universal sovereignty'? How does this explain why human suffering has been permitted for thousands of years? Write a paragraph.\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Satan was an angel who wanted people to worship him instead of Jehovah.\nAshelyn: 1. Pride (looked at own beauty/wisdom). 2. Covetousness (desired worship). 3. Deception (lied to Eve to get followers).\nKayla: The lie Satan raised was that Jehovah's sovereignty was not necessary — that life without Him could be successful. This had to be tested and proven false over time — destroying Satan immediately would not settle the question.\nRyan: Universal sovereignty issue: Satan's challenge was whether Jehovah has the right to rule and whether His subjects truly need His guidance; human history is demonstrating the answer; Jehovah's kingdom will vindicate His name and sovereignty permanently.", "9": "The Temptation and the Fall\nScripture: Gen 3:1-7; 2 Cor 11:3; Rom 5:12; 1 Tim 2:13-14\nSong: #36 — We Guard Our Hearts  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“Has anyone ever been told something that wasn't true — and believed it at first? Maybe an advertisement that promised something amazing but the product was terrible? Or a friend who said something was safe when it wasn't?”\n[Let children respond.]]\n“Today we are going to study the most effective deception in human history. Three lies. Six words. And it changed everything.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 3:1-3 slowly.]]\n“Notice the serpent's first move — he distorts what Jehovah actually said. Jehovah said do not eat from ONE tree. The serpent made it sound like the restriction was much larger than it was. This is the first tactic: exaggerate the restriction to make the Lawgiver look unreasonable.”\n[Read verse 4.]]\n“You will certainly not die. This is the second tactic: directly deny what Jehovah said. This is where lying was born.”\n[Read verse 5.]]\n“Jehovah knows that when you eat it your eyes will be opened. The third tactic: suggest Jehovah is withholding something good. Make the restriction look selfish instead of loving.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 3:6-7.]]\n“Eve saw, she desired, she took, she ate, she gave. Five verbs in rapid succession. What started as deception ended in action. And then her eyes were opened — but not the way Satan promised. They were opened to their own exposure, their own guilt, their own need to hide from Jehovah.”\n[Read Romans 5:12.]]\n“Through one man — Adam — sin entered the world. And death through sin. Every human being born since then has been born imperfect. This was not Jehovah's fault. But it became His greatest project to fix.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n“Notice what Eve did not do: she did not go to Jehovah and ask Him to explain the restriction. She went to a conversation with someone who contradicted Jehovah and entertained it. This is the model Satan still uses — he creates doubt first, then denial, then an alternative truth that sounds better. Today his tactics come through entertainment, education systems, and peer pressure. The defense is the same: go directly to Jehovah first, not to those who contradict Him.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nWhat three things did the snake say to trick Eve? Can you remember even one?\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhat were the three tactics Satan used in his temptation of Eve? Give an example of how each one is used in the world today.\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nEve 'saw that the tree was good for food, delightful to look at, and desirable to gain understanding.' Compare this to 1 John 2:16. What principle does this reveal?\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nResearch: How does 2 Corinthians 11:3 and Revelation 12:9 identify the serpent in Eden? What method does Satan still use? Write a response connecting the Eden account to a modern example.\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Any recollection of one tactic correct — celebrate.\nAshelyn: 1. Exaggerate the restriction. 2. Directly deny Jehovah's word. 3. Suggest Jehovah is withholding something good. Modern examples: many valid — media, peer pressure, academic arguments against the Bible.\nKayla: The desire of the flesh, the desire of the eyes, and the showy display of one's means of life — 1 John 2:16 matches the three things that attracted Eve exactly.\nRyan: 2 Cor 11:3 — the serpent led Eve astray; Rev 12:9 — the great serpent = Satan; his method is still: cast doubt, deny Jehovah's word, offer an alternative that appeals to selfishness.", "10": "Week 2 Review + The Seed Promise\nScripture: Gen 3:8-24; Gen 3:15; Gal 3:16; Rev 12:17\nSong: #30 — My Father God And Friend  —  Play BEFORE beginning the lecture\n\n\n🎣  HOOK — Opening Activity (3–5 minutes)\nPurpose: Create curiosity and engagement in every child before a word of instruction is spoken. Mykah must be engaged here.\n\n\n“In the middle of the worst moment in human history — when Adam and Eve had just thrown away everything Jehovah had given them — Jehovah gave them something they didn't deserve and couldn't have expected. He gave them hope. Today we find out what that hope was.”\n\n\n🌱  FOUNDATION — Layer 1 & 2 — All Ages (7–8 minutes)\nPurpose: Simple, clear, beautiful truth that a 10-year-old can understand and a 17-year-old can appreciate.\n\n\n[Read Genesis 3:15.]]\n“This is the very first prophecy in the entire Bible. A seed that would crush the serpent's head. Jehovah made this promise the same day sin entered the world. Before He pronounced any consequences — He gave them hope. That is who Jehovah is.”\n\n\n📚  BUILDING — Layer 3 — Scriptures & Discussion (10–12 minutes)\nPurpose: Open Bibles together. Read scriptures aloud. Discuss. Mykah listens and absorbs.\n\n\n[Read Galatians 3:16 — the seed is Christ.]]\n“Paul identifies this seed as Jesus Christ. The promise made in the garden — Genesis 3:15 — was fulfilled 4,000 years later through Jesus. The Bible is one story with one Author. Every piece connects.”\n[Read Revelation 12:17.]]\n“The war between the serpent's offspring and the woman's seed — that war is still happening. And we are in the middle of it. We are part of the woman's offspring.”\n\n\n🔬  DEPTH — Layer 4 — Ryan & Kayla (8–10 minutes)\nPurpose: Deeper theological content. Ashelyn listens. Mykah absorbs. Directed at the two oldest.\n\n\n“Genesis 3:15 is the seed from which the entire rest of the Bible grows. Every covenant, every prophecy, every event in the Hebrew scriptures points toward this seed. Isaiah 53, Daniel 9:25, Micah 5:2 — they all zoom in on exactly who this seed would be and how he would fulfill the promise. Ryan and Kayla — your assignment is to trace this prophecy. Find three scriptures between Genesis 3:15 and the Gospels that point to Jesus. Due Friday.”\n\n\n❓  APPLICATION QUESTIONS — One Per Level\nAsk in order youngest to oldest. Allow a full 30-60 seconds for each person to answer. Do not rush.\n\n\nMykah — 4th Grade • Level 1\nWho is the Seed that Jehovah promised would crush the serpent? His name starts with J!\n\n\nAshelyn — 8th Grade • Level 2-3\nWhat did Jehovah promise in Genesis 3:15? How do we know it refers to Jesus? Find one New Testament scripture that confirms it.\n\n\nKayla — 11th Grade • Level 4J\nTrace the Seed Promise: Gen 3:15 → Gal 3:16 → Rev 12:17. Write two sentences explaining the full arc of what Jehovah promised and how it will be fulfilled.\n\n\nRyan — 12th Grade • Level 4A\nBuild a research chain: Gen 3:15 → Insight on the Scriptures: 'Seed' entry → three messianic prophecies in Isaiah and Micah → NT fulfillment. Write one paragraph on what you find.\n\n\n\n\n✅  ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jesus!\nAshelyn: Seed = the promised one who would crush Satan's power; Gal 3:16 confirms it is Christ specifically.\nKayla: In Eden Jehovah promised a future seed who would undo the damage of Satan's rebellion; Gal 3:16 identifies that seed as Jesus; Rev 12:17 shows the war is ongoing and we are participants.\nRyan: Insight 'Seed' entry discusses the line of promise; Is 53, Is 9:6, Mic 5:2 — accept well-sourced research.", "11": "Jehovah's Judgment and First Consequences\nScripture: Gen 3:8-24; Rom 8:20-22\nSong #130  \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Have you ever done something wrong and tried to hide it? What happened when the truth came out?' TEACH: Read Gen 3:8-13. Adam hid, Eve blamed the serpent. Consequences followed — not as revenge but as natural outcomes of sin. The ground cursed = toil. Childbirth painful = physical toll. Death = sin's wage. None of this was cruel — it was honest. SAY: 'Jehovah warned Adam. The warning was loving. The consequence was real. That is what truth and love look like together.'\n🌱 FOUNDATION + BUILDING + DEPTH\nKey points: 1. Jehovah sought Adam out — 'Where are you?' Jehovah initiates even after sin. 2. Each received a specific consequence tied to their specific action. 3. Jehovah made garments of skin — the first sacrifice, foreshadowing the ransom. 4. Banishment from the garden was protective — if they ate from the tree of life in their sinful state they would live forever in misery.\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jehovah make to cover Adam and Eve? Who helped them?\nAshelyn: What three consequences did Jehovah give — one for the serpent, one for Eve, one for Adam? What was the purpose of each?\nKayla: Why did Jehovah banish Adam and Eve from the garden? Was it cruel or was it protective? Explain with a scripture.\nRyan: How does Gen 3:21 (garments of skin) connect to Heb 9:22 and the ransom? Write a 5-sentence theological explanation.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Garments of skin — Jehovah made them.\nAshelyn: Serpent: crawl on belly, enmity with woman's seed. Eve: pain in childbirth, desire for husband. Adam: toil, thorns, return to dust. Each tied to their action.\nKayla: Banishment was protective — eating from the tree of life in a sinful state would mean living forever in an imperfect condition; Gen 3:22-24 supports this — Jehovah was not punishing them but protecting them from a worse outcome.\nRyan: Garments = first animal death; blood shed to cover shame; Heb 9:22 — no forgiveness without blood; this was a shadow pointing forward to Jesus' ransom.", "12": "Cain and Abel — Two Kinds of Worship\nScripture: Gen 4:1-16; Heb 11:4; 1 John 3:12\nSong #108  \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Has anyone ever worked hard at something and felt like it wasn't appreciated — while someone else got more recognition? How did that feel?' TEACH: Cain and Abel both brought offerings. One was accepted — one wasn't. The difference was not the TYPE of offering alone — it was the FAITH behind it (Heb 11:4) and likely the QUALITY (Abel brought firstlings — the best). Ask: What does it mean to give Jehovah your BEST vs. what is convenient?\n🌱 FOUNDATION + BUILDING + DEPTH\nKey points: 1. Cain knew what was right — Jehovah warned him in Gen 4:6-7. 2. The anger came before the act — Jehovah gave him a chance to redirect it. 3. 'Sin is crouching at the door' — sin is not just action; it starts as a feeling we can still choose how to handle. 4. Cain asked 'Am I my brother's guardian?' — contrasting with true love which DOES look out for others.\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Cain do that was wrong? What could he have done instead?\nAshelyn: What was the difference between Cain's offering and Abel's? What made Abel's offering more acceptable according to Hebrews 11:4?\nKayla: What warning did Jehovah give Cain before the murder? What does 'sin is crouching at the door' mean in practical terms for us today?\nRyan: Research on wol.jw.org: What was the mark Jehovah put on Cain? What does this reveal about Jehovah's character even in judgment?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: He killed his brother instead of doing better — he could have improved his offering.\nAshelyn: Abel brought firstlings of his flock and fat portions — the best; Heb 11:4 says Abel offered by FAITH — the quality reflected the faith behind it.\nKayla: Warning: sin is crouching at the door; you can still master it. Practical: when anger or jealousy rises, we must recognize it before it leads to action.\nRyan: Mark was a sign of protection — Jehovah showed mercy even to a murderer; connects to Ex 34:6 — slow to anger, merciful, loyal love.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nUNITS 2-9 — WEEKS 5-36: ALL REMAINING LECTURES\nDays 21–180  •  Complete Teaching Outlines with Full Application Questions", "26": "Abram's Call — Leave Everything\nScripture: Gen 12:1-9; Heb 11:8-10\nSong #28 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'If Jehovah told you today to pack up, leave your home and family, and go somewhere He hasn't told you yet — would you go? Be honest.' Teach: That is exactly what Abram did. And he is called the friend of Jehovah.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Ur of the Chaldees was wealthy and sophisticated — Abram left comfort. 2. Heb 11:8 — he went WITHOUT knowing where. 3. He set up an altar at each stop — kept Jehovah first. Ask: What does it mean to 'set up an altar' in your everyday life?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Abram go when Jehovah called him?\nAshelyn: Heb 11:8 says Abram obeyed 'without knowing where he was going.' What kind of trust does that require?\nKayla: What does it mean that Abram was called 'the friend of Jehovah' (Is 41:8; James 2:23)? What did he do to earn that title?\nRyan: How does Abraham's faith in Gen 12 compare to the faith required in Heb 11:1? Write a definition of faith using Abraham's example.", "27": "Abram in Canaan — Living by Faith\nScripture: Gen 12:10-13:18\nSong #31 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'What do you do when you're following Jehovah's direction and things get hard — do you keep going or look for a shortcut?' Teach: Abram went to Egypt during a famine — a shortcut that created problems. He lied about Sarai. Jehovah protected them despite Abram's fear.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Going to Egypt was not Jehovah's direction — Abram acted on fear. 2. Despite Abram's mistake Jehovah protected Sarai and blessed Abram. 3. After Egypt Abram returned to where he had first built the altar. 4. Lot chose by sight; Abram chose by faith. Ask: Lot 'saw' the plain was beautiful — how do we make decisions: by sight or by faith?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who traveled with Abram? What happened when they got too big to share land?\nAshelyn: How did Lot choose his land? How did Abram choose? What does this reveal about their priorities?\nKayla: Abram made a fear-based decision in Egypt. What can we learn about recovering from fear-based choices?\nRyan: Compare 2 Cor 5:7 (walk by faith, not sight) with Lot's choice in Gen 13. Write a paragraph on what this means for decision-making.", "28": "Jehovah's Covenant With Abraham\nScripture: Gen 15:1-21; 17:1-27; Gal 3:8\nSong #30 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'What is the most unbreakable promise you have ever made or received? What made it so binding?' Teach: In Gen 15 Jehovah made a covenant — literally walked between cut animal pieces — the most binding ancient agreement form. This is how serious He was about His promise to Abraham.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Count the stars — so shall your offspring be. 2. Deep sleep — Jehovah passed through the pieces ALONE. This meant He alone bore responsibility for the covenant. 3. Gal 3:8 — the gospel was declared in advance to Abraham: all nations will be blessed through you. 4. Circumcision = sign of the covenant (Gen 17). Ask: Jehovah bore the full covenant responsibility alone — what does that tell you about His commitment to His promises?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many stars are in the sky? How many children did Jehovah promise Abraham?\nAshelyn: What was the significance of the smoking fire pot and flaming torch passing between the animal pieces?\nKayla: How does Gal 3:8 connect the Abrahamic covenant to the gospel? What was already promised to Abraham about Jesus?\nRyan: Research on wol.jw.org: What are the terms of the Abrahamic covenant? How was it partially fulfilled in Israel and ultimately through Christ?", "29": "Three Visitors — Abraham's Hospitality\nScripture: Gen 18:1-22; Heb 13:2\nSong #100 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Has anyone ever entertained a guest and found out later it was someone much more important than you realized?' Teach: Abraham welcomed three strangers — two were angels; one was Jehovah Himself manifested through an angel.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Abraham ran to meet the strangers — he didn't wait. 2. He offered the best he had immediately. 3. Heb 13:2 — some entertained angels without knowing it. 4. Jehovah shared His plan to destroy Sodom with Abraham — 'he is my friend.' Ask: What does it mean that Jehovah shared His plans with Abraham? What kind of relationship had they built?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many visitors came to Abraham? Who were they?\nAshelyn: Why did Jehovah choose to tell Abraham about Sodom? What does this say about the friendship between them?\nKayla: Abraham ran, bowed, and offered his best to strangers. How does this contrast with the casual, self-serving hospitality we often see today?\nRyan: James 2:23 calls Abraham 'the friend of Jehovah.' Based on Gen 18, what specific behaviors built that friendship? Write a paragraph.", "30": "Week 6 Review\nScripture: Gen 12-18\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nReview: Abraham's call, choices by faith vs. sight, the covenant, hospitality. Quiz: What does it mean to walk by faith?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nFull review, presentations, and family discussion: What is one thing Abraham did this week that you want to imitate?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Share your drawing!\nAshelyn: Present personal project.\nKayla: Read essay paragraph.\nRyan: Present research paper.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: All celebrated.\nAshelyn: Portfolio check.\nKayla: Essay check.\nRyan: Research paper check.", "31": "Sodom — Justice and Intercession\nScripture: Gen 18:23-19:29\nSong #148 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'If you knew Jehovah was about to judge a city — and you had people you loved there — what would you do?' Teach: Abraham interceded 6 times for Sodom. He didn't just accept judgment passively — he boldly appealed to Jehovah's sense of justice.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Abraham's intercession showed both boldness and humility. 2. Jehovah listened and would have spared the city for 10 righteous. 3. Lot was rescued because of Abraham's intercession. 4. Lot's wife looked back — her heart was still there. Ask: What does Lot's wife's backward look teach us about divided loyalties?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Did Jehovah listen when Abraham asked Him to spare Sodom?\nAshelyn: Why do you think Lot's wife looked back? What is the spiritual lesson for us?\nKayla: How many times did Abraham ask Jehovah to spare the city? What does this persistence tell us about prayer?\nRyan: Compare Abraham's intercession in Gen 18 with Jesus' intercession in Heb 7:25. What does this pattern reveal about Jehovah's willingness to hear advocates?", "32": "Isaac — The Son of Promise\nScripture: Gen 21:1-7; Rom 4:19-21; Heb 11:11-12\nSong #118 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Have you ever waited so long for something that you stopped expecting it — and then it happened?' Teach: Abraham was 100 years old. Sarah was 90. Every human reason said impossible. Yet Isaac was born exactly when Jehovah said.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Rom 4:19-21 — Abraham considered his body as good as dead but did NOT weaken in faith. 2. Sarah laughed (Gen 18:12) but then Heb 11:11 says she received power through faith. 3. Isaac's name means 'laughter' — joy after impossible wait. 4. Jehovah is never late. Ask: What promise of Jehovah are you waiting for? How does Abraham's example encourage you?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What does the name Isaac mean? Why did Sarah laugh?\nAshelyn: What does Romans 4:19-21 say Abraham did even when his body was 'as good as dead'?\nKayla: Sarah went from laughing in doubt to being listed in Heb 11 as a woman of faith. What changed? What can we learn from her journey?\nRyan: How does Isaac's birth serve as a pattern for what Jehovah can do when humans consider something humanly impossible? Find two other examples in Scripture.", "33": "Mount Moriah — The Ultimate Test\nScripture: Gen 22:1-18; Heb 11:17-19; Rom 8:32\nSong #33 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'What is the most precious thing you have ever had to let go of? Or what would be hardest for you to let go of for Jehovah?' Teach: Abraham had waited 25 years for Isaac. Now Jehovah asked for him back.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Abraham rose EARLY the next morning — no hesitation, no negotiation. 2. 'Jehovah will provide the offering' — Abraham BELIEVED Isaac would come back somehow (Heb 11:19). 3. Heb 11:19 — he reasoned Jehovah could raise him from the dead. 4. This is a picture of Jehovah giving His own Son. Ask: Rom 8:32 — He who did not spare His own Son — how is the Moriah account a preview of Calvary?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jehovah stop Abraham from doing? Who did He provide instead?\nAshelyn: Heb 11:19 says Abraham 'reasoned' that Jehovah could raise Isaac from the dead. What does this tell us about how faith works?\nKayla: How does the Moriah account parallel Jehovah giving Jesus? Use Gen 22 and John 3:16 to make the comparison.\nRyan: Research on wol.jw.org: How does the Insight on the Scriptures entry for 'Moriah' connect this account to Jesus' ransom? Write a paragraph.", "34": "Jehovah-Jireh — Jehovah Provides\nScripture: Gen 22:8-14; Phil 4:19; Matt 6:31-33\nSong #20 — Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Jehovah-Jireh. That is a name Abraham gave to the mountain where Jehovah provided the ram. It means Jehovah will provide. What has Jehovah provided for your family that you didn't expect?' Teach: Every provision traces back to Jehovah.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The ram was ALREADY there — Jehovah had prepared it before Abraham needed it. 2. Phil 4:19 — Jehovah will fully supply every need. 3. The name Jehovah-Jireh became a location landmark — a testimony. 4. Matt 6:31-33 — seek the kingdom first and all these things will be added. Ask: What is the difference between Jehovah providing what we need vs. Jehovah providing what we want?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What does Jehovah-Jireh mean? Can you say it? What did Jehovah provide on the mountain?\nAshelyn: How does the ram being 'already there' before Abraham needed it show Jehovah's foreknowledge and care?\nKayla: Connect the ram on Mount Moriah to Phil 4:19. How does this principle apply to a specific situation in your life today?\nRyan: Research the principle of divine provision in the Hebrew scriptures. Find three accounts where Jehovah provided before the person realized they needed it. Write a paragraph.", "35": "Week 7 Review\nScripture: Gen 21-22\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nReview: Impossible birth, ultimate test, Jehovah provides. Family question: What is one thing from the life of Abraham that challenges your faith level today?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nQuiz: 1. What does Isaac mean? 2. What did Heb 11:19 say Abraham reasoned when told to offer Isaac? 3. What does Jehovah-Jireh mean? 4. What was the ram a picture of?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Share your drawing!\nAshelyn: Present personal project.\nKayla: Read essay paragraph.\nRyan: Present research paper.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: All celebrated.\nAshelyn: Portfolio check.\nKayla: Essay check.\nRyan: Research paper check.", "36": "A Wife for Isaac — Trusting Jehovah's Guidance\nScripture: Gen 24:1-67\nSong #54 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you let a stranger pick your wife based on a prayer?' Then read Gen 24:12-14 and notice how specific Abraham’s servant’s prayer was.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Abraham sent his servant to find a wife from his own relatives, not the Canaanites (24:3-4). 2. The servant prayed, and Rebekah answered the prayer before he finished (24:15-20). 3. Laban and Bethuel said 'This is from Jehovah' (24:50). Ask: Why does it matter to marry within the faith (1 Cor 7:39)?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who did Abraham send to find a wife for Isaac, and what did he pray?\nAshelyn: Rebekah said “I will go” (Gen 24:58) after seeing how Jehovah answered Abraham’s servant’s prayer. What evidence convinced her, and what would you want to see before making a life-changing decision?\nKayla: How was the servant’s prayer answered? List three details that showed it was from Jehovah (Gen 24:12-27).\nRyan: Compare Abraham’s concern about Isaac’s marriage with 1 Cor 7:39 and 2 Cor 6:14. What principles do they share for choosing a marriage mate?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a servant looked for Rebekah and prayed.\nAshelyn (8th grade): She saw the servant’s prayer answered exactly (water for the camels), heard the family confirm it was from Jehovah, and trusted. Look for: evidence + prayer + wise counsel.\nKayla: Rebekah gave water to him and the camels, matching the sign; family agreed.\nRyan: Marry ‘only in the Lord’; avoid unequal yoking.", "37": "Rebekah's Courage — Following Jehovah's Leading\nScripture: Gen 24:54-67\nSong #137 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold up a glass of water. Ask: 'How much water does a thirsty camel drink?' (Up to about 25 gallons.) Then ask what that says about Rebekah’s willingness to work.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Rebekah volunteered to water ten camels (Gen 24:19-20). 2. She was modest and hospitable. 3. When asked 'Will you go with this man?' she said 'I will go' (24:58). Ask: What qualities make a person trustworthy?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What kind thing did Rebekah do for the servant?\nAshelyn: Rebekah left her family at once and traveled to a land she had never seen. In 3–4 sentences, explain how her courage is similar to Abraham’s, and name one way you can show courage this week.\nKayla: Rebekah’s hard work and hospitality showed her heart. What do the actions in Gen 24:15-25 reveal about her character?\nRyan: Rebekah left her home immediately (Gen 24:57-58). How does this compare with Abraham’s obedience in Gen 12:1-4, and with Luke 9:62?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: gave water to him and camels.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Both acted on faith in Jehovah’s direction without full knowledge of the outcome. Accept a specific, realistic personal example.\nKayla: industrious, generous, modest, willing to go beyond what was asked.\nRyan: both left security for Jehovah’s purpose; Luke 9:62 calls for wholehearted commitment.", "38": "Esau and Jacob — Two Brothers, Two Choices\nScripture: Gen 25:19-34; 27:1-40\nSong #135 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Offer two treats: one small now, one big tomorrow. Ask which they would choose, then read how Esau chose.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Esau and Jacob were twins but very different (Gen 25:27). 2. Esau sold his birthright for lentil stew (25:29-34). 3. Jacob and Rebekah then deceived Isaac for the blessing (27). Ask: Who was wrong, and how did Jehovah view each?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Esau trade away for a bowl of stew?\nAshelyn: Esau traded his birthright for a bowl of stew (Gen 25:29-34). What does this show about how he valued sacred things (Heb 12:16), and what is a modern ‘bowl of stew’ that tempts young people?\nKayla: Esau is called 'not appreciative of sacred things' (Heb 12:16). Explain what that means and give one modern example.\nRyan: Jacob valued the birthright, but the way he and Rebekah obtained the blessing was deceptive. Discuss how Jehovah’s purpose was carried out despite human error (Gen 25:23).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: his birthright.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He valued an immediate desire over a lasting spiritual privilege. Modern examples: entertainment over meetings, popularity over integrity.\nKayla: he put immediate desires first and treated spiritual privileges as worthless.\nRyan: Jehovah foretold the elder would serve the younger; His purpose stands, but deception brought painful consequences.", "39": "Jacob's Ladder — The Dream That Changed His Life\nScripture: Gen 28:10-22; John 1:51\nSong #48 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Ask the children to close their eyes and picture a stairway reaching the sky. Ask: 'Who might be on it?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jacob fled from Esau and slept at Bethel with a stone for a pillow (Gen 28:10-11). 2. Jehovah promised: 'I am with you and will guard you' (28:15). 3. Jacob made a vow to Jehovah (28:20-22). Ask: When did you most need to know Jehovah was with you?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jacob see in his dream?\nAshelyn: Jacob dreamed of a ladder with angels going up and down (Gen 28:12-15). What promise did Jehovah repeat to him, and why might Jacob have needed that reassurance just then?\nKayla: What four promises did Jehovah give Jacob at Bethel (Gen 28:13-15)? Which one matters most to someone who is far from home?\nRyan: Jesus referred to Jacob’s dream in John 1:51. What was he teaching Nathanael about his own role?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: angels on a ladder.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Promise of land, offspring, and ‘I am with you.’ Jacob was alone, fleeing from Esau, with nothing – he needed to know Jehovah was with him.\nKayla: land, many descendants, blessing to all families, and guarding him.\nRyan: Jesus is the connection between heaven and earth; heavenly access comes through him.", "40": "Unit 2 Review + Celebration — Days 21-40\nScripture: Gen 12-28; Heb 11:8-16\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Play a quick game: shuffle cards with names Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah, Jacob. Each child draws a card and gives one fact.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nReview Days 21–40: faith of Abraham, test of Isaac, Rebekah’s courage, Jacob’s dream. Ask each child to retell one account in two minutes. Celebrate with a favorite song and a treat.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell your favorite story from the last four weeks.\nAshelyn: Review Days 21–40: choose ONE person we studied (Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah, Jacob) and explain in 4–5 sentences the quality you most want to copy and the scripture that shows it.\nKayla: Name three faith-building lessons from Abraham’s life and the scripture for each.\nRyan: Write a 5-sentence comparison of Abraham and Jacob’s relationship with Jehovah. Where did each grow?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any story.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Any person is acceptable if the quality is supported by a specific scripture and a personal application.\nKayla: leaving Ur (Heb 11:8); offering Isaac (Heb 11:17); covenant (Gen 15).\nRyan: look for growth from fear/deception to trust and a new name.", "41": "Jacob and Laban — Jehovah Watches Everything\nScripture: Gen 29:1-30:24\nSong #34 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Have you ever worked really hard and someone cheated you? What did you want to do?' Read Gen 31:7.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jacob worked 20 years for Laban (Gen 31:41). 2. Laban changed his wages ten times, yet Jehovah blessed Jacob (31:7-12). 3. Jacob grew a large family and flocks. Ask: How did Jehovah protect Jacob from unfair treatment?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who tricked Jacob when he wanted to marry Rachel?\nAshelyn: Laban tricked Jacob again and again, yet Jacob prospered (Gen 31:7-12). What does this teach about how Jehovah handles unfair treatment? Write 3 sentences.\nKayla: How did Jehovah show He was watching over Jacob during his time with Laban? Use Gen 31:7-12.\nRyan: Compare the way Jacob deceived Esau (Gen 27) with how Laban deceived him (Gen 29:23-25). What principle from Gal 6:7 applies?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Laban.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah sees everything and can protect His servants even when others cheat them; Jacob did not need revenge.\nKayla: protected his flocks and revealed what Laban was doing.\nRyan: he reaped what he sowed; Jehovah allowed discipline but did not abandon him.", "42": "Jacob Prepares to Face Esau — Courage After Wrongdoing\nScripture: Gen 32:1-21\nSong #44 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What do you do when you’re scared of meeting someone you wronged?' Read Gen 32:7 (Jacob was 'very afraid and distressed').\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jacob heard Esau was coming with 400 men. 2. He prayed humbly (32:9-12). 3. He sent gifts ahead (32:13-21). Ask: Why did Jacob do both?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jacob do when he heard Esau was coming?\nAshelyn: Jacob prayed before meeting Esau (Gen 32:9-12) and also sent gifts ahead. Why were BOTH the prayer and the practical action important? Give a modern example of doing both.\nKayla: Analyze Jacob’s prayer in Gen 32:9-12. List three elements (humility, reminder of promise, request) and explain why they make it a model prayer.\nRyan: Jacob combined prayer with practical action. How does this reflect Prov 16:3 and Jas 2:22?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: prayed.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Faith and reasonable effort work together. Example: praying about a test AND studying.\nKayla: ‘I am unworthy’, quoted Jehovah’s promise, asked for rescue.\nRyan: faith and works go together.", "43": "Wrestling With the Angel — Jacob Becomes Israel\nScripture: Gen 32:22-32; Hos 12:3-4\nSong #38 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What is something you would refuse to let go of?' Then read Gen 32:26.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. A man (an angel) wrestled with Jacob all night (32:24). 2. Jacob would not let go until blessed (32:26). 3. His name was changed to Israel (32:28). Ask: What does persistence in spiritual matters look like?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What new name did Jacob get?\nAshelyn: Jacob refused to let the angel go until he blessed him (Gen 32:26). What does this show about Jacob’s attitude toward spiritual things, and why was he renamed Israel?\nKayla: What does 'Israel' mean and why did Jacob receive it (Gen 32:28; Hos 12:3-4)?\nRyan: Jacob’s limp was a lasting reminder. How can physical or emotional struggles keep us humble and dependent on Jehovah (2 Cor 12:7-10)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Israel.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He deeply valued Jehovah’s blessing and persevered. Israel means ‘contends with God’ – a sign of his persistence.\nKayla: ‘one who contends with God’; he persisted.\nRyan: weakness reveals reliance on Jehovah’s power.", "44": "Jacob and Esau Reunited — The Power of Forgiveness\nScripture: Gen 33:1-20; Matt 5:9\nSong #130 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show the children a photo of reunited family or friends. Ask how they think Jacob and Esau felt.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Esau ran, embraced and kissed Jacob (33:4). 2. Jacob insisted Esau accept his gift (33:10-11). 3. They parted peacefully. Ask: What does it take on each side to reconcile?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How did Esau greet Jacob?\nAshelyn: Esau ran to meet Jacob and embraced him (Gen 33:4). What had to change in BOTH brothers for this reconciliation to happen? Connect your answer to Matthew 5:23-24.\nKayla: What attitudes did both brothers show that allowed peace? Connect to Matt 5:9, 23-24.\nRyan: Contrast Esau’s earlier threat (Gen 27:41) with his response in Gen 33. What might have softened his heart, and how should we respond when wronged?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: he hugged him.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jacob humbled himself and made peace; Esau let go of anger. Peace requires taking initiative.\nKayla: Jacob humbled himself; Esau let go of anger.\nRyan: time, Jehovah’s influence, and Jacob’s humility; forgive as Jehovah forgives (Eph 4:32).", "45": "Week 9 Review\nScripture: Gen 29-33\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Quick-fire: ask each child to name one thing about Jacob that they admire and one thing to learn from.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nReview Gen 29–33: Laban’s deception, Jacob’s wives and children, fear of Esau, wrestling, and reconciliation. Ask children to arrange the events in order.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Which event in Jacob’s life was your favorite this week?\nAshelyn: Review Days 41–45 (Gen 29–33): list three problems Jacob faced and for each one name how Jehovah helped him. Which help would you most want in your own life?\nKayla: List four events from Gen 29–33 in order and name the lesson from each.\nRyan: Write a short paragraph on how Jacob changed from Gen 27 to Gen 33.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Laban’s deceit → prospered; fear of Esau → protection and reconciliation; wrestling/guilt → blessing and new name.\nKayla: accurate order with lessons.\nRyan: from scheming to humble dependence on Jehovah.", "46": "Joseph and His Brothers — Seeds of Jealousy\nScripture: Gen 37:1-28\nSong #34 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What would you feel if your dad gave one sibling a special coat?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jacob loved Joseph most and gave him a multicolored robe (Gen 37:3). 2. Joseph’s dreams stirred hatred (37:5-11). 3. The brothers sold him for 20 pieces of silver (37:28). Ask: What led jealousy to grow into hatred?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Why were Joseph’s brothers upset with him?\nAshelyn: Joseph’s brothers hated him (Gen 37:3-11). Identify two things that fed their jealousy and explain how Jacob’s favoritism and Joseph’s dreams each played a part. What could have been done differently?\nKayla: Name two things that fed the brothers’ jealousy and one thing each side could have done differently.\nRyan: Compare Joseph’s treatment by his brothers with Jesus’ treatment by his own people (John 1:11; Acts 7:9-10).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the coat and dreams.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Favoritism (the special robe) and Joseph’s dreams told without tact. Better: fair treatment, humility and wisdom in sharing.\nKayla: favoritism and the dreams; fair treatment/humility.\nRyan: both were hated without cause, sold for silver, and later saved their people.", "47": "Sold Into Slavery — Jehovah Was With Him\nScripture: Gen 37:29-36; 39:1-6\nSong #3 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'How would you feel being taken from your family to a foreign land?' Read Gen 39:2.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Joseph was sold to Potiphar in Egypt. 2. 'Jehovah was with Joseph' (39:2,21). 3. Everything he did succeeded. Ask: How could Joseph still be successful as a slave?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who bought Joseph in Egypt?\nAshelyn: From the pit to Potiphar’s house, ‘Jehovah was with Joseph’ (Gen 39:2). How can you be sure Jehovah is with you when things go badly? Answer in 3 sentences using a scripture.\nKayla: What does 'Jehovah was with Joseph' mean? How could he feel that in slavery?\nRyan: How do Joseph’s attitude and work ethic reflect Col 3:23 and Prov 22:29?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Potiphar.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accept Ps 34:18, Is 41:10 or similar; key point: His presence is shown by strength and help, not the absence of trials.\nKayla: Jehovah supported and blessed his work.\nRyan: he worked wholeheartedly, as for Jehovah, and was noticed.", "48": "Potiphar's House — Moral Strength When No One Watches\nScripture: Gen 39:7-23; 1 Cor 6:18-20\nSong #36 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What would you do if someone tempted you and no one would ever find out?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Potiphar’s wife pressed Joseph daily (39:7-12). 2. He said 'How could I sin against God?' 3. He fled. Ask: Why is fleeing sometimes the wisest course (1 Cor 6:18)?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Joseph do when Potiphar’s wife tempted him?\nAshelyn: Joseph said, ‘How could I do this great badness and sin against God?’ (Gen 39:9). What reason did he give, and why is ‘when no one is watching’ the real test of character?\nKayla: Why did Joseph refer to God, not just Potiphar, when he refused? What principle does this show?\nRyan: Joseph was punished for doing right. How does 1 Pet 3:14 apply?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ran away.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He viewed it as sin against Jehovah, not only against Potiphar. Integrity is who you are when unseen (Prov 15:3).\nKayla: it would be sin against Jehovah.\nRyan: suffering for righteousness is blessed.", "49": "Joseph in Prison — Keeping Faith in Darkness\nScripture: Gen 40:1-23; Ps 105:18-19\nSong #128 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'How can you stay hopeful when nothing seems to change?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Joseph was put in prison though innocent. 2. Jehovah gave him favor with the warden (39:21-23). 3. He interpreted the dreams of the cupbearer and baker (40). Ask: What did Joseph do to help in prison?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Joseph do for the other prisoners?\nAshelyn: Joseph helped the cupbearer and baker even while in prison and gave credit to Jehovah (Gen 40:8). How can helping others make our own hard times easier to bear?\nKayla: How did Joseph give Jehovah credit for his ability to interpret dreams (40:8)?\nRyan: The cupbearer forgot Joseph for two years (40:23). How might this have tested his faith, and what does Ps 105:19 say?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: interpreted dreams.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Focus outward, stay useful, keep dependence on Jehovah. Accept personal examples.\nKayla: ‘Do not interpretations belong to God?’\nRyan: Jehovah refined him; the word of Jehovah tested him.", "50": "Week 10 Review\nScripture: Gen 37-40\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Play a timeline game: write Joseph’s events on cards and have the children arrange them.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nReview Gen 37–40: dreams, pit, slavery, Potiphar, prison. Ask each child to explain where Joseph showed faith.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What was Joseph’s hardest day?\nAshelyn: Review Days 46–50: write a short timeline of Joseph’s life from age 17 to prison. Underline the moment you think tested his faith the most and explain why.\nKayla: Write a four-sentence summary of Joseph’s trials and Jehovah’s support.\nRyan: Write a short essay on why Jehovah allowed Joseph to suffer.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Timeline: dreams → pit → sold → Potiphar → falsely accused → prison. Any choice with a reason is acceptable.\nKayla: accurate summary.\nRyan: prepare him, protect the family, fulfill promises.", "51": "Pharaoh's Dreams — Jehovah Gives Wisdom\nScripture: Gen 41:1-36; Dan 2:28\nSong #88 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What if you had to explain a dream to a king?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Pharaoh had two dreams (41:1-7). 2. Joseph said 'It is not me; God will answer' (41:16). 3. Seven good years followed by seven of famine. Ask: Why give Jehovah credit?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Whose dreams did Joseph explain?\nAshelyn: Joseph told Pharaoh, ‘It is not me; God will answer’ (Gen 41:16). Why is giving Jehovah the credit important when we are praised, and how does Daniel 2:28 show the same attitude?\nKayla: What plan did Joseph recommend in Gen 41:33-36? What qualities did it show?\nRyan: Compare Joseph before Pharaoh with Daniel before Nebuchadnezzar (Dan 2:27-28).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Pharaoh’s.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Humility – gifts come from Jehovah. Daniel also credited God, not himself, for revealing the dream.\nKayla: store grain during the plentiful years.\nRyan: both credited Jehovah as the revealer of secrets.", "52": "Joseph's Rise — Humility Before Promotion\nScripture: Gen 41:37-57\nSong #89 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What would you do if you suddenly became a ruler?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Pharaoh put Joseph over Egypt (41:41). 2. Joseph managed grain stores wisely. 3. He named his sons Manasseh and Ephraim (41:51-52). Ask: Why keep humility when promoted?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How old was Joseph when he became ruler? (30)\nAshelyn: Joseph was suddenly made second in command of Egypt. What dangers come with sudden success or promotion, and what in Joseph’s earlier life prepared him to handle it? Cite Gen 41:51-52.\nKayla: What did the names of Joseph’s sons reveal about his heart?\nRyan: What dangers come with promotion and how did Joseph avoid them? Compare 1 Pet 5:6.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: 30.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Pride and forgetting Jehovah. Joseph was humbled by trials and named his sons to remember Jehovah’s help.\nKayla: Manasseh – forget hardship; Ephraim – fruitfulness.\nRyan: pride avoided by remembering Jehovah’s hand.", "53": "Joseph's Brothers Come to Egypt\nScripture: Gen 42:1-43:34\nSong #119 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What would you do if you saw the person who hurt you years ago?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Famine drove Joseph’s brothers to Egypt (42:1-6). 2. Joseph recognized them but tested them (42:7-24). 3. They felt guilt about their earlier deed. Ask: Why did Joseph conceal his identity?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Why did Joseph’s brothers go to Egypt?\nAshelyn: Joseph tested his brothers instead of revealing himself right away (Gen 42–44). What do you think he was trying to find out, and did the brothers pass? Support with Gen 44:33.\nKayla: What clues show that the brothers’ attitude had changed since Gen 37?\nRyan: Why did Joseph set up the silver cup test (Gen 44)? What did Judah’s response reveal (44:33)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: to buy food.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He wanted to know whether they had changed. Judah offered himself in Benjamin’s place – they passed.\nKayla: they felt guilt and cared for Benjamin.\nRyan: to test their sincerity; Judah offered himself instead of Benjamin.", "54": "\"I Am Joseph Your Brother\" — The Great Reunion\nScripture: Gen 44:1-45:15; 50:19-21\nSong #109 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Write 'I am Joseph, your brother' on the board and read it with strong emotion.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Joseph wept and revealed himself (45:1-3). 2. 'God sent me ahead to preserve life' (45:5-8). 3. He forgave them. Ask: What does forgiveness free us from?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Joseph say to his brothers?\nAshelyn: Joseph said, ‘You meant harm, but God meant it for good’ (Gen 50:20). Does this mean the brothers’ actions were acceptable? Explain the difference between Jehovah using a bad situation and approving it.\nKayla: Explain Gen 45:5-8 and 50:20 in your own words. How did Joseph see Jehovah’s hand?\nRyan: How does Joseph’s forgiveness illustrate Col 3:13 and Matt 18:21-22?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ‘I am Joseph.’\nAshelyn (8th grade): No – they were still guilty. Jehovah can turn evil into good without causing or excusing the evil.\nKayla: Jehovah used evil for good.\nRyan: forgive freely without limit.", "55": "Week 11 Review\nScripture: Gen 41-45\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Joseph (Gen 41-45) — Pharaoh’s dreams, Joseph’s rise, his brothers’ test, and the reunion. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Joseph (Gen 41-45): Pharaoh’s dreams, Joseph’s rise, his brothers’ test, and the reunion. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Days 51–55: Joseph showed wisdom, humility, and forgiveness. Choose the one quality you find hardest and write two specific steps to develop it.\nKayla: Choose one account from Joseph (Gen 41-45) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Joseph (Gen 41-45) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Any quality with realistic steps (prayer, study, practicing in small situations).\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate steps.\nRyan: Jehovah works things for good for those who love Him.", "56": "Jacob's Family Moves to Egypt\nScripture: Gen 46:1-47:12\nSong #101 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you move to another country because of a promise?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jacob heard Joseph was alive (45:26-28). 2. Jehovah assured him at Beer-sheba (46:2-4). 3. 70 persons entered Egypt (46:27). Ask: What gave Jacob confidence to go?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who did Jacob get to see in Egypt?\nAshelyn: Jacob was afraid to go to Egypt until Jehovah spoke to him (Gen 46:1-4). Why do you think he needed to hear from Jehovah, and how does Jehovah speak to us today?\nKayla: Why was Jehovah’s reassurance important for Jacob’s move (Gen 46:3-4)?\nRyan: How did Joseph’s wisdom help his family settle in Goshen (47:1-6)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Joseph.\nAshelyn (8th grade): The move seemed to contradict the promise of Canaan. Today Jehovah speaks through His Word and the congregation.\nKayla: it confirmed the covenant.\nRyan: Goshen separated them from Egyptian worship and gave good pasture.", "57": "Jacob's Final Blessings — Prophetic Words\nScripture: Gen 49:1-28; Heb 11:21\nSong #95 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'If you could say one last thing to each person in your family, what would you tell them?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jacob called his twelve sons together to tell them what would happen 'in the final part of the days' (Gen 49:1). 2. Each blessing described the character and future of a tribe. 3. Judah received the promise of the scepter, pointing to the Messiah (49:10). Ask: Why was Judah, not Reuben, given this honor?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many sons did Jacob bless, and which son’s blessing mentions a ruler’s scepter?\nAshelyn: Jacob blessed each son with a prophecy, and Judah’s blessing mentions the scepter (Gen 49:10). Why is this blessing important to the promised Seed?\nKayla: Reuben, Simeon and Levi lost privileges because of earlier wrongs (Gen 49:3-7). What does this show about the long-term effects of anger and immorality? Give a modern example.\nRyan: Trace the royal line from Judah through David to Jesus (Gen 49:10; 2 Sam 7:16; Matt 1:1-3,6,16). Why is the exact record of Jesus’ ancestry important to his claim to the throne?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: 12; Judah.\nAshelyn (8th grade): The Messiah would come through Judah; kingship would belong to his line until Shiloh comes.\nKayla: bad choices cost them; consequences can last; accept a reasoned example.\nRyan: legal right to David’s throne through Judah proves Jesus is the promised Heir.\nKayla: kingship would remain in his line.\nRyan: Judah → David → Jesus, Shiloh in Gen 49:10.", "58": "Joseph's Faithfulness to the End\nScripture: Gen 50:1-26; Heb 11:22\nSong #124 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What do you want people to remember about you?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Joseph forgave his brothers again (50:15-21). 2. He told them Jehovah would bring them out (50:24). 3. His bones were later carried to Canaan (Ex 13:19; Heb 11:22). Ask: Why was his request important?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Joseph ask the Israelites to do with his bones?\nAshelyn: Before dying, Joseph made Israel promise to carry his bones to Canaan (Gen 50:24-25; Heb 11:22). What did this request show about his faith?\nKayla: What did Joseph’s final words show about his faith?\nRyan: Evaluate the lessons of Joseph’s life: pick three qualities and show where each appears.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: take them to Canaan.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He believed Jehovah’s promise would be fulfilled even though he was living in Egypt.\nKayla: he believed Jehovah’s promise.\nRyan: integrity, forgiveness, faith.", "59": "Joseph as a Model of Forgiveness and Providence\nScripture: Review Gen 37-50; Rom 8:28\nSong #130 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold up a puzzle and say each piece looks random alone.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nReview Gen 37–50 and Rom 8:28. Discuss how Jehovah guided events for good.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did you learn from Joseph?\nAshelyn: Romans 8:28 says all things work together for good for those who love God. Using Joseph as the example, explain what that does and does NOT mean.\nKayla: Explain how three events in Joseph’s life seemed bad but led to good.\nRyan: Defend the claim that Jehovah is in control even when it appears otherwise.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate.\nAshelyn (8th grade): It means Jehovah can bring good from trials for those who love Him; it does not mean suffering itself is good or painless.\nKayla: slavery, prison, famine all led to preservation.\nRyan: use Gen 50:20; Rom 8:28; Jehovah’s sovereignty and human responsibility.", "60": "Unit 3 Review + Celebration — Days 41-60\nScripture: Gen 37-50; Rom 8:28\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Throw a Joseph celebration: serve Egyptian-themed snacks and share testimonies.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nUnit 3 review. Let each child present a short summary of Joseph’s life and one personal lesson.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Share your favorite part of Joseph’s story.\nAshelyn: Unit 3 Review: Joseph forgave deeply. Describe a situation where forgiveness is hard for a teenager and explain how Col 3:13 and Joseph’s example could help.\nKayla: Present three leadership lessons from Joseph.\nRyan: Write a reflective paragraph on how Joseph’s example shapes your plans.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accept honest, specific situations; key points: forgive as Jehovah forgave, release revenge, trust Jehovah to judge.\nKayla: wisdom, integrity, forgiveness.\nRyan: look for depth and application.", "61": "400 Years of Silence — Jehovah Hears Every Cry\nScripture: Ex 1:1-22; Acts 7:6-7; Ps 10:17\nSong #41 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What happens when a new king forgets about the good done for the nation?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. A new Pharaoh feared Israel (Ex 1:8-10). 2. He enslaved them. 3. Jehovah heard their cries (Ex 2:23-25). Ask: Does Jehovah notice suffering?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Why did Pharaoh make Israel slaves?\nAshelyn: Pharaoh ordered baby boys killed (Ex 1:15-22). Shiphrah and Puah ‘feared God’ instead. What does that tell you about who we must obey when commands conflict? Cite Acts 5:29.\nKayla: Why did Jehovah allow Israel to suffer for 400 years (Gen 15:13-16)?\nRyan: Contrast Pharaoh’s fear with Jehovah’s compassion (Ex 2:24-25; Ps 10:17).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: he feared them.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah’s law over human law; they respected life and Jehovah blessed them.\nKayla: the nations’ sin was not yet full; Israel multiplied.\nRyan: Jehovah ‘remembered His covenant.’", "62": "Baby Moses — A Mother's Faith\nScripture: Ex 2:1-10; Heb 11:23\nSong #134 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a small basket and ask: 'Could you trust this with your baby?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jochebed hid Moses three months. 2. She placed him in a papyrus basket (Ex 2:3). 3. Pharaoh’s daughter found him. Ask: How did Jehovah protect him?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Jochebed put baby Moses?\nAshelyn: Jochebed hid Moses for three months and then placed him in a basket (Ex 2:1-10; Heb 11:23). What risk did she take, and how did her faith protect Moses’ future?\nKayla: What risks did Moses’ parents take, and how did Heb 11:23 describe their faith?\nRyan: How did Jehovah arrange for Moses’ mother to nurse him? What does it show about His care?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: in a basket.\nAshelyn (8th grade): She disobeyed an unjust decree, trusted Jehovah, and he was raised in Pharaoh’s own house – preserved for Jehovah’s purpose.\nKayla: they ignored the king’s order.\nRyan: Miriam suggested a nurse; his mother was paid to care for him.", "63": "Moses in the Palace — Two Worlds, One Heart\nScripture: Ex 2:11-15; Acts 7:22; Heb 11:24-26\nSong #56 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'If you were raised in a palace and could have anything, would you give it up for the truth?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses was educated in all the wisdom of Egypt (Acts 7:22). 2. He chose to be mistreated with God’s people rather than enjoy temporary sin (Heb 11:24-26). 3. He tried to defend an Israelite by killing an Egyptian and had to flee (Ex 2:11-15). Ask: What did he get right, and what did he get wrong?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Moses grow up, and what did he choose to give up?\nAshelyn: Moses grew up in Pharaoh’s palace but chose to identify with Israelites (Heb 11:24-26). List what he gave up and what he gained. Would you have made the same choice?\nKayla: Heb 11:25-26 says Moses considered the reproach of the Christ greater riches than Egypt’s treasures. What did he see that others missed?\nRyan: Moses acted out of real love for his people but at the wrong time and in the wrong way (Ex 2:12-14; Acts 7:23-25). Explain why zeal must be guided by Jehovah’s direction (Rom 10:2).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: in the palace; the riches.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Gave up wealth, status, comfort; gained approval of Jehovah and ‘the reward.’ Accept honest personal reflection.\nKayla: he saw the lasting reward Jehovah promised.\nRyan: zeal without knowledge and timing causes harm; wait on Jehovah’s direction.\nKayla: gave up status; gained Jehovah’s approval.\nRyan: right desire, wrong timing.", "64": "Moses Flees to Midian — A Fresh Start\nScripture: Ex 2:15-25; Acts 7:29-30\nSong #38 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a map of Egypt and Midian and trace his path.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses fled to Midian (Ex 2:15). 2. He married Zipporah and herded sheep for 40 years. 3. Jehovah prepared him for leadership. Ask: Why was shepherding good training?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Moses go after he left Egypt?\nAshelyn: Moses tried to fix injustice his own way and ended up fleeing to Midian (Ex 2:11-15). What can we learn about waiting on Jehovah’s timing?\nKayla: How might 40 years as a shepherd prepare Moses to lead Israel?\nRyan: Compare Moses’ 40 years in Midian with Paul’s time in Arabia (Gal 1:17-18).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Midian.\nAshelyn (8th grade): His timing and method were not Jehovah’s; 40 more years of preparation followed.\nKayla: patience, humility, wilderness skills.\nRyan: both used quiet periods for preparation.", "65": "Week 13 Review\nScripture: Ex 1-2; Acts 7\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Moses’ early life (Ex 1-2; Acts 7) — the midwives, baby Moses, the palace, and the flight to Midian. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Moses’ early life (Ex 1-2; Acts 7): the midwives, baby Moses, the palace, and the flight to Midian. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Ex 1–2: Name three people who protected life or obeyed Jehovah at personal risk and compare their motives. What did they all have in common?\nKayla: Choose one account from Moses’ early life (Ex 1-2; Acts 7) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Moses’ early life (Ex 1-2; Acts 7) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Shiphrah/Puah, Jochebed (and Miriam), Moses. Common thread: fear of Jehovah over fear of man.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: midwives, Jochebed, Miriam, Pharaoh’s daughter.\nRyan: God’s law supersedes human law.", "66": "\"I Will Become What I Need to Become\" — The Burning Bush\nScripture: Ex 3:1-15\nSong #2 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a small flame in a safe lamp and ask what it might mean for a bush to burn without being consumed.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses saw a burning bush (Ex 3:2). 2. Jehovah said 'I Will Become What I Need to Become' (3:14). 3. Moses removed his sandals (3:5). Ask: What does this name tell us about Jehovah?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Moses see?\nAshelyn: Jehovah said ‘I Will Become What I Need to Become’ (Ex 3:14). How does that name comfort someone facing something new and scary? Give an example from your own life.\nKayla: Explain the meaning of Jehovah’s name in Ex 3:14 and how it shapes our trust.\nRyan: Why did Jehovah emphasize holiness at the bush (Ex 3:5)? Connect to 1 Pet 1:15-16.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a burning bush.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah adapts to every need and fulfills His purpose; accept personal example.\nKayla: Jehovah becomes whatever He needs to fulfill promises.\nRyan: approach Him with reverence.", "67": "Moses' Excuses and Jehovah's Patient Answers\nScripture: Ex 3:16-4:17\nSong #73 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What excuses do we make when asked to do something hard?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses gave five excuses (3:11; 3:13; 4:1; 4:10; 4:13). 2. Jehovah answered each. 3. Aaron became his spokesman. Ask: Which excuse do you give?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who helped Moses speak?\nAshelyn: Moses gave five excuses (Ex 3:11–4:13). Pick two, and explain how Jehovah answered each one. Which of Moses’ excuses sounds most like something you’ve said?\nKayla: List two of Moses’ excuses and Jehovah’s answers.\nRyan: Compare Moses’ reluctance with Isaiah’s eagerness (Is 6:8). What changes?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Aaron.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Examples: ‘Who am I?’ → ‘I will be with you.’ ‘They won’t listen’ → signs. ‘Slow of speech’ → ‘I will teach you what to say.’\nKayla: ‘Who am I?’ – ‘I will be with you.’\nRyan: Moses depended on himself; Isaiah on Jehovah’s cleansing.", "68": "Moses Returns to Egypt — The Courage of Obedience\nScripture: Ex 4:18-31; Acts 7:34-35\nSong #61 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you return to a place where someone wanted you gone?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jehovah told Moses the men seeking his life were dead (4:19). 2. He took his family and returned. 3. Israel believed when they saw signs (4:30-31). Ask: What helps us act with courage?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Moses go?\nAshelyn: Moses obeyed and went back to Egypt, even though his old enemies might still be alive (Ex 4:19). Why does obedience often require courage before we feel ready?\nKayla: How did Jehovah make Moses ready to return to Egypt?\nRyan: Zipporah circumcised her son (Ex 4:24-26). What does the incident teach about obeying Jehovah’s requirements?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Egypt.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah removed the threat; courage grows as we act on trust.\nKayla: signs, Aaron, assurance.\nRyan: no exceptions to Jehovah’s requirements, even for leaders.", "69": "\"Let My People Go\" — First Meeting With Pharaoh\nScripture: Ex 5:1-6:12\nSong #55 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Role-play: one child is Pharaoh, one is Moses. Moses says 'Let my people go,' Pharaoh says 'Who is Jehovah?' Ask: How would you answer?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses and Aaron asked Pharaoh to let Israel hold a festival (Ex 5:1). 2. Pharaoh said 'I do not know Jehovah' and made the work harder (5:2-9). 3. Jehovah renewed His promise with seven 'I will' statements (6:6-8). Ask: Why did things get worse before they got better?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Pharaoh say when Moses asked him to let Israel go?\nAshelyn: Pharaoh said, ‘Who is Jehovah that I should obey him?’ (Ex 5:2). How is this the same question people ask today, and how would you answer using Ex 9:16?\nKayla: The Israelite foremen blamed Moses (Ex 5:20-21), and Moses brought his complaint to Jehovah (5:22-23). What is the right way to handle discouragement?\nRyan: Ex 6:9 says the people would not listen 'because of their broken spirit.' How can severe hardship affect our ability to hear good news, and how can we help others in that state (1 Thess 5:14)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ‘I do not know Jehovah.’\nAshelyn (8th grade): People don’t know Jehovah; He would make His name known throughout the earth by His power.\nKayla: take it to Jehovah in prayer, honestly, and listen to His reply.\nRyan: be patient, comfort the depressed, and help the weak.\nKayla: they lost trust; they needed patience.\nRyan: trials do not mean abandonment.", "70": "Week 14 Review\nScripture: Ex 3-6\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the call of Moses (Ex 3-6) — the burning bush, Moses’ excuses, the return to Egypt, and Pharaoh’s first refusal. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the call of Moses (Ex 3-6): the burning bush, Moses’ excuses, the return to Egypt, and Pharaoh’s first refusal. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Ex 3–6: Summarize in 5 sentences how Jehovah prepared Moses and the Israelites for the Exodus. Include the promise in Ex 6:6-7.\nKayla: Choose one account from the call of Moses (Ex 3-6) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the call of Moses (Ex 3-6) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Burning bush, call, signs, Aaron as helper, four ‘I will’ promises in Ex 6:6-7.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: bring out, rescue, redeem, take as people, be God, bring in, give land.\nRyan: Jehovah’s name stands behind each promise.", "71": "Why Jehovah Used the Plagues — His Greater Purpose\nScripture: Ex 7:1-7; Rom 9:17; Ex 12:12\nSong #10 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Why would Jehovah send ten plagues instead of just freeing Israel?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jehovah wanted His name declared in all the earth (Ex 9:16). 2. The plagues judged Egypt’s gods (Ex 12:12). 3. Israel would see His power and trust Him. Ask: What does this show about Jehovah’s patience and justice?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who did Jehovah want to know His name?\nAshelyn: Jehovah said the plagues would show He is greater than Egypt’s gods (Ex 12:12). Pick one plague and explain which Egyptian god or belief it humbled.\nKayla: Why did Jehovah send plagues instead of freeing Israel immediately?\nRyan: How does Rom 9:17 explain Jehovah’s purpose in dealing with Pharaoh?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: everyone.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Examples: Nile/Hapi (blood), Heqet (frogs), Ra (darkness), Apis/Hathor (livestock).\nKayla: to display power and judge false gods.\nRyan: Jehovah used Pharaoh’s stubbornness to publicize His name.", "72": "Plagues 1-4: Blood, Frogs, Gnats, Flies\nScripture: Ex 7:14-8:32\nSong #148 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Pour red-tinted water into a clear glass and ask what the Nile looked like.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Blood, frogs, gnats, flies (Ex 7:14–8:32). 2. Magicians copied the first plagues but not gnats (8:18-19). 3. Jehovah began separating Goshen (8:22-23). Ask: What do these plagues show about Egypt’s gods?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Name two of the plagues.\nAshelyn: The magicians copied the first two plagues but could not make gnats (Ex 8:18-19). What did they conclude, and why do people sometimes refuse to accept clear evidence?\nKayla: What did the magicians admit in Ex 8:19? Why do people deny clear evidence?\nRyan: What does the separation of Goshen from the plagues show about Jehovah’s care for His people?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: blood, frogs, gnats, flies.\nAshelyn (8th grade): ‘It is the finger of God.’ Pride and stubbornness hardened Pharaoh.\nKayla: ‘the finger of God’; pride blinds.\nRyan: Jehovah protects His servants and distinguishes them.", "73": "Plagues 5-7: Livestock, Boils, Hail\nScripture: Ex 9:1-35\nSong #9 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'If a weather alert said a hailstorm was coming, what would you do with your pets and your belongings?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Before the seventh plague, Jehovah told Egyptians to bring their livestock inside (Ex 9:19). 2. Those who feared Jehovah’s word obeyed and were spared; those who ignored it lost everything (9:20-21). 3. Pharaoh said 'I have sinned' yet hardened his heart again (9:27-35). Ask: How is saying sorry different from repenting?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What was the seventh plague?\nAshelyn: Some Egyptians took shelter when warned of the hail and some did not (Ex 9:20-21). What does this show about the power of a warning and personal choice?\nKayla: Some Egyptians believed the warning and some did not. What does this show about Jehovah’s fairness and about personal choice?\nRyan: Compare Pharaoh’s repeated 'I have sinned' (Ex 9:27; 10:16) with true repentance (2 Cor 7:10-11). What fruits mark genuine repentance?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: hail.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Those who listened were saved; Jehovah is fair and gives opportunity to act on faith.\nKayla: Jehovah gave a warning and a way to be saved; people chose.\nRyan: godly sadness changes behavior; Pharaoh’s sorrow was only fear of consequences.\nKayla: those who feared Jehovah’s word sheltered.\nRyan: Pharaoh’s regret wasn’t sincere; repentance changes behavior.", "74": "Plagues 8-10: Locusts, Darkness, the Passover\nScripture: Ex 10:1-11:10; 12:1-30\nSong #149 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Turn off the lights for a moment and ask what total darkness felt like.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Locusts and darkness (Ex 10). 2. Israel had light in Goshen (10:23). 3. The tenth plague—death of firstborn—led to Passover (Ex 12). Ask: How did blood protect Israel?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened at Passover?\nAshelyn: Goshen was spared from darkness and the Passover required blood on doorposts (Ex 10:23; 12:7). What does this teach about how Jehovah protects those who follow His instructions exactly?\nKayla: Explain how the Passover lamb pictures Christ (1 Cor 5:7; John 1:29).\nRyan: Why was it important that Israelites follow instructions exactly (Ex 12:21-28)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jehovah passed over their houses.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Protection came through obedience and trust, not the people’s merit; connects to ransom (1 Cor 5:7).\nKayla: Jesus’ blood saves.\nRyan: obedience demonstrates faith.", "75": "Week 15 Review\nScripture: Ex 7-12\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the plagues (Ex 7-12) — the ten plagues and the first Passover. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the plagues (Ex 7-12): the ten plagues and the first Passover. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Ex 7–12: Make a list of the ten plagues in order and, next to each, write one word describing what it showed about Jehovah.\nKayla: Choose one account from the plagues (Ex 7-12) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the plagues (Ex 7-12) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Blood, frogs, gnats, flies, livestock, boils, hail, locusts, darkness, firstborn. Words such as power, justice, protection.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate chart.\nRyan: Jehovah’s name declared throughout earth.", "76": "The Night Israel Left Egypt\nScripture: Ex 12:31-42; 13:17-22\nSong #63 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Pack a small bag and ask what you’d take if you had to leave tonight.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Israel left in haste (Ex 12:33-39). 2. Jehovah led them by cloud and fire (13:21). 3. They took Joseph’s bones. Ask: What does being led by Jehovah mean?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What guided Israel by day?\nAshelyn: Jehovah led Israel by a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night (Ex 13:21-22). How does Jehovah guide His people today? Give two examples.\nKayla: How did Jehovah choose the route in Ex 13:17-18? What does it show?\nRyan: Compare Jehovah’s guidance then with His guidance today (Ps 119:105; Prov 3:5-6).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a cloud.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Through the Bible, holy spirit, the faithful and discreet slave, the congregation.\nKayla: avoided war so they would not lose heart.\nRyan: the Bible and the congregation guide us.", "77": "The Red Sea — Jehovah Fights for His People\nScripture: Ex 14:1-31; Heb 11:29\nSong #150 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold a ruler between your hands and ask the group what it would feel like to see water walls.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Pharaoh’s army pursued (Ex 14:5-9). 2. Moses said 'Do not be afraid' (14:13). 3. The sea parted and closed (14:21-28). Ask: What did Israel have to do?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened at the Red Sea?\nAshelyn: At the Red Sea Moses said, ‘Do not be afraid… Jehovah will fight for you’ (Ex 14:13-14). What did Israel actually have to DO, and what did Jehovah do? Why are both parts important?\nKayla: How did Jehovah show His power? What did Israel have to do?\nRyan: Heb 11:29 says it was by faith they crossed. Explain.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jehovah parted the sea.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Israel had to move forward in faith; Jehovah delivered. Faith includes action.\nKayla: they walked through; he fought for them.\nRyan: faith acts.", "78": "The Song of Moses — Praising Jehovah After Victory\nScripture: Ex 15:1-21\nSong #62 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Play a lively praise song and ask what they would sing after being rescued.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses and Israel sang (Ex 15:1-19). 2. Miriam led women with tambourines. 3. They exalted Jehovah’s name. Ask: How do we praise Jehovah after He helps us?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Miriam do?\nAshelyn: The Song of Moses (Ex 15) praises Jehovah right after the victory. Write two sentences of your own praise to Jehovah for a recent time He helped you.\nKayla: What qualities of Jehovah are praised in Ex 15?\nRyan: Why is it significant that the song looks ahead to the Promised Land (Ex 15:13-17)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: led singing with tambourine.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accept sincere personal expression.\nKayla: strength, salvation, holiness, loyal love.\nRyan: confident faith in future fulfillment.", "79": "Manna, Quail, and Water — Jehovah Provides Daily\nScripture: Ex 16:1-17:7; John 6:31-35\nSong #46 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Give each child a small portion of bread and ask how they’d feel if that’s all they got for the day.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Israel grumbled (Ex 16:2-3). 2. Jehovah sent manna (16:14-15). 3. Gather daily; double on the sixth (16:19-26). Ask: What does manna teach about trust?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What food did Jehovah give Israel?\nAshelyn: Manna could only be gathered one day at a time, except before Sabbath (Ex 16:19-26). What lesson did this teach, and how does it relate to Matthew 6:11?\nKayla: Why did manna spoil if kept overnight (Ex 16:19-20)? What lesson does this teach?\nRyan: Jesus called himself the bread of life (John 6:31-35). Explain the connection.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: manna.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Daily dependence on Jehovah; trust that He provides what we need.\nKayla: daily dependence.\nRyan: true spiritual food gives everlasting life.", "80": "Unit 4 Review + Celebration — Days 61-80\nScripture: Ex 12-17; Ps 78:12-16\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the Exodus and wilderness (Ex 12-17) — the Red Sea, the song of Moses, and manna. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Celebrate with a treat or special activity.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the Exodus and wilderness (Ex 12-17): the Red Sea, the song of Moses, and manna. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Unit 4 Review: Choose the miracle that impressed you most from Exodus 3–17 and explain what it taught the Israelites AND what it teaches you.\nKayla: Choose one account from the Exodus and wilderness (Ex 12-17) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the Exodus and wilderness (Ex 12-17) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Any miracle with a thoughtful two-part answer.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate timeline.\nRyan: He is faithful, powerful, provider.", "81": "Israel at Sinai — Meeting With Jehovah\nScripture: Ex 19:1-25; Deut 4:10-13\nSong #1 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What would you do to get ready if the king was coming to your house?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Israel arrived at Mount Sinai three months after leaving Egypt (Ex 19:1). 2. Jehovah offered a covenant: 'a kingdom of priests and a holy nation' (19:5-6). 3. The people washed their garments and prepared for three days (19:10-15). Ask: What does preparation show about respect?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Jehovah speak to Israel, and how many days did they prepare?\nAshelyn: Before the Law was given Israel had to consecrate themselves (Ex 19:10-15). Why does preparing our hearts matter before we worship Jehovah?\nKayla: Why did Jehovah set boundaries around the mountain (Ex 19:12,21-24)? What does this teach about approaching Him?\nRyan: Compare Ex 19:5-6 with 1 Pet 2:9. How did the promise to Israel find a greater fulfillment?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Mount Sinai; three.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Respect and reverence; being ready to listen sets the tone for meetings, study and prayer.\nKayla: He is holy and must be approached with reverence.\nRyan: spiritual Israel is a royal priesthood declaring Jehovah’s praises.\nKayla: treasured possession, kingdom of priests.\nRyan: spiritual Israel inherits this role.", "82": "The Ten Commandments Part 1 — Commands 1-5\nScripture: Ex 20:1-12; Deut 5:7-16\nSong #89 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold up five fingers and have the children recite the first five commandments.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The first four involve our relationship with Jehovah (Ex 20:1-11). 2. The fifth involves honoring parents (20:12). 3. Obedience brings blessings (Eph 6:1-3). Ask: Why start with Jehovah?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What is the fifth commandment?\nAshelyn: The first four commandments concern our relationship with Jehovah. Pick one and explain how people break it today without realizing it (for example, Command 1 and idols such as money or fame).\nKayla: Why is exclusive devotion the foundation of all commands?\nRyan: How does Jesus’ summary in Matt 22:37-40 relate to the structure of the Ten Commandments?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: honor your father and mother.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Exclusive devotion; modern idols such as entertainment, possessions, sports. Accept reasoned examples.\nKayla: relationship with Jehovah shapes everything.\nRyan: first four = love God; last six = love neighbor.", "83": "The Ten Commandments Part 2 — Commands 6-10\nScripture: Ex 20:13-17; Matt 5:21-30\nSong #127 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold up five fingers for the last five commandments.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Do not murder, commit adultery, steal, bear false witness, or covet (Ex 20:13-17). 2. Jesus taught that attitudes matter (Matt 5:21-28). 3. Love fulfills the law (Rom 13:8-10). Ask: How do these protect people?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Which commandment says not to steal?\nAshelyn: Jesus explained that hatred is connected to murder and lust to adultery (Matt 5:21-28). Why does Jehovah care about attitudes and not just actions?\nKayla: Choose one command and explain how it protects people.\nRyan: How does coveting lead to breaking the others?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the eighth.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Actions begin in the heart; stopping bad thoughts early prevents wrong deeds.\nKayla: accept reasoned answers.\nRyan: desire leads to theft, lies, adultery, even murder.", "84": "Jehovah's Laws Are a Gift, Not a Burden\nScripture: Ps 119:97; Matt 11:28-30; Rom 7:12\nSong #97 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a fence around a playground. Ask: 'Is this fence a cage or protection?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Ps 119:97 — 'How I love your law!' 2. Jesus said his yoke is kindly and his load light (Matt 11:28-30). 3. 1 John 5:3 — Jehovah’s commands are not burdensome. Ask: What changes when we obey out of love?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Why does a fence around a playground help children?\nAshelyn: Psalm 119:97 says ‘How I love your law!’ What is the difference between following rules because you’re afraid and following them because you love the one who made them?\nKayla: What is the difference between following rules because you fear punishment and following them because you love the Lawgiver? Give an example from your own life.\nRyan: Read Ps 19:7-11. List four benefits the psalmist finds in Jehovah’s law and say how you have experienced one.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: it keeps them safe.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Motive: love produces willing obedience and joy; fear produces minimum compliance.\nKayla: love produces willing, joyful obedience.\nRyan: refreshing the soul, making wise, rejoicing the heart, brightening the eyes.\nKayla: love gives joy; fear gives minimum.\nRyan: principles shaped by love guide the conscience.", "85": "Week 17 Review\nScripture: Ex 19-20; Deut 5\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Mount Sinai (Ex 19-20; Deut 5) — the covenant at Sinai and the Ten Commandments. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Mount Sinai (Ex 19-20; Deut 5): the covenant at Sinai and the Ten Commandments. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Ex 19–20: Rank the Ten Commandments from easiest to hardest for a teen to keep today, then explain your #1 hardest with a scripture to help.\nKayla: Choose one account from Mount Sinai (Ex 19-20; Deut 5) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Mount Sinai (Ex 19-20; Deut 5) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Opinion-based; look for honest reasoning and a relevant supporting text.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: honest ranking.\nRyan: Rom 13:10.", "86": "The Golden Calf — How Quickly People Forget\nScripture: Ex 32:1-35; Acts 7:40-43\nSong #122 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a gold-colored toy and ask: 'What if you made this and called it god?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. While Moses was on the mountain, the people asked Aaron to make a god (Ex 32:1). 2. Aaron made a golden calf and called it a festival to Jehovah (32:4-5). 3. Moses broke the tablets and about 3,000 died (32:19,28). Ask: Why did they turn so quickly?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did the people make while Moses was away?\nAshelyn: Israel made a golden calf only weeks after promising to obey (Ex 24:3; 32:1-6). What factors can cause even sincere people to drift quickly? What protects us?\nKayla: Aaron blamed the people and said the calf 'just came out' of the fire (Ex 32:22-24). What does this show about excuses, and how should we take responsibility?\nRyan: 1 Cor 10:6-14 uses this event as a warning. Which modern 'idols' tempt Christians, and how do we 'flee from idolatry'?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a golden calf.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Impatience, peer pressure, absence of leadership, lack of focus. Protection: regular study, prayer, association (Heb 3:12-13).\nKayla: excuses avoid blame; admit and correct mistakes.\nRyan: money, entertainment, fame; keep Jehovah first.\nKayla: he shifted blame; impatience.\nRyan: warnings for us.", "87": "Moses Intercedes — Jehovah's Mercy Is Boundless\nScripture: Ex 33:1-34:9; Ps 103:8-14\nSong #42 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you speak up for someone who hurt you?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses interceded (Ex 32:11-14). 2. Jehovah revealed His name and qualities (Ex 34:5-7). 3. He remains merciful. Ask: What qualities stand out?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who prayed for Israel?\nAshelyn: Moses asked Jehovah to forgive Israel and even offered to be blotted out (Ex 32:32). What does this show about Moses’ love for the people and how does it compare with Jesus?\nKayla: List five qualities Jehovah described about Himself (Ex 34:6-7).\nRyan: How does Moses’ intercession foreshadow Christ (Heb 7:25)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Moses.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Self-sacrificing love; Jesus actually gave his life as a ransom.\nKayla: merciful, gracious, slow to anger, loyal, forgiving.\nRyan: Jesus intercedes for us.", "88": "The Tabernacle — Jehovah Dwells Among His People\nScripture: Ex 25:1-9; 26:1-30; 40:34-38\nSong #92 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw the tabernacle floor plan on the board together: courtyard, Holy, Most Holy.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jehovah gave exact instructions for the tabernacle (Ex 25-27; 35-40). 2. The ark with the cherubs was in the Most Holy (25:10-22). 3. When it was finished, Jehovah’s glory filled it (40:34-35). Ask: Why did Jehovah want to live among His people?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What was the tabernacle, and what was inside the Most Holy?\nAshelyn: Jehovah gave very detailed instructions for building the tabernacle (Ex 25–31). Why do details matter in worship? Connect to Hebrews 8:5.\nKayla: Why did Jehovah give such detailed instructions for the tabernacle? Connect your answer to Heb 8:5.\nRyan: Heb 9:1-12 compares the tabernacle with Christ’s heavenly service. Describe two ways the tabernacle was a shadow of greater things.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a portable tent for worship; the ark.\nAshelyn (8th grade): It was a ‘shadow’ of heavenly things; accuracy honored Jehovah and taught lessons.\nKayla: it was a pattern of heavenly realities; care shows respect.\nRyan: one high priest entering once vs. Jesus entering once for all with his own blood.\nKayla: different access levels.\nRyan: shadow of heavenly reality.", "89": "The Sacrificial System — What It Foreshadowed\nScripture: Ex 25:10-22; Lev 1:1-9; Heb 10:1-10\nSong #18 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Light a candle safely and ask: 'What do symbols like light and bread teach us?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The altar, lampstand, table of showbread, and incense altar each had meaning (Ex 25-30). 2. Animal sacrifices could not take away sins (Heb 10:1-4). 3. The high priest entered the Most Holy once a year on the Day of Atonement (Lev 16). Ask: What did all this point forward to?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What was burned on the altar of sacrifice?\nAshelyn: The animal sacrifices could not truly remove sin (Heb 10:1-4). What did they point forward to?\nKayla: If animal sacrifices could not truly remove sin, why did Jehovah require them? (Gal 3:24; Heb 10:1)\nRyan: Explain how Heb 9:11-14 shows that Jesus’ sacrifice is superior. Use the words 'once for all' in your answer.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: animals.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jesus’ perfect ransom sacrifice.\nKayla: they showed sin’s seriousness and pointed to the ransom.\nRyan: Jesus’ perfect blood cleanses the conscience; no repetition needed.\nKayla: imperfect and repeated.\nRyan: Jesus offered once for all.", "90": "MIDYEAR — Day 90 Milestone + Family Testimonies\nScripture: Ex 25-40; Heb 9:1-10\nSong #155 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the tabernacle and Midyear (Ex 25-40) — the golden calf, Moses’ intercession, and the tabernacle. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the tabernacle and Midyear (Ex 25-40): the golden calf, Moses’ intercession, and the tabernacle. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Midyear milestone: Write a letter to yourself from 90 days ago. What have you learned about Jehovah, about school, and about yourself? Include one goal for the second half.\nKayla: Choose one account from the tabernacle and Midyear (Ex 25-40) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the tabernacle and Midyear (Ex 25-40) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Reflective, specific writing; check for at least one spiritual, one academic and one personal observation.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: specific goals.\nRyan: measurable steps.", "91": "The 12 Spies — Faith Versus Fear\nScripture: Num 13:1-14:45; Heb 3:16-19\nSong #119 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a large bunch of grapes. Ask: 'If you saw fruit this big, would you fear the giants who lived there, or trust Jehovah?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Twelve spies explored Canaan for 40 days (Num 13:25). 2. Ten gave a fearful report; Caleb and Joshua said 'Jehovah is with us' (13:30; 14:6-9). 3. The people wept and wanted to return to Egypt. Ask: Why did the same land look different to the two groups?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Which two spies trusted Jehovah?\nAshelyn: Ten spies gave a fearful report and two gave a faithful one (Num 13:30-33; 14:6-9). Both groups saw the same land. What made the difference in their reports?\nKayla: Both groups saw the same land and the same people. What made the difference in their reports?\nRyan: Heb 3:16-19 says unbelief kept them out. How does unbelief show itself today, and how can we guard against it (Heb 3:12-13)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Caleb and Joshua.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Focus: ten looked at the giants; Joshua and Caleb looked at Jehovah’s power.\nKayla: ten looked at giants; two looked at Jehovah.\nRyan: encourage one another daily; avoid a hard heart.\nKayla: they focused on giants, not Jehovah.\nRyan: unbelief hardens hearts.", "92": "40 Years in the Wilderness — Consequences of Unbelief\nScripture: Num 14:26-45; Ps 95:8-11\nSong #114 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Count to forty slowly and ask what it’s like to wait that long.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jehovah decreed 40 years (Num 14:33-34). 2. That generation died in the wilderness. 3. Joshua and Caleb survived. Ask: Why did consequences last?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How long did Israel wander?\nAshelyn: Because of unbelief, that generation did not enter the Promised Land (Num 14:29-34). How is Hebrews 3:12 a warning for us, and what can we do to keep ‘a faith-filled heart’?\nKayla: What principle about consequences is shown in Num 14:26-35?\nRyan: Compare with Ps 95:8-11 and the warning to us.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: 40 years.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Don’t develop an unbelieving heart; study, prayer, meetings, encouraging one another daily.\nKayla: one year per day of spying.\nRyan: do not harden hearts.", "93": "Korah's Rebellion — Respecting Jehovah's Arrangement\nScripture: Num 16:1-50; Jude 11\nSong #123 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What if a team member said, I should be captain instead of the one chosen?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Korah, Dathan and Abiram challenged Moses and Aaron (Num 16:1-3). 2. Moses said Jehovah would show whom He had chosen. 3. The earth swallowed the rebels (16:31-33). Ask: Who were they really complaining against?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened to Korah and his group?\nAshelyn: Korah and his group challenged Moses and Aaron (Num 16). What were they really rejecting, and what is a modern way people reject Jehovah’s arrangement?\nKayla: Num 16:11 says Korah’s group was really complaining against Jehovah. How do we show respect for Jehovah’s arrangements today?\nRyan: Jude 11 warns against 'the rebellious talk of Korah.' What were the roots of Korah’s rebellion, and how can pride creep into our thinking?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the earth swallowed them.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah’s chosen leadership; today – criticizing or ignoring direction from the congregation.\nKayla: respect those Jehovah appoints and cooperate.\nRyan: ambition, envy, pride; stay humble (1 Pet 5:5-6).\nKayla: Jehovah’s appointed leadership.\nRyan: avoid rebellion against theocratic order.", "94": "The Bronze Serpent — Looking to Jehovah for Life\nScripture: Num 21:4-9; John 3:14-15\nSong #80 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw a snake on a pole and ask: 'What would you think if looking at this could save your life?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Israel complained and fiery snakes bit them (Num 21:4-6). 2. Moses made a bronze serpent on a pole (21:8-9). 3. Whoever looked at it lived. Ask: What did the people need to do?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did the people do to be healed?\nAshelyn: Those bitten by snakes had only to look at the bronze serpent to live (Num 21:8-9). Why would this seem too simple, and how did Jesus use it in John 3:14-15?\nKayla: Why would looking at a bronze serpent require faith? What does this say about Jehovah’s provision?\nRyan: Jesus said, 'As Moses lifted up the serpent… so the Son of man must be lifted up' (John 3:14-15). Explain the comparison.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: look at the bronze snake.\nAshelyn (8th grade): It required trust in Jehovah’s provision; Jesus’ being lifted up provides life to those who exercise faith.\nKayla: it seemed too simple but required trust in Jehovah’s instructions.\nRyan: faith in Jesus lifted on the stake brings everlasting life.\nKayla: trust in Jehovah’s provision.\nRyan: Jesus’ being lifted on the stake gives life.", "95": "Week 19 Review\nScripture: Num 13-21\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the wilderness wanderings (Num 13-21) — the twelve spies, Korah, and the bronze serpent. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the wilderness wanderings (Num 13-21): the twelve spies, Korah, and the bronze serpent. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Num 13–21: Choose one event and write a 5-sentence paragraph describing what happened, the choice people made, the result, and the lesson for today.\nKayla: Choose one account from the wilderness wanderings (Num 13-21) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the wilderness wanderings (Num 13-21) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Complete paragraph with all four parts; check accuracy.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: complete paragraph.\nRyan: grumbling, rebellion, unbelief.", "96": "Balaam and His Donkey — Jehovah Speaks Clearly\nScripture: Num 22:1-24:25; 2 Pet 2:15-16\nSong #40 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Has anyone ever ignored a clear warning? What happened?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. King Balak hired Balaam to curse Israel (Num 22:5-6). 2. Jehovah opened a donkey’s mouth to rebuke Balaam (22:28-30). 3. Balaam could only bless Israel (Num 23-24). Ask: What was wrong with Balaam’s heart?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What animal spoke to Balaam?\nAshelyn: Jehovah used a donkey to correct Balaam (Num 22:28-30). Why do you think Balaam kept going even after clear warnings, and what does 2 Peter 2:15 say about his motive?\nKayla: 2 Pet 2:15 says Balaam 'loved the wages of wrongdoing.' How does the love of money cloud good judgment?\nRyan: Balaam failed to curse Israel but later advised their downfall through immorality (Num 25; 31:16; Rev 2:14). What does this show about Satan’s methods and how we should guard against them?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a donkey.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Greed: he ‘loved the reward of wrongdoing.’ Lesson: warnings are for our good.\nKayla: greed makes people ignore Jehovah’s clear direction.\nRyan: if you cannot attack from outside, corrupt from inside; stay morally clean.\nKayla: love of reward.\nRyan: nobody can curse those Jehovah blesses.", "97": "Deuteronomy — Moses' Final Messages of Love\nScripture: Deut 1:1-3:29; 4:1-14\nSong #86 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Read a loving letter from a parent and ask: 'Why do parents repeat things they have already told us?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Deuteronomy records Moses’ final messages to the new generation (Deut 1:1-5). 2. He reviewed Jehovah’s deeds to build trust (1-3). 3. 'Take care not to forget the things your eyes have seen' (4:9). Ask: Why is remembering so powerful?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who gave the messages in Deuteronomy?\nAshelyn: In Deuteronomy, Moses reminds the new generation of what Jehovah did. Why is it important to remember history when facing the future (Deut 4:9)?\nKayla: Why did Moses review the past before giving new instructions? Explain how history can strengthen faith.\nRyan: Deut 4:9 says to teach these things to your children and grandchildren. Describe two practical ways your family keeps Jehovah’s works in mind.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Moses.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Remembering Jehovah’s help builds trust and prevents repeating mistakes.\nKayla: remembering Jehovah’s help builds trust and warns against repeating errors.\nRyan: family worship, daily text, sharing experiences.\nKayla: to build trust.\nRyan: parents are teachers (Prov 22:6).", "98": "Jehovah's Formula for Blessing\nScripture: Deut 6:4-9; 11:13-21; Josh 1:8\nSong #49 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Write Deut 6:4-5 on the board and have everyone say it aloud together.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. 'Jehovah our God is one Jehovah' (6:4). 2. Love Him with all heart, soul and strength (6:5). 3. Talk about His words at home, on the road, when lying down and rising (6:6-7). Ask: How does this shape our day?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Say Deut 6:5 in your own words.\nAshelyn: Deut 6:6-7 says to teach the commandments to your children and ‘impress them.’ In your own words, describe how your family does this and one way you could help.\nKayla: What do 'heart, soul and strength' each include? Give one example of loving Jehovah with each.\nRyan: Josh 1:7-9 and Deut 11:13-21 promise blessing for obedience. How do we apply these principles without falling into a 'prosperity gospel' view?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: love Jehovah with all you have.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accept honest family-specific answers: daily text, family worship, conversations.\nKayla: heart – desires; soul – whole life; strength – energy and abilities.\nRyan: blessings are spiritual security and Jehovah’s favor, not guaranteed wealth.\nKayla: whole person.\nRyan: meditation leads to success.", "99": "Moses' Death — A Faithful Servant's Legacy\nScripture: Deut 34:1-12; Heb 11:24-28\nSong #151 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a mountain picture and ask how Moses felt seeing the land.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Moses viewed the land from Mount Nebo (Deut 34:1-4). 2. He died at 120. 3. Jehovah buried him (34:5-6). Ask: Why didn’t he enter?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Moses see?\nAshelyn: Moses saw the Promised Land but did not enter (Deut 34:4). Yet Jehovah called him a faithful servant. What does this teach about success in Jehovah’s eyes?\nKayla: Why did Jehovah say Moses would not enter (Num 20:12)?\nRyan: What does Deut 34:10 say about Moses’ relationship with Jehovah?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the land.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Faithfulness matters more than reaching every goal; he is remembered for his relationship with Jehovah (Deut 34:10).\nKayla: he struck the rock.\nRyan: Jehovah knew him face to face.", "100": "DAY 100 PARTY + Unit 5 Celebration\nScripture: Deut 6:4; Josh 1:7-9; Ps 1:1-3\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the first 100 days (Gen–Deut) — Jehovah’s name, creation, the patriarchs, the Exodus and the Law. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Celebrate with a treat or special activity.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the first 100 days (Gen–Deut): Jehovah’s name, creation, the patriarchs, the Exodus and the Law. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Day 100! Make a list of five things you know about Jehovah today that you didn’t know on Day 1. Circle the one that has changed you most and explain how.\nKayla: Choose one account from the first 100 days (Gen–Deut) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the first 100 days (Gen–Deut) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Five accurate items with a thoughtful reflection on one.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate list.\nRyan: sincere reflection.", "101": "\"Be Strong and Courageous\" — Joshua Takes Over\nScripture: Josh 1:1-18\nSong #55 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hand out a 'commander’s order' card reading: 'Be courageous and strong.' Ask: Where does real courage come from?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. After Moses died, Jehovah told Joshua to lead Israel into Canaan (Josh 1:1-2). 2. Three times: 'Be courageous and strong' (1:6,7,9). 3. Meditate on the Law day and night (1:8). Ask: What was Joshua’s source of courage?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who became the leader after Moses?\nAshelyn: Jehovah told Joshua three times to ‘be courageous and strong’ (Josh 1:6,7,9). What was the source of his courage, and what is the connection to reading the Law daily (Josh 1:8)?\nKayla: What two things did Jehovah tell Joshua to do to be successful (Josh 1:7-8)?\nRyan: Joshua’s courage came from knowing Jehovah was with him (Josh 1:9). Describe a time you needed courage and what helped you.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Joshua.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Courage came from Jehovah’s presence and from daily reading, which kept his thinking aligned with God’s.\nKayla: obey the Law and read it daily.\nRyan: accept personal example linking Jehovah’s promise to action.\nKayla: be courageous.\nRyan: daily reading yields success.", "102": "Rahab — Faith in the Most Unlikely Person\nScripture: Josh 2:1-24; Heb 11:31; James 2:25\nSong #118 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Tie a red ribbon to a window and ask: 'What would this signal if the town were in danger?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Rahab hid the spies and confessed Jehovah as God (Josh 2:9-11). 2. She asked for protection for her family (2:12-13). 3. The scarlet cord marked her house (2:18; 6:25). Ask: How did Rahab show her faith?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Rahab hang from her window?\nAshelyn: Rahab was a prostitute from a condemned city, yet she is praised for faith (Heb 11:31; Jas 2:25). What actions proved her faith, and what does this show about how Jehovah views people who change?\nKayla: Rahab had a bad reputation but is praised for faith (Heb 11:31; Jas 2:25). What does that teach about Jehovah’s view of people who change?\nRyan: Rahab became an ancestor of Jesus (Matt 1:5). What does her inclusion show about Jehovah’s mercy and His purpose?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a red cord.\nAshelyn (8th grade): She hid the spies, confessed her belief, and asked for protection. Jehovah values repentance and faith, not past reputation.\nKayla: He values faith and repentance over past reputation.\nRyan: Jehovah accepts those who turn to Him from any background.\nKayla: hid spies and trusted Jehovah.\nRyan: faith with works.", "103": "Crossing the Jordan — Jehovah Honors Active Faith\nScripture: Josh 3:1-4:24\nSong #61 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Line up chairs as 'stepping stones' across a pretend river and walk across slowly.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The Jordan was at flood stage (Josh 3:15). 2. The priests carrying the ark stepped in first and the water stopped (3:13-17). 3. All Israel crossed on dry ground. Ask: When did the miracle happen?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What stopped the Jordan River?\nAshelyn: The Jordan only stopped when the priests’ feet touched the water (Josh 3:13-16). What does this tell you about when Jehovah often acts, and how could this apply to a decision you need to make?\nKayla: Why do you think Jehovah made the priests step into the water before it stopped? What does this teach about acting in faith?\nRyan: Compare the crossing of the Jordan with the crossing of the Red Sea (Ex 14). What similarities and differences stand out?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the priests stepping in with the ark.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Faith acts first; Jehovah supports the step. Accept personal application.\nKayla: faith acts before sight.\nRyan: both involved dry ground; one was after waiting, the other fleeing; both strengthened faith.\nKayla: step in.\nRyan: 2 Cor 5:7.", "104": "Stones of Remembrance — Teaching Children Jehovah's Works\nScripture: Josh 4:1-9; Ps 78:4-6\nSong #134 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Stack twelve stones on a table and ask: 'If I ask, What do these stones mean? what would you say?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Twelve men took stones from the Jordan (Josh 4:2-3). 2. They built a memorial at Gilgal (4:19-20). 3. Children would ask about them and parents would explain (4:6-7,21-24). Ask: How do we pass on Jehovah’s deeds?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many stones did Israel set up?\nAshelyn: Joshua set up twelve stones as a memorial so children would ask ‘What do these stones mean?’ (Josh 4:6-7). What ‘memorials’ could your family create to help future generations remember Jehovah’s help?\nKayla: What was the purpose of the stones? List two ways your family remembers Jehovah’s help.\nRyan: Ps 78:4-7 speaks of telling the coming generation. What is the goal of this teaching?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: twelve.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Photos, journals, testimony books, traditions. Accept creative, realistic ideas.\nKayla: to remind them of Jehovah’s deeds; family journals, traditions.\nRyan: that they place confidence in God and not forget His works.\nKayla: to remind.\nRyan: pass on Jehovah’s acts to the next generation.", "105": "Week 21 Review\nScripture: Josh 1-4\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at crossing the Jordan (Josh 1-4) — Joshua’s commission, Rahab, and the memorial stones. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review crossing the Jordan (Josh 1-4): Joshua’s commission, Rahab, and the memorial stones. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Josh 1–4: Write a four-sentence summary: who led, how they entered, how Jehovah showed His support, and how they remembered.\nKayla: Choose one account from crossing the Jordan (Josh 1-4) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from crossing the Jordan (Josh 1-4) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Joshua; crossed Jordan on dry ground; priests carried ark and waters stopped; twelve memorial stones.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: order.\nRyan: connect to Josh 1:9.", "106": "Jericho Falls — Obedience + Trust = Victory\nScripture: Josh 6:1-27\nSong #149 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: March around the room in silence seven times and ask how long they could keep quiet.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Israel marched around Jericho once a day for six days and seven times on the seventh (Josh 6:3-4). 2. They were not to speak until Joshua gave the order (6:10). 3. The walls fell flat (6:20). Ask: Why did Jehovah use this method?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many times did Israel march on the seventh day?\nAshelyn: The Jericho march looked foolish to human eyes (Josh 6). Why might Jehovah choose methods that seem foolish (1 Cor 1:27)? What was required of Israel besides marching?\nKayla: Why did Jehovah choose a plan that looked foolish to human eyes (1 Cor 1:27)?\nRyan: Heb 11:30 says the walls fell 'by faith.' What parts of the plan required faith from the people?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: seven.\nAshelyn (8th grade): So credit goes to Jehovah and trust is developed; they had to obey exactly, in silence, for seven days.\nKayla: so credit would go to Him.\nRyan: marching, silence, patience, and obedience without understanding.\nKayla: silence and obedience.\nRyan: faith brings victory.", "107": "Achan's Sin — One Person's Unfaithfulness Affects All\nScripture: Josh 7:1-26; Gal 6:7-8\nSong #34 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hide a coin in the room and ask: 'What if one person took something that belonged to everyone?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jehovah had ordered the spoils of Jericho set aside (Josh 6:18-19). 2. Achan secretly took silver, gold and a robe (7:1,21). 3. Israel lost at Ai and 36 men died (7:5). Ask: Why did one man’s secret sin affect everyone?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Achan take?\nAshelyn: Achan’s hidden sin cost 36 men their lives (Josh 7). Why might Jehovah hold a whole group accountable for one person’s secret? What does Galatians 6:7 add?\nKayla: Why did Jehovah hold Israel accountable for one man’s hidden sin? What does this show about the effect of our actions on the congregation?\nRyan: Gal 6:7-8 says we reap what we sow. How did Achan’s story prove it?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: things Jehovah said not to take.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Sin harms the community and Jehovah’s name; we reap what we sow. Secret sin is never really secret.\nKayla: sin harms the group and Jehovah’s name.\nRyan: secret sin eventually has consequences.\nKayla: Jehovah’s displeasure.\nRyan: sin has consequences.", "108": "Starting Over After Failure\nScripture: Josh 8:1-35; Ps 37:23-24\nSong #38 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What should you do after you fail a test or make a big mistake?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. After Achan was dealt with, Jehovah told Joshua 'Do not be afraid' (Josh 8:1). 2. Israel defeated Ai with a wise battle plan. 3. They renewed the covenant at Mounts Ebal and Gerizim (8:30-35). Ask: How did they start again?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Did Israel win the second battle at Ai?\nAshelyn: After the defeat at Ai, Jehovah told Joshua to get up and try again (Josh 7:10; 8:1). What does this show about how Jehovah views failure when we repent?\nKayla: What does Josh 8 teach about how Jehovah treats those who repent and return to Him?\nRyan: Ps 37:23-24 and Prov 24:16 describe someone who falls but gets up. What attitude helps us rise after failure?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: yes.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He forgives and gives opportunities to return to obedience; Prov 24:16.\nKayla: Jehovah forgives and restores.\nRyan: humility and persistence.\nKayla: obedience.\nRyan: Jehovah upholds the fallen.", "109": "The Gibeonite Deception — Always Consult Jehovah First\nScripture: Josh 9:1-27; Prov 3:5-6\nSong #88 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show old, patched clothes and ask: 'Would you believe someone who showed up like this and said they came from far away?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The Gibeonites pretended to come from far away (Josh 9:3-13). 2. Israel did not consult Jehovah (9:14). 3. They made a treaty and had to keep it (9:15-20). Ask: What would have prevented the mistake?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who tricked Israel?\nAshelyn: The Israelites didn’t ask Jehovah before making a treaty with the Gibeonites (Josh 9:14). What are some decisions teens make where they should ‘ask Jehovah’ first? How do we do that?\nKayla: What mistake did Israel make? How can we make sure we 'ask Jehovah' before important decisions?\nRyan: Prov 3:5-6 and Jas 1:5 describe seeking Jehovah’s guidance. Write a short prayer for a decision you need to make.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the Gibeonites.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Friends, dating, schooling, entertainment; pray, research in the Bible and publications, ask mature ones.\nKayla: pray, research Bible principles, seek counsel.\nRyan: sincere prayer reflecting trust in Jehovah.\nKayla: did not ask Jehovah.\nRyan: lean on Him.", "110": "Week 22 Review\nScripture: Josh 6-9\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Jericho to Gibeon (Josh 6-9) — Jericho, Achan, Ai, and the Gibeonites. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Jericho to Gibeon (Josh 6-9): Jericho, Achan, Ai, and the Gibeonites. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Josh 6–9: Create a cause-and-effect chart showing four events and the result of each (obedience at Jericho, sin of Achan, repentance, Gibeon).\nKayla: Choose one account from Jericho to Gibeon (Josh 6-9) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Jericho to Gibeon (Josh 6-9) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Obedience → victory; Achan’s theft → defeat; repentance → renewed support; no prayer → deception.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: order.\nRyan: tie to Josh 1:8.", "111": "The Cycle of the Judges — A Pattern to Learn From\nScripture: Judg 2:10-23; Ps 106:34-43\nSong #95 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw a circle with four arrows on the board and label it: sin, trouble, cry out, rescue.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. After Joshua’s generation died, a new generation did not know Jehovah (Judg 2:10). 2. They worshiped Baal and were oppressed (2:11-15). 3. Jehovah raised up judges to deliver them (2:16-19). Ask: Why did the cycle repeat?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What is a judge in the Bible?\nAshelyn: The Judges period repeated a cycle: sin → oppression → crying out → deliverance → peace (Judg 2:11-19). Why did the cycle keep repeating? What could have broken it?\nKayla: Describe the cycle in Judges 2. What could have stopped it?\nRyan: Ps 106:34-43 explains the root cause. What was it, and how can we avoid it?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: someone Jehovah raised to rescue Israel.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Short-lived loyalty and failing to teach the next generation; consistent worship and obedience.\nKayla: sin, oppression, repentance, rescue; teaching the next generation.\nRyan: they failed to remove false worship and mixed with the nations.\nKayla: cycle steps.\nRyan: failure to remove idols.", "112": "Deborah — Jehovah Uses Whoever Is Willing\nScripture: Judg 4:1-5:31\nSong #137 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a tent peg and hammer and ask: 'Who would use these in a battle?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Deborah was a prophetess who judged Israel (Judg 4:4). 2. Barak led the army and Jehovah routed Sisera (4:14-16). 3. Jael finished the fight (4:21). Ask: Who gets the credit?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who was the prophetess who judged Israel?\nAshelyn: Deborah, Barak, and Jael each played a part in the victory (Judg 4–5). Compare how they showed courage and faith. Why did Jehovah use ordinary people?\nKayla: Deborah, Barak and Jael each had a role. What does this show about Jehovah using different people?\nRyan: Judg 5 is Deborah’s song of praise. What themes does it highlight about Jehovah’s help?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Deborah.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Deborah gave the message, Barak led, Jael acted. Jehovah uses whoever is willing and obedient.\nKayla: He uses the willing, regardless of position.\nRyan: Jehovah’s power, human cooperation, praise.\nKayla: Deborah, Barak, Jael.\nRyan: He uses the willing.", "113": "Gideon's 300 — Jehovah's Strength in Human Weakness\nScripture: Judg 6:1-7:25; 1 Cor 1:27-29\nSong #73 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold up a clay jar and a flashlight and ask: 'How could these be used in a battle?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Gideon began with 32,000 men (Judg 7:3). 2. Jehovah reduced the army to 300 (7:7). 3. Trumpets, jars and torches routed Midian (7:19-22). Ask: Why did Jehovah want fewer men?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many men did Gideon end with?\nAshelyn: Gideon began with 32,000 men and ended with 300 (Judg 7:2-8). Why did Jehovah reduce the army, and how does that apply to our own sense of ‘not enough’?\nKayla: Gideon felt weak and unqualified (Judg 6:15). How does 1 Cor 1:27-29 apply to feeling inadequate?\nRyan: Contrast Gideon’s early fear with his later boldness. What changed?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: 300.\nAshelyn (8th grade): So Israel would not boast; Jehovah’s strength shows when we feel weak (2 Cor 12:9-10).\nKayla: Jehovah’s strength works through weakness.\nRyan: assurance and obedience.\nKayla: avoid boasting.\nRyan: Jehovah’s power in weakness.", "114": "Samson — Great Potential, Tragic Compromise\nScripture: Judg 13:1-16:31; 1 Cor 9:27\nSong #36 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Who is the strongest person you know? Is strength enough to make wise choices?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Samson was set apart as a Nazirite (Judg 13). 2. He was repeatedly drawn to Philistine women (14:1; 16:1). 3. He revealed his secret to Delilah and lost his strength (16:17-21). Ask: What weakened him first, his hair or his heart?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What was the source of Samson’s strength?\nAshelyn: Samson had incredible strength but poor judgment. Identify two choices that weakened him spiritually, and write one safeguard a teen can use against compromise.\nKayla: Identify two choices that led to Samson’s downfall and one safeguard that could have protected him.\nRyan: 1 Cor 9:27 speaks of disciplining the body. How does self-control protect our spiritual strength?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jehovah’s spirit.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Associating with Philistine women, treating his Nazirite vow lightly, Delilah’s pressure; safeguards: choose friends carefully, honor commitments.\nKayla: toying with temptation; avoid bad associations.\nRyan: self-control keeps us qualified.\nKayla: wrong associations.\nRyan: self-discipline.", "115": "Week 23 Review\nScripture: Judg 2-16\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the Judges (Judg 2-16) — Deborah, Gideon, and Samson. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the Judges (Judg 2-16): Deborah, Gideon, and Samson. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Judges: Choose Deborah, Gideon, or Samson and write a 5-sentence character study with strengths, weakness, key scripture and lesson.\nKayla: Choose one account from the Judges (Judg 2-16) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the Judges (Judg 2-16) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Complete, accurate character study.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: complete.\nRyan: faith vs compromise.", "116": "Ruth's Choice — Loyalty That Costs Something Real\nScripture: Ruth 1:1-22; Matt 10:37\nSong #108 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you move to a foreign land to take care of someone you love?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Naomi lost her husband and sons in Moab (Ruth 1:3-5). 2. Ruth said, 'Your people will be my people, and your God my God' (1:16). 3. They returned to Bethlehem. Ask: What did Ruth give up?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who went with Naomi to Bethlehem?\nAshelyn: Ruth told Naomi, ‘Your people will be my people and your God my God’ (Ruth 1:16). What did she give up, and what motivated her choice?\nKayla: What did Ruth give up, and what does her choice tell you about her faith?\nRyan: Matt 10:37 says loving Jehovah must come first. How did Ruth illustrate that?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Ruth.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Homeland, family, prospects of remarriage; love for Naomi and faith in Jehovah.\nKayla: homeland, family, security; she chose Jehovah.\nRyan: faith that puts God’s will before family ties.\nKayla: land, family.\nRyan: Jehovah first.", "117": "Ruth and Boaz — Kindness Is Never Wasted\nScripture: Ruth 2:1-23\nSong #116 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Scatter some dried beans on a table and let the children 'glean' them.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Ruth gleaned barley in Boaz’s field (Ruth 2:2-3). 2. Boaz noticed her loyalty and told his men to leave extra (2:8-16). 3. He prayed that Jehovah would reward her (2:12). Ask: What qualities did Boaz admire?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Whose field did Ruth glean in?\nAshelyn: Ruth gleaned in the field and Boaz noticed her kindness (Ruth 2:11-12). What qualities did Boaz admire? Which of these can you develop right now?\nKayla: How did Boaz show kindness, and how does it reflect Lev 19:9-10?\nRyan: Ruth 2:12 mentions taking refuge under Jehovah’s wings. What does this picture teach about Jehovah’s care?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Boaz.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Loyalty, humility, hard work, taking refuge under Jehovah’s wings.\nKayla: generosity and respect.\nRyan: protection like a bird covering its young.\nKayla: loyalty and work.\nRyan: provision for the poor.", "118": "The Kinsman-Redeemer — A Picture of Jehovah's Love\nScripture: Ruth 3:1-4:12; Heb 2:14-15\nSong #26 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a pawn ticket and ask: 'How does someone get back something that was sold?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. A kinsman-redeemer could buy back family land (Lev 25:25). 2. Boaz offered to redeem and take Ruth as wife (Ruth 4:1-10). 3. The elders blessed them. Ask: How does Boaz picture Jehovah’s love?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who redeemed Naomi’s land and married Ruth?\nAshelyn: A kinsman-redeemer buys back what was lost (Ruth 4:9-10). How does this picture Jehovah’s love and point toward Jesus’ ransom?\nKayla: Explain the role of a kinsman-redeemer.\nRyan: Heb 2:14-15 describes Jesus as our redeemer. Compare Boaz to Christ.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Boaz.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Redeemer pays the price to restore family and inheritance; Jesus’ ransom restores life.\nKayla: a relative who buys back family property.\nRyan: Jesus pays the price to free us.\nKayla: buys back.\nRyan: ransom parallels.", "119": "Ruth in the Line of the Messiah — Jehovah's Bigger Plan\nScripture: Ruth 4:13-22; Matt 1:5; Gal 3:28\nSong #27 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a family tree and trace it from Ruth to David.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Ruth and Boaz had Obed (Ruth 4:13-17). 2. Obed was the grandfather of David. 3. Ruth is in the line of Jesus (Matt 1:5). Ask: Why does it matter that Ruth was a Moabite?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who was Obed’s grandson?\nAshelyn: Ruth, a Moabite, became an ancestor of David and Jesus (Matt 1:5). What does this show about Jehovah’s view of nationality, and how should it shape how we treat others?\nKayla: Why is it significant that a Moabite woman became an ancestor of the Messiah?\nRyan: Gal 3:28 and Acts 10:34-35 show Jehovah’s impartiality. How does Ruth’s story illustrate this?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: David.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He accepts people from every nation who fear Him (Acts 10:34-35).\nKayla: Jehovah welcomes people from all nations.\nRyan: faith matters more than background.\nKayla: Jehovah’s inclusion.\nRyan: no favoritism.", "120": "Unit 6 Review + Celebration — Days 101-120\nScripture: Ruth 1-4; Prov 31:30-31\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Ruth and Unit 6 (Ruth 1-4) — Ruth’s loyalty, Boaz’s kindness, and the redeemer. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Celebrate with a treat or special activity.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Ruth and Unit 6 (Ruth 1-4): Ruth’s loyalty, Boaz’s kindness, and the redeemer. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Unit 6 Review: In Prov 31:30 and Ruth, what does a ‘worthy’ person look like? Write a paragraph describing worth in Jehovah’s eyes versus the world’s.\nKayla: Choose one account from Ruth and Unit 6 (Ruth 1-4) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Ruth and Unit 6 (Ruth 1-4) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Fear of Jehovah, loyalty, diligence, kindness vs. appearance, popularity, wealth.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: loyalty.\nRyan: fear of Jehovah.", "121": "Hannah's Prayer — Persistence and Trust in Jehovah\nScripture: 1 Sam 1:1-2:11; Luke 1:46-55\nSong #41 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What do you do when you ask for something and the answer is wait?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Hannah was childless and grieved (1 Sam 1:1-8). 2. She poured out her heart in prayer and vowed to give her son to Jehovah (1:10-11). 3. Eli first misjudged her, then blessed her. Ask: What changed Hannah’s face after praying?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Hannah ask Jehovah for?\nAshelyn: Hannah poured out her heart to Jehovah, and then went away with a calm face (1 Sam 1:10-18). How does prayer change our attitude even before the answer arrives (Phil 4:6-7)?\nKayla: How did Hannah’s attitude change after she prayed (1 Sam 1:18)? Why?\nRyan: Read Hannah’s prayer (1 Sam 2:1-10) and compare it with Mary’s in Luke 1:46-55. What themes do both share?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a son.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Casting anxieties on Jehovah brings peace; the burden is shared.\nKayla: her worry lifted; she cast her burden on Jehovah.\nRyan: Jehovah lifts the lowly and brings down the proud.", "122": "Samuel — Dedicated to Jehovah From Childhood\nScripture: 1 Sam 3:1-4:1; 1 Sam 2:26; Luke 2:52\nSong #133 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Whisper the child’s name from another room and see if they answer.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Samuel served at the tabernacle as a boy (1 Sam 2:18). 2. Jehovah called him three times (3:4-10). 3. He said 'Speak, for your servant is listening.' Ask: How does Jehovah speak today?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many times did Jehovah call Samuel?\nAshelyn: Young Samuel heard Jehovah call his name and answered, ‘Speak, for your servant is listening’ (1 Sam 3:10). What would listening look like for you today?\nKayla: Why did Eli’s instruction matter to Samuel? What can an older worshipper do for young ones?\nRyan: Samuel had to deliver a hard message to Eli (3:11-18). What does this teach about faithfulness?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: three.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Daily reading, attentive at meetings, obeying direction. Accept personal answers.\nKayla: Eli taught him how to respond.\nRyan: loyalty to Jehovah’s message over personal comfort.", "123": "Israel Demands a King — Jehovah Grants It With a Warning\nScripture: 1 Sam 8:1-22; Hos 13:11\nSong #9 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Ask the children to design what a perfect king would be like.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Israel demanded a king (1 Sam 8:5). 2. Jehovah said they rejected Him (8:7). 3. Samuel warned what a king would take (8:11-18). Ask: Why was their request wrong?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Israel ask for?\nAshelyn: Israel asked for a king ‘like all the nations’ (1 Sam 8:5-7). Jehovah said they had rejected Him. Why is wanting to be ‘like everyone else’ a risk for us?\nKayla: Why did Jehovah say the people had rejected Him?\nRyan: Compare Israel’s desire to be like the nations with Rom 12:2.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a king.\nAshelyn (8th grade): It replaces trust in Jehovah with human approval and standards (Rom 12:2).\nKayla: they wanted human rule instead of trusting Him.\nRyan: conforming to the world replaces reliance on Jehovah.", "124": "Saul — A Good Start, A Heartbreaking End\nScripture: 1 Sam 9:1-10:27; 15:1-35; 1 Sam 15:22\nSong #127 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Set a candle burning low and ask: 'What happens when we start strong but stop paying attention?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Saul was humble when chosen (1 Sam 9:21; 10:22). 2. He offered sacrifices himself (13:8-14) and spared King Agag (15:9). 3. Samuel said 'To obey is better than sacrifice' (15:22). Ask: How did pride grow?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who was Israel’s first king?\nAshelyn: Saul started humble but ended disobedient (1 Sam 10:21-22; 15:17-23). Trace the change. What warning signs of pride can we watch for?\nKayla: Trace the steps in Saul’s downfall. Which one would be the easiest to repeat today?\nRyan: Explain 1 Sam 15:22-23 in your own words.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Saul.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Excuses, partial obedience, wanting people’s approval; ‘to obey is better than sacrifice.’\nKayla: impatience, partial obedience, excuses.\nRyan: Jehovah values obedience more than outward acts.\nKayla: impatience, partial obedience, excuses.\nRyan: obedience matters more than ritual.", "125": "Week 25 Review\nScripture: 1 Sam 1-15\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Samuel and Saul (1 Sam 1-15) — Hannah, Samuel, the demand for a king, and Saul’s fall. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Samuel and Saul (1 Sam 1-15): Hannah, Samuel, the demand for a king, and Saul’s fall. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review 1 Sam 1–15: Match the person to the quality: Hannah, Samuel, Saul. Then explain which example you want to avoid and why.\nKayla: Choose one account from Samuel and Saul (1 Sam 1-15) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Samuel and Saul (1 Sam 1-15) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Hannah – prayerful trust; Samuel – listening and faithfulness; Saul – disobedience and pride.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: complete paragraph.\nRyan: Samuel accepted; Saul excused.", "126": "Jehovah Looks at the Heart — David Is Chosen\nScripture: 1 Sam 16:1-23; Ps 139:23-24\nSong #49 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Line up the children by height and ask who looks like the best leader. Then read 1 Sam 16:7.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Samuel anointed the youngest son, David (16:11-13). 2. Jehovah looks at the heart. 3. David played music for Saul. Ask: What did Jehovah see in David?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who was chosen as king?\nAshelyn: Jehovah told Samuel, ‘Man sees what appears to the eyes, but Jehovah sees what the heart is’ (1 Sam 16:7). How should that affect how we judge others and ourselves?\nKayla: What does 1 Sam 16:7 teach about how to judge people?\nRyan: David was chosen but did not become king for years. What preparation do you see in his life?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: David.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Don’t judge by looks or status; work on inner qualities.\nKayla: heart over appearance.\nRyan: shepherd work, music, trust in Jehovah.", "127": "David and Goliath — Faith Conquers Fear\nScripture: 1 Sam 17:1-58\nSong #55 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Give each child a small stone to hold during the story.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Goliath mocked Jehovah’s armies (1 Sam 17:10, 26). 2. David said 'The battle belongs to Jehovah' (17:47). 3. He used a sling and five stones. Ask: Where did David get courage?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did David use to defeat Goliath?\nAshelyn: David told Goliath, ‘I come in the name of Jehovah’ (1 Sam 17:45). List three things that prepared David for this moment, and one ‘giant’ in your life you face with Jehovah’s help.\nKayla: What past experiences prepared David? Which 'giants' do you face?\nRyan: Compare Saul’s armor offer with 2 Cor 10:3-4. What armor should we rely on?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: sling and stone.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Experience protecting sheep, trust in Jehovah, knowledge of His name. Personal giant accepted.\nKayla: lion and bear experiences; personal giant accepted.\nRyan: spiritual weapons over human methods.", "128": "David and Jonathan — What True Friendship Looks Like\nScripture: 1 Sam 18:1-20:42; Prov 17:17\nSong #101 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What does a true friend do when it costs something?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jonathan loved David as himself (1 Sam 18:1-4). 2. He warned David of Saul’s plan (20). 3. He accepted Jehovah’s choice of David. Ask: What did Jonathan give up?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who was David’s best friend?\nAshelyn: Jonathan gave David his robe and weapons (1 Sam 18:1-4). What does this show about true friendship, especially when one friend is the rival of one’s own father?\nKayla: Describe three ways Jonathan showed loyal love.\nRyan: How does Jonathan’s attitude toward David’s kingship show humility and faith?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jonathan.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Loyalty, unselfishness, putting Jehovah’s will first (Prov 17:17).\nKayla: gave robe/weapons, warned him, defended him.\nRyan: he set aside his own claim to the throne.", "129": "Saul Pursues David — Trusting Jehovah When Life Is Unfair\nScripture: 1 Sam 24:1-22; Ps 7:1-2\nSong #33 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'If you had the perfect chance to get back at someone who was hunting you, what would you do?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Saul chased David with 3,000 men (1 Sam 24:2). 2. David cut a corner of Saul’s robe in the cave but would not harm him (24:4-7). 3. Saul wept and admitted David was more righteous (24:16-20). Ask: Why didn’t David take revenge?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Whom did David spare in the cave?\nAshelyn: David spared Saul’s life twice, saying he would not harm ‘Jehovah’s anointed’ (1 Sam 24:6). Why didn’t he take revenge, and what does Romans 12:19 add?\nKayla: David said he would not harm 'Jehovah’s anointed' (1 Sam 24:6). What does this show about his respect for Jehovah’s arrangements?\nRyan: Rom 12:17-21 echoes David’s attitude. How can overcoming evil with good be practical for a teenager?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: King Saul.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He left judgment to Jehovah; vengeance belongs to Jehovah.\nKayla: he left judgment to Jehovah.\nRyan: refuse revenge; do good to enemies.\nKayla: Jehovah is the judge.\nRyan: overcome evil with good.", "130": "Week 26 Review\nScripture: 1 Sam 16-24; Ps 59\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at David’s rise (1 Sam 16-24) — David’s anointing, Goliath, Jonathan, and Saul’s pursuit. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review David’s rise (1 Sam 16-24): David’s anointing, Goliath, Jonathan, and Saul’s pursuit. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review 1 Sam 16–24: Create a chart comparing Saul and David on: attitude toward Jehovah, handling of pressure, and treatment of others.\nKayla: Choose one account from David’s rise (1 Sam 16-24) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from David’s rise (1 Sam 16-24) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Saul – jealous, disobedient, fearful; David – trusting, patient, respectful.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate chart.\nRyan: refuge and loyalty.", "131": "David Becomes King — Long-Awaited Promise Fulfilled\nScripture: 2 Sam 2:1-5:12; Ps 2:6\nSong #22 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw a crown and ask: 'How long would you wait to become king after being promised?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. David was anointed as a youth but ruled Judah in Hebron years later (2 Sam 2:1-4). 2. After seven and a half years he became king over all Israel (5:1-5). 3. He captured Jerusalem (5:6-10). Ask: What did the wait teach?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What city did David capture and make his capital?\nAshelyn: David waited about seven years to rule all Israel (2 Sam 2–5). What did this wait teach him, and where do we need patience while waiting for Jehovah’s promises?\nKayla: David waited years between anointing and ruling. What does this teach about trusting Jehovah’s timing?\nRyan: Jehovah promised David a house that would last forever (2 Sam 7:12-16). Show how Luke 1:32-33 connects to this promise.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jerusalem.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Humility, trust in timing; waiting for Kingdom blessings.\nKayla: patience and dependence on Jehovah.\nRyan: Jesus inherits David’s throne forever.\nKayla: his throne would last forever.\nRyan: Jesus is the heir.", "132": "The Ark Comes to Jerusalem — Pure Joy in Worship\nScripture: 2 Sam 6:1-23; Ps 150\nSong #93 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Play joyful music and have the children march behind a 'box' as if carrying the ark.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. David moved the ark on a new cart (2 Sam 6:3). 2. Uzzah touched it and died (6:6-7). 3. David later brought it correctly, with joy (1 Chron 15:2,13-16). Ask: Why did the first attempt fail?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did David bring to Jerusalem?\nAshelyn: Uzzah touched the ark and died (2 Sam 6:6-7). David was upset, then learned to follow Jehovah’s instructions exactly (1 Chron 15:13). What does this show about worshiping Jehovah ‘in the right way’?\nKayla: Uzzah’s intentions seem good, yet Jehovah struck him. What does this teach about worshiping in the way Jehovah directs?\nRyan: Compare Num 4:15 with 1 Chron 15:13. What did David learn?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the ark.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Good intentions don’t replace Jehovah’s direction; the ark was to be carried by Levites with poles.\nKayla: good intentions don’t replace obedience.\nRyan: the Levites were to carry it with poles; Jehovah’s instructions matter.\nKayla: it was carried the wrong way.\nRyan: worship must follow Jehovah’s instructions.", "133": "David's Sin — No One Is Above Accountability\nScripture: 2 Sam 11:1-27; Ps 51:1-4\nSong #42 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'How do small choices turn into big trouble?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. David stayed home while the army fought (2 Sam 11:1). 2. He looked, inquired, sent for Bathsheba, tried to hide it, and had Uriah killed (11:2-17). 3. Jehovah was displeased (11:27). Ask: Where could David have stopped?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What sin did David commit with Bathsheba?\nAshelyn: David’s sin with Bathsheba began with idle time and a look (2 Sam 11:1-4). Write the chain of steps that led to adultery and murder, then identify the best point to stop.\nKayla: List the steps in David’s downward spiral and identify the best point to turn back.\nRyan: Jas 1:14-15 describes the path from desire to death. Apply it to this account.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: he took another man’s wife.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Staying home, looking, inquiring, sending for her, covering up, murder; stopping at the look – Job 31:1.\nKayla: idle time, looking, desire, scheming, murder; stop at the look.\nRyan: desire conceives sin; sin brings death.\nKayla: idleness, looking, desire, cover-up.\nRyan: desire gives birth to sin and death.", "134": "Nathan's Parable — How Jehovah Corrects Those He Loves\nScripture: 2 Sam 12:1-25; Heb 12:5-6\nSong #130 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Tell a short story about a rich man who took a poor man’s only lamb.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Nathan told David a parable (2 Sam 12:1-4). 2. David burned with anger and judged the man (12:5-6). 3. Nathan said 'You are the man!' (12:7). Ask: Why use a story?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who confronted David?\nAshelyn: Nathan’s story made David judge himself (2 Sam 12:1-7). Why was a story more effective than a direct accusation? How should we accept correction?\nKayla: Why was the parable effective in helping David see his sin?\nRyan: Compare David’s response (12:13; Ps 51) with Saul’s excuses in 1 Sam 15. What makes genuine repentance?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Nathan.\nAshelyn (8th grade): It touched David’s conscience; accept with humility (Heb 12:5-6, Ps 141:5).\nKayla: David judged the injustice before he saw it was himself.\nRyan: David confessed without excuses; Saul blamed others.\nKayla: David saw the wrong in another.\nRyan: confess and forsake sin to receive mercy.", "135": "Week 27 Review\nScripture: 2 Sam 2-12; Ps 51\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at David as king (2 Sam 2-12; Ps 51) — David’s kingdom, the ark, his sin, and his repentance. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review David as king (2 Sam 2-12; Ps 51): David’s kingdom, the ark, his sin, and his repentance. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review 2 Sam 2–12 and Ps 51: What did David do after he was confronted? Highlight three expressions of real repentance in Ps 51:1-4,10-12.\nKayla: Choose one account from David as king (2 Sam 2-12; Ps 51) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from David as king (2 Sam 2-12; Ps 51) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Confession, asking for mercy, asking for a clean heart.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: confession and request for a clean heart.\nRyan: forgiveness does not always remove consequences.", "136": "Solomon Asks for Wisdom — What We Should Really Pray For\nScripture: 1 Kings 3:1-28; Prov 4:7; James 1:5\nSong #45 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'If Jehovah said, Ask for anything, what would you ask?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Solomon asked for an obedient heart to judge the people (1 Ki 3:9). 2. Jehovah gave wisdom, plus riches and honor (3:12-13). 3. The two mothers’ case showed his wisdom (3:16-28). Ask: Why did Jehovah give more?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Solomon ask for?\nAshelyn: Solomon asked for ‘an obedient heart’ (1 Ki 3:9) instead of riches. If Jehovah offered you one request, what would it be? Explain how your request would help you serve Him.\nKayla: Why did Solomon’s request please Jehovah (1 Ki 3:10-12)? What does it reveal about his heart?\nRyan: Jas 1:5 invites us to ask for wisdom. How do we receive it?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: wisdom/an obedient heart.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accept sincere request; look for connection to serving Jehovah and helping others.\nKayla: he put others’ needs above selfish gain.\nRyan: ask in faith, study the Bible, apply it.\nKayla: he felt young and inadequate.\nRyan: ask in faith.", "137": "Building the Temple — Giving Jehovah Our Best\nScripture: 1 Kings 6:1-38; 8:1-21\nSong #92 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Build a model temple from blocks and count the years it takes.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The temple took seven years to build (1 Ki 6:38). 2. Gold, cedar and stone were used (6:14-22). 3. Jehovah’s glory filled it (8:10-11). Ask: Why give our best?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How long did it take to build the temple?\nAshelyn: Solomon spent seven years building the temple with the best materials (1 Ki 6:38). What does this teach about the quality of our offering to Jehovah (Col 3:23)?\nKayla: What does the care in building the temple teach about the quality of our worship?\nRyan: 1 Cor 3:16-17 calls Christians God’s temple. How should this affect how we treat our bodies and minds?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: seven years.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Give Jehovah our best, not leftovers – time, effort, resources.\nKayla: Jehovah deserves our best, not leftovers.\nRyan: keep ourselves clean and holy.\nKayla: honoring Jehovah.\nRyan: we are God’s temple.", "138": "Solomon's Dedication Prayer — Model of Deep Prayer\nScripture: 1 Kings 8:22-66; Ps 72\nSong #47 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Kneel together and pray a short prayer of thanks.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Solomon prayed publicly at the dedication (1 Ki 8:22-53). 2. He asked Jehovah to hear when people repented and when foreigners prayed (8:41-43). 3. Fire and glory showed approval. Ask: What makes a good prayer?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who prayed at the temple dedication?\nAshelyn: In his dedication prayer Solomon asked Jehovah to hear foreigners too (1 Ki 8:41-43). What does that show about Jehovah’s purpose for all nations?\nKayla: Identify three elements of Solomon’s prayer. How can you use them in your own?\nRyan: Why would Solomon ask Jehovah to hear foreigners? What does this show about Jehovah’s purpose?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Solomon.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah wants people of all nations to know Him.\nKayla: praise, confession, request.\nRyan: Jehovah wants all nations to know Him (Is 56:7).\nKayla: praise, humility, request.\nRyan: Jehovah’s purpose includes all nations.", "139": "Solomon's Downfall — Compromise Is Always Costly\nScripture: 1 Kings 11:1-43; Eccl 12:13-14\nSong #135 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Tilt a table slightly and roll a marble to show a slow drift.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Solomon loved many foreign women (1 Ki 11:1-3). 2. They turned his heart to other gods (11:4-8). 3. Jehovah told him the kingdom would be torn (11:9-13). Ask: What warning did he ignore?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What turned Solomon’s heart away?\nAshelyn: Solomon’s foreign wives turned his heart away (1 Ki 11:1-6). Which warning from Jehovah did he ignore, and what can we do to guard against gradual compromise?\nKayla: Deut 17:17 warned kings not to take many wives. Why did Solomon ignore it, and what does it teach about gradual compromise?\nRyan: Eccl 12:13-14 is Solomon’s conclusion. How does it show what he learned too late?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: his wives and their false gods.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Deut 17:17 and Jehovah’s direct warnings; keep close to Jehovah, choose associations wisely.\nKayla: pride and desire; small steps.\nRyan: fear God and keep His commandments.\nKayla: warnings about foreign wives.\nRyan: fear God and keep commandments.", "140": "Unit 7 Review + Celebration — Days 121-140\nScripture: 1 Kings 3-11; Eccl 12:13\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Solomon and Unit 7 (1 Ki 3-11) — Solomon’s wisdom, the temple, his prayer, and his downfall. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Celebrate with a treat or special activity.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Solomon and Unit 7 (1 Ki 3-11): Solomon’s wisdom, the temple, his prayer, and his downfall. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Unit 7 Review: Compare David and Solomon. How were their starts similar, and how did their endings differ? What lesson will you apply?\nKayla: Choose one account from Solomon and Unit 7 (1 Ki 3-11) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Solomon and Unit 7 (1 Ki 3-11) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Both began humble; David repented, Solomon drifted. Faithfulness must last to the end.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: parallels and contrasts.\nRyan: perseverance.", "141": "The Kingdom Divides — North and South\nScripture: 1 Kings 12:1-33; Amos 3:7\nSong #9 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Cut a paper heart in two and ask: 'What happens when leaders ignore good advice?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Rehoboam rejected the elders’ counsel (1 Ki 12:6-14). 2. Ten tribes broke away (12:16-20). 3. Jeroboam set up golden calves (12:28-30). Ask: Who gave the better advice?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened to the kingdom after Solomon?\nAshelyn: Rehoboam ignored the elders’ advice and followed his friends (1 Ki 12:6-14). How do you decide whose advice to follow? Use Prov 13:20.\nKayla: Why did Rehoboam follow his young friends? How do we choose whose counsel to trust (Prov 13:20)?\nRyan: Jeroboam created a counterfeit worship system out of fear (12:26-33). What does this show about how fear can lead to compromise?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: it split in two.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Seek wisdom from experienced ones who love Jehovah; avoid advice that is harsh or selfish.\nKayla: pride and peer pressure.\nRyan: trusting human plans over Jehovah.\nKayla: he followed young friends.\nRyan: wise counsel from experienced ones.", "142": "Elijah and the Drought — Jehovah Controls All Things\nScripture: 1 Kings 17:1-24; James 5:17-18\nSong #7 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Pour out the last bit of water from a pitcher and ask: 'What if this was all we had?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Elijah announced a drought (1 Ki 17:1). 2. Ravens fed him by the Cherith (17:2-6). 3. The widow of Zarephath shared her last meal and was provided for (17:8-16). Ask: What did she need to believe?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who fed Elijah at the brook?\nAshelyn: Elijah was fed by ravens and later by a widow with little food (1 Ki 17). What does each story show about Jehovah’s provision and about what we must be willing to give?\nKayla: What did the widow have to do before Jehovah provided? What does this teach about faith?\nRyan: Jas 5:17-18 says Elijah was a man with feelings like ours. How does this encourage us to pray?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ravens.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah provides in unexpected ways; faith requires acting even when supplies are low.\nKayla: make Elijah a cake first.\nRyan: righteous prayers are powerful.\nKayla: bake him a cake first.\nRyan: he was human like us and prayed.", "143": "Mount Carmel — The Showdown\nScripture: 1 Kings 18:1-46\nSong #32 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Have the children build a pretend altar of blocks.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Elijah challenged Baal’s prophets (1 Ki 18). 2. Fire from Jehovah consumed the sacrifice. 3. Israel cried 'Jehovah is the true God.' Ask: What proves Jehovah?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What came down from heaven?\nAshelyn: On Mount Carmel, Elijah asked, ‘How long will you be limping between two opinions?’ (1 Ki 18:21). What is a modern example of trying to serve two masters (Matt 6:24)?\nKayla: Why did Elijah ask for water on the sacrifice?\nRyan: 1 Ki 18:21: how do people limp between two opinions today?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: fire.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Trying to please God and the world; being inconsistent in conduct.\nKayla: to show no trickery.\nRyan: divided loyalty.", "144": "Elijah's Discouragement — Jehovah's Tender Care\nScripture: 1 Kings 19:1-21; Ps 34:18\nSong #44 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What helps when you are tired and discouraged?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Elijah ran from Jezebel and asked to die (1 Ki 19:1-4). 2. An angel gave him food and rest (19:5-8). 3. Jehovah spoke in a calm, low voice (19:11-13). Ask: How did Jehovah care for him?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who fed Elijah under the broom tree?\nAshelyn: Elijah asked to die after fear and exhaustion, but Jehovah gave him food, rest, and a quiet voice (1 Ki 19). How does Jehovah help discouraged servants today?\nKayla: What did Jehovah do to restore Elijah’s strength and perspective?\nRyan: Ps 34:18 and 1 Ki 19:18 give comfort. Write a short encouragement for a discouraged friend.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: an angel.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Through rest, meals, friends (Elisha), the Bible, and prayer.\nKayla: rest, food, listening, giving him tasks and a helper.\nRyan: Jehovah is close to the brokenhearted and has others who are loyal.\nKayla: quiet voice, not wind or fire.\nRyan: Jehovah is near the brokenhearted.", "145": "Week 29 Review\nScripture: 1 Kings 17-19\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Elijah (1 Ki 17-19) — the drought, Mount Carmel, and Jehovah’s quiet voice. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Elijah (1 Ki 17-19): the drought, Mount Carmel, and Jehovah’s quiet voice. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review 1 Ki 17–19: Make a three-column chart: Elijah’s faith, Elijah’s fears, Jehovah’s care.\nKayla: Choose one account from Elijah (1 Ki 17-19) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Elijah (1 Ki 17-19) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Faith – drought, Carmel; fear – Jezebel; care – food, angel, still small voice, a successor.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate chart.\nRyan: provision, power, comfort.", "146": "Elisha — Faithful in Small Things Before Great Things\nScripture: 2 Kings 2:1-25; Luke 16:10\nSong #48 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you leave your farm and family to serve someone as an assistant?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Elijah threw his cloak over Elisha (1 Ki 19:19). 2. Elisha burned his plowing equipment and followed (19:21). 3. He served by pouring water on Elijah’s hands (2 Ki 3:11). Ask: What did he give up?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Whom did Elisha follow?\nAshelyn: Elisha left his farm and followed Elijah, serving him for years (1 Ki 19:19-21; 2 Ki 3:11). Why is being ‘faithful in small things’ important (Luke 16:10)?\nKayla: Elisha served before he led. Why is serving humbly good preparation?\nRyan: Luke 16:10 says faithfulness in little leads to faithfulness in much. How can you apply it this week?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Elijah.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Small tasks prove trustworthiness and prepare us for larger responsibilities.\nKayla: it builds humility and skill.\nRyan: be dependable with small tasks.\nKayla: burned the plow, served.\nRyan: faithfulness in little.", "147": "Elisha's Miracles — Power From Jehovah Alone\nScripture: 2 Kings 4:1-44; Acts 5:12\nSong #104 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Fill a small jar with water and say the widow’s jars never ran out of oil.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. A widow’s oil multiplied to pay debts (2 Ki 4:1-7). 2. The Shunammite woman’s hospitality was rewarded and her son raised (4:8-37). 3. A poisoned stew was made safe (4:38-41). Ask: What did these women do in faith?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened to the widow’s oil?\nAshelyn: Elisha helped a widow with oil and later raised a boy to life (2 Ki 4). Which miracle shows Jehovah’s care for ordinary families? How did the people involved show faith?\nKayla: What does the widow’s obedience show about faith?\nRyan: Luke 7:11-17 shows Jesus doing a similar miracle. What do both show about Jehovah’s compassion?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: it kept filling jars.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accept either; faith shown by borrowing vessels, hospitality, persistence.\nKayla: she borrowed many empty vessels.\nRyan: Jehovah cares for widows and families.\nKayla: she borrowed many vessels.\nRyan: Jehovah’s care for the needy.", "148": "Naaman — Healing and the Humility to Accept Help\nScripture: 2 Kings 5:1-27\nSong #80 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Would you be willing to wash in a muddy river if it healed you?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Naaman, a Syrian commander, had leprosy (2 Ki 5:1). 2. A captive Israelite girl told him about Elisha (5:2-3). 3. He washed seven times in the Jordan and was healed (5:10-14). Ask: What nearly stopped him?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who was healed of leprosy?\nAshelyn: Naaman almost refused to wash in the Jordan because it seemed too simple (2 Ki 5:11-14). What stopped his healing from starting sooner? Where is humility needed today?\nKayla: How did a young servant girl help Naaman? What can young ones do to help others?\nRyan: Gehazi’s greed brought leprosy (5:20-27). What warning does this give?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Naaman.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Pride and expectations; listening to simple directions from Jehovah’s servants.\nKayla: she spoke up about Jehovah’s prophet.\nRyan: greed and deceit have consequences.\nKayla: expectation of grandeur.\nRyan: greed brings punishment.", "149": "Jehoshaphat — Seeking Jehovah in Crisis\nScripture: 2 Chron 20:1-30; Ps 46:1-3\nSong #150 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Stand in a circle and say, 'We do not know what to do, but our eyes are on You.'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. A vast army came against Judah (2 Chron 20:1-2). 2. Jehoshaphat prayed and proclaimed a fast (20:3-12). 3. The singers went ahead and Jehovah defeated the enemy (20:20-24). Ask: How did they show faith?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jehoshaphat do when he was afraid?\nAshelyn: King Jehoshaphat said, ‘We do not know what to do, but our eyes are on you’ (2 Chron 20:12). How can this prayer help us when a problem seems too big?\nKayla: 2 Chron 20:12 is a model prayer in a crisis. List what Jehoshaphat admitted and what he trusted.\nRyan: Ps 46:1-3 echoes this confidence. Which part gives you comfort and why?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: prayed.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Admit our limits, look to Jehovah, wait for His direction.\nKayla: admitted weakness; trusted Jehovah.\nRyan: accept sincere reflection.\nKayla: eyes on Jehovah.\nRyan: refuge and strength.", "150": "Week 30 Review\nScripture: 2 Kings 2-5; 2 Chron 20\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Elisha and Jehoshaphat (2 Ki 2-5; 2 Chron 20) — Elisha’s miracles, Naaman, and Jehoshaphat’s prayer. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Elisha and Jehoshaphat (2 Ki 2-5; 2 Chron 20): Elisha’s miracles, Naaman, and Jehoshaphat’s prayer. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review 2 Ki 2–5; 2 Chron 20: Choose a prophet or king and write 5 sentences describing how he showed dependence on Jehovah.\nKayla: Choose one account from Elisha and Jehoshaphat (2 Ki 2-5; 2 Chron 20) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Elisha and Jehoshaphat (2 Ki 2-5; 2 Chron 20) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Complete paragraph with accurate details.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: complete.\nRyan: dependence.", "151": "\"Here I Am, Send Me!\" — Isaiah Sees Jehovah's Holiness\nScripture: Is 6:1-13\nSong #75 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Dim the lights and describe a vision of a throne in a great temple.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Isaiah saw Jehovah on His throne (Is 6:1-4). 2. He said 'I am a man unclean in lips' (6:5). 3. A coal cleansed him and he said 'Here I am! Send me!' (6:6-8). Ask: Why did seeing Jehovah make him feel small?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Isaiah say when Jehovah asked who would go?\nAshelyn: Isaiah saw Jehovah’s glory and said, ‘Here I am! Send me!’ (Is 6:8). What had Isaiah just experienced that made him willing to serve?\nKayla: Why did seeing Jehovah’s holiness make Isaiah aware of his sin, and how did Jehovah respond?\nRyan: Is 6:9-10 is quoted in Matt 13:14-15. Explain what it means that people could hear but not understand.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ‘Here I am! Send me!’\nAshelyn (8th grade): He felt his sinfulness, was cleansed, and heard Jehovah’s need.\nKayla: holiness reveals our flaws; Jehovah forgave.\nRyan: hardened hearts cannot grasp truth.\nKayla: holiness reveals sin.\nRyan: people close their hearts.", "152": "Isaiah's Great Messianic Prophecies\nScripture: Is 7:14; 9:6-7; 11:1-10; 53:1-12\nSong #15 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Lay out cards of prophecies and fulfillments and ask the children to match them.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Is 7:14 – Immanuel. 2. Is 9:6-7 – Prince of Peace. 3. Is 53 – the suffering servant. Ask: How did Jesus match?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Name one title given to the Messiah.\nAshelyn: Isaiah 9:6-7 and 53:3-5 are Messianic prophecies. Choose one and explain how it shows Jesus’ role.\nKayla: Choose one Messianic prophecy from Isaiah and show how Jesus fulfilled it.\nRyan: Is 53:4-12 foretold the Messiah’s sacrifice. Explain how this supports the ransom doctrine.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Prince of Peace.\nAshelyn (8th grade): 9:6 – Prince of Peace, eternal Father role; 53 – suffered for our sins.\nKayla: accurate fulfillment.\nRyan: he bore our sins and was counted among sinners.\nKayla: accurate fulfillment.\nRyan: he bore our sins.", "153": "Jeremiah — Called Before Birth, Faithful to the End\nScripture: Jer 1:1-19; 20:1-18; Heb 11:37\nSong #153 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Have you ever felt too young for a big job?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jeremiah was called before birth (Jer 1:5). 2. He said 'I am just a boy' (1:6). 3. Jehovah promised 'I am with you' (1:8). Ask: How did Jehovah help him?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jeremiah say about his age?\nAshelyn: Jeremiah felt too young and afraid, yet Jehovah said ‘Do not say I am only a boy’ (Jer 1:6-8). How does Jehovah help young people serve Him today?\nKayla: Jehovah touched Jeremiah’s mouth and put words in it. How does Jehovah equip those who serve Him?\nRyan: Compare Jeremiah’s experience with Ps 148:12-13 and 1 Tim 4:12.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ‘I am a boy.’\nAshelyn (8th grade): Gives words, supports with His presence, uses their zeal (Ps 148:12-13).\nKayla: He gives words, courage and support.\nRyan: young people can praise and set an example.\nKayla: ‘I am with you.’\nRyan: young people can praise.", "154": "\"I Know the Plans I Have for You\" — Jeremiah's Letter\nScripture: Jer 29:1-14; 31:31-34\nSong #156 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Write a short letter to a faraway friend and read it aloud.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jeremiah wrote to the exiles in Babylon (Jer 29:1-7). 2. He told them to build homes and seek the city’s peace. 3. Jehovah promised restoration after 70 years (29:10-14). Ask: How do we live well while waiting?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jeremiah tell the exiles to do?\nAshelyn: Jeremiah 29:11 promises ‘a future and a hope’ — but it was written to people in exile for 70 years. What does that teach about what Jehovah’s ‘good plans’ look like?\nKayla: Jer 29:11 is often quoted. What was the context, and what does it teach about Jehovah’s promises?\nRyan: Jer 31:31-34 foretells a new covenant. How does Heb 8:8-12 apply it?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: build homes and gardens.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Jehovah’s plans are real and certain but may involve waiting and enduring.\nKayla: wait patiently, trust the 70 years.\nRyan: law written on hearts.\nKayla: plans of peace after 70 years.\nRyan: law written on hearts.", "155": "Week 31 Review\nScripture: Is 6-53; Jer 1; 29\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Isaiah and Jeremiah (Is 6-53; Jer 1; 29) — Isaiah’s commission, Messianic prophecies, and Jeremiah’s letter. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Isaiah and Jeremiah (Is 6-53; Jer 1; 29): Isaiah’s commission, Messianic prophecies, and Jeremiah’s letter. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Is 6–53; Jer 1; 29: List one thing Isaiah and Jeremiah had in common and one difference. What do both teach about speaking up for Jehovah?\nKayla: Choose one account from Isaiah and Jeremiah (Is 6-53; Jer 1; 29) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Isaiah and Jeremiah (Is 6-53; Jer 1; 29) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Common – obedient despite opposition; difference – Isaiah’s royal setting vs Jeremiah’s suffering. Courage comes from Jehovah.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: similarities and differences.\nRyan: fulfillment confirms Jehovah’s word.", "156": "Daniel's Resolve — Pure in a Corrupt World\nScripture: Dan 1:1-21; Rom 12:2; 1 Cor 15:33\nSong #34 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Offer vegetables and water and ask: 'Who would pass up the royal feast?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Daniel and his friends were taken to Babylon (Dan 1:1-4). 2. Daniel resolved not to defile himself (1:8). 3. After ten days they looked healthier (1:12-15). Ask: Why decide beforehand?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who refused the king’s food?\nAshelyn: Daniel ‘resolved in his heart’ not to defile himself (Dan 1:8). Why is deciding BEFORE the test so powerful? Write one decision you’ve made in advance.\nKayla: Daniel ‘resolved in his heart’ before the test came. Why does deciding beforehand protect us?\nRyan: Rom 12:2 says do not be molded by this system. How did Daniel show this?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Daniel.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Pre-decisions prevent compromise under pressure; accept honest example.\nKayla: it removes hesitation in the moment.\nRyan: renew the mind.\nKayla: to avoid compromise.\nRyan: don’t conform.", "157": "Nebuchadnezzar's Dream — World Empires Revealed\nScripture: Dan 2:1-49; Rev 17:10-11\nSong #22 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw a statue with a head of gold and feet of iron and clay.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Nebuchadnezzar dreamed of a huge statue (Dan 2:31-35). 2. Daniel explained the metals as kingdoms (2:36-43). 3. A stone cut without hands crushed it (2:44-45). Ask: What does the stone represent?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did the statue represent?\nAshelyn: Nebuchadnezzar’s statue had metals that declined in value and ended with clay and iron (Dan 2). What does this say about human governments, and what replaces them (Dan 2:44)?\nKayla: What does the stone in Dan 2:44-45 represent, and what will happen to human governments?\nRyan: Match the parts of the statue with the world powers: Babylon, Medo-Persia, Greece, Rome, Anglo-American.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: kingdoms.\nAshelyn (8th grade): They are temporary and divided; Jehovah’s Kingdom will crush them and stand forever.\nKayla: Jehovah’s Kingdom ending all human rule.\nRyan: gold, silver, bronze, iron, iron and clay.\nKayla: Jehovah’s Kingdom.\nRyan: it stands forever.", "158": "\"Even If He Does Not\" — The Fiery Furnace\nScripture: Dan 3:1-30; Heb 13:6\nSong #122 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold a lit flashlight and ask: 'Would you bow if everyone else did?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Nebuchadnezzar set up a gold image (Dan 3:1-6). 2. Three Hebrews refused to bow (3:12). 3. They were thrown in the furnace and saved (3:24-27). Ask: Why 'even if He does not'?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many men were thrown into the furnace?\nAshelyn: ‘Even if he does not’ (Dan 3:18). How does this show faith that doesn’t depend on being rescued? How can we show it when facing pressure?\nKayla: What does 'even if He does not' (Dan 3:18) show about their faith?\nRyan: Heb 13:6 says 'Jehovah is my helper.' How can we remember this under pressure?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: three.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Loyalty regardless of outcome; trust that Jehovah’s way is best.\nKayla: loyalty regardless of outcome.\nRyan: Jehovah is our helper; do not fear man.\nKayla: loyalty regardless of outcome.\nRyan: Jehovah is my helper.", "159": "Daniel in the Lions' Den — No Compromise in Prayer\nScripture: Dan 6:1-28; Acts 5:29\nSong #124 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Open a window and ask: 'Would you pray openly if you knew you would be punished?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Darius signed a decree against praying to anyone but him (Dan 6:7-9). 2. Daniel prayed as usual (6:10). 3. Jehovah shut the lions’ mouths (6:22). Ask: Why keep praying?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened to Daniel in the lions’ den?\nAshelyn: Daniel kept praying openly even after the decree (Dan 6:10). Why did he pray the same way when it could be dangerous? What is the difference between courage and stubbornness?\nKayla: Why did Daniel continue praying openly instead of hiding? Is that courage or foolishness?\nRyan: Acts 5:29 – 'We must obey God as ruler rather than men.' How is Daniel an example?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the lions did not harm him.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Consistent devotion; he didn’t provoke but would not compromise worship.\nKayla: his routine showed devotion without defiance.\nRyan: obey God over men when laws conflict.\nKayla: consistency.\nRyan: obey God as ruler.", "160": "Unit 8 Review + Celebration — Days 141-160\nScripture: Dan 1-6; Is 53\nSong #159 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Daniel (Dan 1-6) — Daniel’s resolve, the statue dream, the furnace, and the lions’ den. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Celebrate with a treat or special activity.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Daniel (Dan 1-6): Daniel’s resolve, the statue dream, the furnace, and the lions’ den. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Unit 8 Review: Compare Daniel, Shadrach/Meshach/Abednego. What three habits protected their faith in Babylon?\nKayla: Choose one account from Daniel (Dan 1-6) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Daniel (Dan 1-6) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Prayer, study of scriptures, loyalty with respect, close association with one another.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: similarities.\nRyan: integrity under pressure.", "161": "Messianic Prophecies Fulfilled — How We Know Jesus Is the One\nScripture: Luke 24:27; Is 7:14; 9:6; Mic 5:2; Dan 9:25\nSong #15 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Pass around a mystery box with clues and ask the children to guess the Messiah.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Luke 24:27 – Jesus explained what was written about him. 2. Prophecies: Bethlehem (Mic 5:2), virgin (Is 7:14), donkey (Zech 9:9). 3. These prove his identity. Ask: Why do prophecies matter?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where was Jesus born?\nAshelyn: Jesus said everything written about him in the Law, Prophets and Psalms must be fulfilled (Luke 24:44). Pick one prophecy (Mic 5:2, Is 7:14, Zech 9:9) and show how it was fulfilled.\nKayla: Choose two prophecies and show their fulfillment.\nRyan: Explain how fulfilled prophecy strengthens confidence in the Bible (Is 46:9-10).\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Bethlehem.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Micah 5:2 – Bethlehem; Is 7:14 – born to a virgin; Zech 9:9 – entered Jerusalem on a donkey.\nKayla: accurate pairs.\nRyan: Jehovah declares the end from the beginning.\nKayla: accurate pairs.\nRyan: pattern of fulfillment.", "162": "Jesus' Birth — Humble Beginning, Heavenly Significance\nScripture: Luke 1:26-56; 2:1-52; Matt 2:1-23\nSong #16 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'Who would be chosen to tell the world about the greatest event?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Gabriel told Mary she would bear Jesus (Luke 1:26-35). 2. Mary said 'Look! Jehovah’s slave girl!' (1:38). 3. Shepherds heard the good news (2:8-14). Ask: Why humble beginnings?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Which angel visited Mary?\nAshelyn: Mary responded to Gabriel with ‘Look! Jehovah’s slave girl! May it happen to me according to your statement’ (Luke 1:38). What does her attitude teach about accepting Jehovah’s assignments?\nKayla: How did Mary respond, and what does it teach about accepting Jehovah’s assignments?\nRyan: Phil 2:5-8 describes Jesus’ humility. How can we imitate it?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Gabriel.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Humility, willingness, trust despite not understanding everything.\nKayla: trust and willingness.\nRyan: empty yourself and serve.\nKayla: willing obedience.\nRyan: Jesus emptied himself.", "163": "Baptism and Temptation — Fully Tested, Fully Faithful\nScripture: Matt 3:13-4:11; Heb 4:15; Luke 4:1-13\nSong #17 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nASK: 'What do you do when someone dares you to do something wrong?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus was baptized and Jehovah approved (Matt 3:16-17). 2. Satan tempted him three times (4:1-11). 3. Jesus answered with 'It is written.' Ask: Why use Scripture?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many times did Satan tempt Jesus?\nAshelyn: Satan tempted Jesus three times and Jesus answered each with scripture (Matt 4:1-11). Why did he say ‘It is written’? Which verses would you memorize to answer your biggest temptation?\nKayla: How did Jesus defeat each temptation? Which Scripture would you use in your own?\nRyan: Heb 4:15 says Jesus sympathizes with our weaknesses. How does that encourage you?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: three.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Scripture is the sword of the spirit; accept personal verses.\nKayla: stones to bread – Deut 8:3; temple jump – Deut 6:16; worship – Deut 6:13.\nRyan: he understands and helps.\nKayla: ‘It is written.’\nRyan: sympathizes with weaknesses.", "164": "The Sermon on the Mount — The Kingdom Way of Life\nScripture: Matt 5:1-7:29\nSong #127 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Sit on a hillside or a pile of pillows and read Matt 5:3.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus began with the Beatitudes (Matt 5:3-12). 2. He taught salt, light and the golden rule. 3. He ended with the house on rock (7:24-27). Ask: What does a firm foundation look like?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Jesus give his Sermon?\nAshelyn: In the Sermon on the Mount Jesus said, ‘Happy are those conscious of their spiritual need’ (Matt 5:3). What does it mean to be conscious of spiritual need and how do you feed it daily?\nKayla: Choose one Beatitude and explain what it means.\nRyan: Compare the house on rock and sand. What choices build on rock?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: on a mountain.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Recognize dependence on Jehovah; daily Bible reading, meetings, prayer.\nKayla: accurate explanation.\nRyan: hearing and doing.\nKayla: accurate explanation.\nRyan: rock vs sand.", "165": "Week 33 Review\nScripture: Is 7; 9; Mic 5; Matt 2-7\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the early life and teaching of Jesus (Matt 2-7) — his birth, baptism, temptation, and the Sermon on the Mount. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the early life and teaching of Jesus (Matt 2-7): his birth, baptism, temptation, and the Sermon on the Mount. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Matt 2–7: List three things Jesus taught about how to treat others and explain how you could apply one of them this week.\nKayla: Choose one account from the early life and teaching of Jesus (Matt 2-7) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the early life and teaching of Jesus (Matt 2-7) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Examples: love enemies, forgive, don’t judge, golden rule.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: accurate list.\nRyan: Kingdom first.", "166": "Jesus' Miracles — Evidence of Kingdom Power\nScripture: John 2:1-11; Mark 5:1-43; John 11:1-44\nSong #104 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Pour water and ask: 'What if this became something else?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus turned water into wine at Cana (John 2:1-11). 2. He healed and raised the dead (Mark 5:21-43). 3. Miracles show compassion and power. Ask: Why miracles?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jesus turn into wine?\nAshelyn: Jesus’ first miracle was turning water to wine at a wedding (John 2:1-11). What does it show about his concern for people’s joy?\nKayla: What do Jesus’ miracles reveal about his personality and about the Kingdom?\nRyan: Compare Rev 21:3-4 with Jesus’ miracles.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: water.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He cared about ordinary needs and happiness; his power was used to help others.\nKayla: compassion and power.\nRyan: a preview of the Kingdom.\nKayla: power and compassion.\nRyan: foretaste of Kingdom.", "167": "The Model Prayer — Praying the Way Jesus Taught\nScripture: Matt 6:9-13; Luke 11:1-13; Ps 145:18-19\nSong #47 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Open your palms and recite the first lines of the Model Prayer.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus taught the Model Prayer (Matt 6:9-13). 2. It starts with Jehovah’s name and Kingdom. 3. Then we ask for daily needs and forgiveness. Ask: Why this order?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Say the first words of the Model Prayer.\nAshelyn: The Model Prayer begins ‘Our Father in the heavens, let your name be sanctified’ (Matt 6:9). Why does it begin there, and how does that shape the order of our own prayers?\nKayla: Why does the prayer put Jehovah’s name before our needs?\nRyan: Luke 11:5-13 teaches persistence in prayer. What does it promise?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ‘Our Father in the heavens.’\nAshelyn (8th grade): Putting Jehovah’s name and will first, then personal needs.\nKayla: He deserves first place.\nRyan: keep asking, seeking, knocking.\nKayla: priorities.\nRyan: ask and keep asking.", "168": "Jesus' Parables — Lessons for Every Level\nScripture: Luke 15:1-32; Matt 13:1-52; Luke 10:25-37\nSong #77 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Tell a short parable and ask what it means.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The lost sheep, coin and son (Luke 15). 2. The sower (Matt 13:1-23). 3. Parables reveal hearts. Ask: Who is each character?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who returned home in the parable?\nAshelyn: The parable of the prodigal son (Luke 15:11-32) has three characters. What does each teach about Jehovah’s mercy and our attitudes toward those who return?\nKayla: What do the three Luke 15 parables share?\nRyan: Why did Jesus teach in parables (Matt 13:10-15)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the prodigal son.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Father – mercy; younger son – repentance; older son – resentment to avoid.\nKayla: joy over one who repents.\nRyan: to reveal the responsive.\nKayla: joy over repentance.\nRyan: to reveal responsive hearts.", "169": "The Two Great Commandments — Love Is the Core\nScripture: Matt 22:34-40; 1 John 4:7-12; Mark 12:28-34\nSong #106 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw two hearts labeled 'Jehovah' and 'neighbor.'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The greatest commandment (Matt 22:37). 2. The second is like it. 3. All the Law hangs on these. Ask: How do they fit together?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What are the two greatest commands?\nAshelyn: Jesus said the two greatest commands are to love Jehovah and love your neighbor (Matt 22:37-40). Explain how each of the Ten Commandments connects to one of these two.\nKayla: Show how the Ten Commandments fit under these two.\nRyan: 1 John 4:7-12 – what does it say about love and God?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: love Jehovah and neighbor.\nAshelyn (8th grade): First four – love of Jehovah; the rest – love of neighbor.\nKayla: first four vs last six.\nRyan: love comes from God.\nKayla: first four/last six.\nRyan: love comes from Jehovah.", "170": "Week 34 Review\nScripture: Matt 5-7; 13; 22; Luke 15\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Jesus’ teaching (Matt 5-7; 13; 22; Luke 15) — the Sermon, parables, and the two greatest commandments. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Jesus’ teaching (Matt 5-7; 13; 22; Luke 15): the Sermon, parables, and the two greatest commandments. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Matt 5–7; 13; 22; Luke 15: Choose one parable or teaching and put it in a modern setting using a story of your own (4–6 sentences).\nKayla: Choose one account from Jesus’ teaching (Matt 5-7; 13; 22; Luke 15) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Jesus’ teaching (Matt 5-7; 13; 22; Luke 15) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Creative retelling that keeps the lesson accurate.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: faithful retelling.\nRyan: theme comparison.", "171": "The Last Supper — Jesus' Final Hours With His Apostles\nScripture: Matt 26:17-30; John 13:1-38; 1 Cor 11:23-26\nSong #19 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Set the table and share bread in an upper-room mood.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus washed the apostles’ feet (John 13:1-17). 2. He instituted the Memorial (Matt 26:26-28). 3. He gave a new command to love (John 13:34-35). Ask: What humbles us?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who washed the apostles’ feet?\nAshelyn: At the Last Supper, Jesus washed the apostles’ feet (John 13:1-17). What did this teach them about leadership, and who do you serve in a similar way?\nKayla: What lesson did Jesus teach about leadership through foot washing?\nRyan: John 13:34-35 – how does love identify disciples?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: Jesus.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Humility and service; accept personal examples.\nKayla: leaders serve.\nRyan: love marks true Christians.\nKayla: leaders serve.\nRyan: love marks disciples.", "172": "\"Your Will, Not Mine\" — Gethsemane and the Arrest\nScripture: Matt 26:36-75; Luke 22:39-53\nSong #44 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Dim the lights and read Luke 22:42 quietly.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus prayed in Gethsemane (Luke 22:39-44). 2. Judas betrayed him with a kiss. 3. Peter denied him (Matt 26:69-75). Ask: What helped Jesus?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Jesus pray?\nAshelyn: In Gethsemane Jesus said, ‘Not my will, but yours’ (Luke 22:42). What does this show about the relationship between prayer and obedience?\nKayla: What does ‘not my will, but yours’ teach about prayer?\nRyan: Heb 5:7-8 says he learned obedience. Explain.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: in a garden.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Prayer aligns our will with Jehovah’s even when it is difficult.\nKayla: aligning our will with Jehovah’s.\nRyan: obedience through suffering.\nKayla: submission.\nRyan: learned obedience.", "173": "The Trial and the Stake — The Ransom Sacrifice\nScripture: Matt 27:1-56; Heb 9:11-14; 1 John 2:1-2\nSong #20 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Hold a price tag and ask: 'What price would buy back everything?'\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus was tried and executed (Matt 27). 2. He gave his life as a ransom (Matt 20:28). 3. Heb 9:11-14 – his blood cleanses. Ask: Why was it necessary?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jesus give for us?\nAshelyn: Hebrews 9:14 says Christ’s blood cleanses our conscience. In your own words, explain what ransom means and why it was necessary (Rom 5:12,18-19).\nKayla: Explain the ransom using Rom 5:12,18-19.\nRyan: Why couldn’t an imperfect man provide the ransom (Ps 49:7-9)?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: his life.\nAshelyn (8th grade): A corresponding price to buy back what Adam lost; Jesus is the ‘last Adam’ giving perfect human life.\nKayla: perfect life for perfect life.\nRyan: no imperfect human could redeem another.\nKayla: corresponding price.\nRyan: one man’s obedience.", "174": "The Resurrection — Jehovah's Most Powerful Act\nScripture: Matt 28:1-20; 1 Cor 15:1-8; Acts 1:1-11; John 20:24-29\nSong #27 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Place an empty box on the table and ask what it reminds the children of.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The tomb was empty (Matt 28:1-6). 2. Jesus appeared to many (1 Cor 15:3-8). 3. The resurrection gives hope. Ask: Why is it central?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What was found empty?\nAshelyn: Paul says that if Christ was not raised our faith is useless (1 Cor 15:14). Why is the resurrection so central, and what hope does it give for loved ones who died?\nKayla: Why does Paul say our faith would be useless without the resurrection (1 Cor 15:14)?\nRyan: John 5:28-29 – what hope does it give for the dead?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: the tomb.\nAshelyn (8th grade): It confirms Jesus’ victory over death and guarantees the future resurrection (John 5:28-29).\nKayla: it proves Jesus’ victory over death.\nRyan: the dead will hear his voice and come out.\nKayla: hope for loved ones.\nRyan: faith vain without it.", "175": "Week 35 Review\nScripture: Matt 26-28; 1 Cor 15; Acts 1\nSong #0 — Child's Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at Jesus’ final days (Matt 26-28; 1 Cor 15) — the Last Supper, Gethsemane, the stake, and the resurrection. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Then play a quick review game: one person describes a person or event without naming it and the others guess.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review Jesus’ final days (Matt 26-28; 1 Cor 15): the Last Supper, Gethsemane, the stake, and the resurrection. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Review Matt 26–28; 1 Cor 15; Acts 1: Put these in order — Last Supper, Gethsemane, trial, stake, resurrection, ascension — and next to each write one lesson.\nKayla: Choose one account from Jesus’ final days (Matt 26-28; 1 Cor 15) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from Jesus’ final days (Matt 26-28; 1 Cor 15) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Accurate order with a thoughtful lesson for each.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: order.\nRyan: ransom, resurrection, commission.", "176": "The Kingdom of God — What It Is and Why Everything Depends on It\nScripture: Dan 2:44; Rev 11:15; Matt 6:10; 24:14\nSong #22 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Draw a crown over the earth and ask what changes when a good king rules.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Dan 2:44 – the Kingdom will crush all others. 2. Rev 11:15 – it belongs to Jehovah and Christ. 3. Matt 6:10 – we pray for it. Ask: What will it do?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What do we pray for in the Model Prayer?\nAshelyn: Jesus told us to pray ‘Let your kingdom come’ (Matt 6:10). List three things the Kingdom will do (Rev 21:3-4; Ps 72:7-8; Is 11:6-9), and explain why you look forward to one.\nKayla: List three things the Kingdom will do and explain why you look forward to one.\nRyan: Why is the Kingdom the answer to the issue of Jehovah’s sovereignty?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: ‘Let your Kingdom come.’\nAshelyn (8th grade): End suffering and death, bring peace, restore earth; accept personal answer.\nKayla: end suffering, bring peace, restore earth.\nRyan: vindicate Jehovah’s rulership.\nKayla: end suffering.\nRyan: vindicates Jehovah’s sovereignty.", "177": "Paradise Earth — Jehovah's Original Purpose Restored\nScripture: Ps 37:29; Is 65:21-25; Rev 21:1-5; John 5:28-29\nSong #139 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show pictures of a garden and ask what a perfect earth would look like.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Ps 37:29 – the righteous will possess the earth. 2. Is 65:21-25 – homes, gardens, peace. 3. Rev 21:3-4 – no more death. Ask: What will you do?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What will the earth be like?\nAshelyn: Isaiah 65:21-25 describes homes, gardens, and peace in the earth. How does this fulfill Jehovah’s original purpose for Adam and Eve (Gen 1:28)?\nKayla: Compare the original purpose in Gen 1:28 with the promises in Is 65.\nRyan: Why will Paradise fulfill Jehovah’s original purpose?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: peaceful and beautiful.\nAshelyn (8th grade): He promised a paradise filled with righteous people caring for the earth.\nKayla: filled with righteous people caring for it.\nRyan: Jehovah’s purpose does not fail.\nKayla: same purpose restored.\nRyan: earth filled with righteous.", "178": "Dedication and Baptism — What It Means to Give Your Life to Jehovah\nScripture: Matt 28:19-20; Rom 6:3-4; Luke 9:23\nSong #50 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Pour water into a bowl and talk about what water baptism means.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jesus commanded disciples to baptize (Matt 28:19-20). 2. Dedication precedes baptism. 3. Baptism symbolizes dedication (Rom 6:3-4). Ask: What does dedication mean?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What is baptism a symbol of?\nAshelyn: Jesus told his followers to make disciples, baptizing them (Matt 28:19-20). Write 4–5 sentences on the difference between simply believing and dedicating your life to Jehovah.\nKayla: Explain the difference between believing and dedicating your life to Jehovah.\nRyan: List steps leading to baptism: knowledge, faith, repentance, conversion, dedication.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: a symbol of dedication.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Dedication is a personal vow of devoting oneself to Jehovah; baptism publicly symbolizes it.\nKayla: a personal promise to Jehovah.\nRyan: knowledge, faith, repentance, conversion, dedication, baptism.\nKayla: personal vow.\nRyan: knowledge, faith, repentance, dedication, baptism.", "179": "Living as Kingdom Citizens Today — Faith That Works\nScripture: James 2:14-26; 1 Cor 15:58; Gal 5:22-23\nSong #143 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: Show a tool and ask what happens if it is never used.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jas 2:14-26 – faith without works is dead. 2. 1 Cor 15:58 – be steadfast. 3. Works prove faith. Ask: What are examples?\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: What are works?\nAshelyn: James 2:17 says faith without works is dead. Give two examples of ‘works’ that show faith in daily life for a young person, and explain how each makes your faith visible.\nKayla: Give two works that show faith for a teenager.\nRyan: Why can’t works replace faith?\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: doing good things.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Preaching, honesty, kindness, regular study, resisting peer pressure.\nKayla: preaching, kindness, honesty.\nRyan: works show living faith.\nKayla: preaching, kindness.\nRyan: lifeless.", "180": "GRAND FINALE — Year-End Celebration + Family Testimonies\nScripture: Prov 22:6; 2 Tim 3:14-17; Phil 4:13\nSong #155 — \n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have reached a milestone! Today we look back at the whole year (Genesis through the Kingdom) — creation, the patriarchs, Moses, the kings, the prophets, and Jesus. Each person gets one minute to tell the family the best thing they learned, and one thing they still want to know.' Celebrate with a treat or special activity.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Review the whole year (Genesis through the Kingdom): creation, the patriarchs, Moses, the kings, the prophets, and Jesus. 2. Ask each child to retell one account in their own words. 3. Ask: What does this section teach us about Jehovah’s personality, and how does it change the way you live? Finish by singing or listening to the song of the day.\n\n\n❓ APPLICATION QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell us your favorite story from this review. Who was in it, and what did Jehovah do?\nAshelyn: Grand Finale! Write a one-page testimony: what you learned about Jehovah this year, a favorite lesson or story, and one goal for next year. Be ready to share with the family.\nKayla: Choose one account from the whole year (Genesis through the Kingdom) and write 4-5 sentences: what happened, what choice a person made, what resulted, and the lesson you can apply.\nRyan: Write a paragraph connecting two accounts from the whole year (Genesis through the Kingdom) under a single theme (faith, obedience, mercy, justice, or courage). Support it with at least two scriptures.\n\n\n✅ ANSWER KEY — Teacher Reference Only\nMykah: celebrate any accurate retelling with a drawing or acting.\nAshelyn (8th grade): Complete, sincere testimony with all three components.\nKayla: all four parts (event, choice, result, lesson) with accurate details.\nRyan: a clear theme backed by two scriptures and examples from both accounts.\nKayla: sincere.\nRyan: goals.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n________________\n\n\nQUICK REFERENCE — ALL RESOURCES\n•  Full Songbook — All 163 Songs\n•  jw.org Main Library\n•  wol.jw.org — Watchtower Online Library\n•  Insight on the Scriptures Vol. 1 (A-J)\n•  Insight on the Scriptures Vol. 2 (J-Z)\n•  Research Guide for Jehovah's Witnesses\n•  NWT Bible — wol.jw.org\n•  Daily Text — Examining the Scriptures Daily\n•  My Book of Bible Stories (audio)\n•  JW Broadcasting — Videos\n•  Watchtower Study Edition — Current\n•  Meeting Workbook — Current Week\n\n\n“Train a child in the way he should go; even when he grows old, he will not depart from it.”\n— Proverbs 22:6\njw.org • wol.jw.org  —  Page", "13": "Enoch — Walking With Jehovah\nScripture: Gen 5:21-24; Heb 11:5; Jude 14-15\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Imagine being so close to Jehovah that He simply took you from this life without your seeing death. That is how the Bible describes one man.' Ask: What do you think it means to ‘walk with God’?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Enoch lived in a world turning away from Jehovah and still walked with Him. 2. Heb 11:5 — he had the witness that he pleased God. 3. Jude 14-15 — he boldly warned the ungodly. Ask: Who around you walks with Jehovah every day, and what does it look like?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What does it mean to ‘walk with Jehovah’? Name one thing you do each day to stay close to Him.\nAshelyn: Enoch spoke boldly about Jehovah’s judgment even though most people ignored him (Jude 14-15). What made it possible for him to keep walking with Jehovah in a corrupt world? Write 3 sentences.\nKayla: Heb 11:5 says Enoch ‘had the witness that he pleased God well.’ What actions could have given him that witness? Compare to Micah 6:8.\nRyan: Enoch’s prophecy in Jude 14-15 is not recorded in Genesis. What does this tell us about the reliability of Jude’s information, and how does it support the inspiration of the Bible (2 Tim 3:16)?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Prayer, Bible reading, obeying parents – celebrate any sincere answer.\nAshelyn: He had faith, regular communication with Jehovah, and loyalty over popularity. Look for specific, reasoned answers.\nKayla: Faith, obedience, justice, loyalty, humility – any with scripture support.\nRyan: Jude was inspired by holy spirit and drew on accurate sources; the Bible’s authors had access beyond Genesis.", "14": "Noah’s World — Violence and Corruption\nScripture: Gen 6:1-8, 11-13; Matt 24:37-39\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Imagine the whole earth being so filled with violence that Jehovah says: This must end.' Pause. 'But one man found favor.' Ask: What do you think made Noah different?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Rebel angels took human wives and produced violent Nephilim (Gen 6:1-4; Jude 6). 2. Every inclination of the heart was bad all day long (Gen 6:5). 3. Noah ‘walked with the true God’ (Gen 6:9). 4. Jesus compared our day to Noah’s (Matt 24:37-39). Ask: What similarities do you see?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Why was Jehovah sad about the earth in Noah’s day?\nAshelyn: Gen 6:5 says every inclination of man’s heart was bad all the time. What does this tell us about the importance of guarding our thoughts (Phil 4:8)? Give two practical ways.\nKayla: Matt 24:37-39 compares our time to Noah’s. List three similarities and explain what Jesus wants us to do.\nRyan: How do Jude 6 and 2 Pet 2:4 help explain the ‘sons of God’ in Gen 6:2? Why does this matter for understanding why the Flood was necessary?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Violence and wickedness; Jehovah cares about what happens to people.\nAshelyn: Thoughts lead to actions; guard what we watch/listen to, fill mind with good things.\nKayla: Daily life going on, ignoring warnings, violence. Jesus wants us to ‘keep on the watch.’\nRyan: Angels who left their assigned place corrupted mankind, producing violence.", "15": "Noah Builds the Ark — Faith in Action\nScripture: Gen 6:9-22; Heb 11:7; 2 Pet 2:5\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Imagine your dad telling you to build a ship on dry land, far from the sea, and the weather forecast says nothing.' Ask: How would your neighbors react?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The ark: about 450 feet long, three decks (Gen 6:14-16). 2. Noah ‘did according to all that God commanded’ (Gen 6:22). 3. He was ‘a preacher of righteousness’ (2 Pet 2:5). Ask: Why was building AND preaching both part of his faith?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Why did Noah build the ark?\nAshelyn: Heb 11:7 says Noah ‘condemned the world’ by his faith. How did his actions show the world’s wrong course? What could your actions show others?\nKayla: Noah followed the instructions ‘exactly’ (Gen 6:22). Why does Jehovah value detailed obedience? Give an example from your own life.\nRyan: Using Heb 11:7 and 2 Pet 2:5, outline Noah’s mission in three parts: warn, build, preserve. What does this teach about the Christian ministry today?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: To obey Jehovah and save his family and animals.\nAshelyn: Faith shows in works; by doing right when others don’t, we show a better way.\nKayla: Complete obedience shows love; Accept personal examples.\nRyan: Warn (preach), build (obey), preserve (family, animals). Today: preach, keep clean, preserve life by Jehovah’s direction.", "16": "The Door Closes — Entering the Ark\nScripture: Gen 7:1-16; Matt 24:38-39\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Imagine standing inside a giant ship hearing the animals walk in two by two. And then Jehovah Himself shuts the door.' Ask: Who closed the door and why does that matter?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Noah’s family and the animals entered by Jehovah’s command (Gen 7:1-9). 2. After seven days rain began (7:10-12). 3. ‘Jehovah shut the door’ (7:16) — protection and finality. Ask: What does the closing of the door teach about timing?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Who shut the door of the ark?\nAshelyn: The people kept eating, drinking and marrying until the Flood came (Matt 24:38-39). Why is ignoring warnings dangerous? Give a modern example.\nKayla: Jehovah gave seven days between the animals boarding and the rain (Gen 7:4,10). What does this tell us about His patience and the opportunity to change?\nRyan: Compare the closing of the ark’s door with the ‘shut door’ in Matt 25:10-12. What lesson about preparedness is shared by both?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Jehovah shut it.\nAshelyn: People had no excuse; we must take warnings seriously.\nKayla: Jehovah is patient (2 Pet 3:9), giving time to repent, but there is a limit.\nRyan: Both stress preparedness: act before the door closes.", "17": "The Flood — Jehovah Destroys and Preserves\nScripture: Gen 7:17–8:14; 2 Pet 2:5; 3:5-7\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Forty days of rain. Water covering the highest mountains. And one boat.' Ask: How would you feel inside the ark?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. The Flood covered the mountains (Gen 7:19-20). 2. ‘God remembered Noah’ (8:1). 3. Noah sent out a raven and doves to test the waters (8:6-12). Ask: What does it mean that God ‘remembered’ Noah?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Name two animals Noah sent out of the ark.\nAshelyn: 2 Pet 3:5-7 says people ‘deliberately ignored’ the Flood. How do some people today dismiss the Flood account? What evidence or reasoning can you use to defend it? (Mention Jesus’ belief in it – Matt 24:37-39.)\nKayla: Gen 8:1 says ‘God remembered Noah.’ Does Jehovah ever forget? Explain what ‘remembered’ means in the Bible.\nRyan: Many ancient cultures have flood accounts. How does this support the Bible’s record, and how does Genesis differ from the others in its details and purpose?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Raven and dove.\nAshelyn: Jesus and Peter treat it as real history; widespread flood legends and Bible’s precise record.\nKayla: ‘Remembered’ means acting on behalf of someone (Ex 2:24).\nRyan: Shared memory of a global event; Genesis gives precise dates, dimensions, and a moral purpose.", "18": "A New Start — The Rainbow Covenant\nScripture: Gen 8:15–9:17\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'After the longest rainstorm in history, Jehovah put something in the sky.' Ask: What does a rainbow remind you of?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Noah’s first act: an altar and sacrifice (8:20). 2. Jehovah promised never again to destroy all flesh (9:11). 3. The rainbow is the sign of the covenant (9:12-17). 4. Blood is sacred (9:4). Ask: Why was Noah’s first act worship?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What is the rainbow a reminder of?\nAshelyn: Noah’s first act was to build an altar to Jehovah (Gen 8:20). What does this show about his priorities? What should be the first thing we do after Jehovah saves us from trouble?\nKayla: Gen 9:4-6 introduces the sacredness of blood and life. Explain how this commandment has continued into Christian times (Acts 15:28-29).\nRyan: Why was a sign like the rainbow needed for the covenant? Compare it with other signs: the Sabbath (Ex 31:16-17) and circumcision (Gen 17:11).\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Jehovah’s promise never to flood the earth.\nAshelyn: Worship and gratitude first. Accept personal reflection.\nKayla: Blood represents life; Acts 15 repeated the command to abstain from blood.\nRyan: Signs are visible reminders of Jehovah’s promises and our obligations.", "19": "The Tower of Babel — Pride and Confusion\nScripture: Gen 11:1-9; Acts 17:26-27\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Imagine everyone on earth speaks one language, and they decide to make a name for themselves.' Ask: What could go wrong?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Nimrod ruled as a rebel against Jehovah (Gen 10:8-10). 2. They built to ‘make a celebrated name’ (11:4). 3. Jehovah confused their language and scattered them (11:7-9). Ask: Why was their goal wrong?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What did Jehovah do when the people built the tower?\nAshelyn: They wanted ‘a celebrated name’ for themselves rather than glorifying Jehovah. How is that similar to today’s social media culture? How can we avoid it?\nKayla: Compare the confusion of languages at Babel with the gift of tongues at Pentecost (Acts 2:5-11). What contrast does it show about Jehovah’s purposes?\nRyan: Babel gave rise to Babylon, a symbol of false religion (Rev 17:5). Trace the connection between Babel and Babylon the Great.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: He mixed up their languages and spread them out.\nAshelyn: Self-promotion vs. giving Jehovah glory (1 Cor 10:31). Accept thoughtful reflections.\nKayla: Babel divided; Pentecost united in understanding the good news.\nRyan: Babel’s rebellion started false worship; the pattern carried into Babylon and is pictured in Revelation.", "20": "Unit 1 Review + Celebration — Days 1-20\nScripture: Gen 1–11; Ps 83:18\nSong: Child’s Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'We have walked from creation to Babel in twenty days. Let’s see what you remember.' Play a quick quiz game (Who said it? Which came first?).\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nReview: Jehovah’s name, six creative periods, Adam and Eve, the Fall, Cain and Abel, Enoch, Noah, the Flood, Babel. Have each child choose their favorite account and tell it in two minutes. Celebrate with a treat.\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Tell the best story from the past 20 days in your own words.\nAshelyn: Choose ONE character from Days 1-20 whose faith you admire. Explain in 5 sentences what they did, what it cost, and what you can imitate.\nKayla: Which account in Unit 1 shows Jehovah’s patience most, and which shows His justice most? Support with scriptures.\nRyan: Prepare a 2-minute outline showing the theme from Gen 1–11: Jehovah’s purpose, mankind’s rebellion, His response. Include Gen 3:15.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Celebrate any accurate retelling.\nAshelyn: Look for specific details, cost, and application.\nKayla: Patience – the 120 years before the Flood; justice – the Flood and Babel.\nRyan: Purpose: earth filled with righteous people; rebellion: Eden → Flood → Babel; response: promise of the Seed, preserving Noah, scattering.", "21": "Nations From Noah — Shem, Ham and Japheth\nScripture: Gen 9:18-10:32; Acts 17:26\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Everyone alive today comes from three brothers.' Ask: What does that tell us about the human family?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Gen 10 lists the nations from Noah’s sons. 2. Acts 17:26 — from one man Jehovah made every nation. 3. Racism is unscriptural. Ask: Why does the unity of mankind matter for the way we treat people?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: How many sons did Noah have? Can you name them?\nAshelyn: Acts 17:26 says all nations come ‘from one man.’ How should this affect how we view people of different races and cultures?\nKayla: Gen 10 lists 70 nations. Why would Jehovah include such a detailed list? What does it show about the Bible’s historical reliability?\nRyan: Nimrod is called ‘a mighty hunter in opposition to Jehovah’ (Gen 10:9). Research what this phrase implies and how it connects to later Babylonian worship.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Shem, Ham, Japheth.\nAshelyn: All humans are relatives; no race is superior. Accept thoughtful statements.\nKayla: The list reflects real peoples and places documented by archaeology and history.\nRyan: ‘In opposition to Jehovah’ shows defiance; he founded Babel and Babylonian rule.", "22": "Job — A Faithful Man Is Tested\nScripture: Job 1:1-22; 2:1-10; Jas 5:11\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Imagine losing everything you own and everyone you love in a single day. What would you say to Jehovah?' Pause. Read Job 1:21.\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Job was ‘blameless and upright.’ 2. Satan challenged Job’s motive (1:9-11) — the question of integrity. 3. Job did not sin with his lips. Ask: Why was Satan’s accusation actually an attack on Jehovah?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: What happened to Job’s family and animals?\nAshelyn: Satan claimed Job served Jehovah only for what he got (Job 1:9-11). How does this challenge apply to all of us? What proves our service is out of love?\nKayla: Job’s wife said ‘Curse God and die’ (Job 2:9). How did Job respond and what can we learn about handling pressure from close ones?\nRyan: Job’s test revolves around the issue of integrity and Jehovah’s sovereignty. Explain how Job’s case ties to Prov 27:11.\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: He lost them in a single day through Satan’s attacks.\nAshelyn: Love for Jehovah regardless of blessings; integrity under trial.\nKayla: He kindly corrected her: ‘Shall we accept only good?’ Stay calm, loyal.\nRyan: Job’s integrity answers Satan’s taunt and brings joy to Jehovah’s heart.", "23": "Job — Jehovah Restores His Servant\nScripture: Job 38:1-11; 42:1-17; Jas 5:11\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'After all his suffering, Jehovah spoke to Job from a windstorm — and He asked Job questions.' Ask: Why would Jehovah ask questions instead of giving explanations?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Jehovah’s questions show His wisdom and power (Job 38). 2. Job humbled himself (42:1-6). 3. Jehovah restored double (42:10). 4. James 5:11 — Jehovah is tender and merciful. Ask: What did Job learn about Jehovah?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: How did Jehovah reward Job at the end?\nAshelyn: Job said, ‘I had heard of you… but now my eye sees you’ (Job 42:5). What difference is there between knowing about Jehovah and truly knowing Him? How can you deepen your relationship?\nKayla: Job’s three companions gave bad advice. What mistakes did they make in comforting Job? Contrast their approach with how we should help those suffering.\nRyan: Job 42:7 shows Jehovah’s approval of Job and rebuke of Eliphaz. How does this show Jehovah’s view of those who misrepresent Him?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: He gave Job twice as much and a new family.\nAshelyn: Personal relationship through prayer, study, and experiences. Accept personal answers.\nKayla: They assumed sin caused suffering, lectured instead of listening. We should be compassionate, listen.\nRyan: Jehovah cares about His reputation; misrepresenting Him is serious.", "24": "Terah’s Family — The Road to Abram\nScripture: Gen 11:10-32; Acts 7:2-4; Josh 24:2\nSong: Open in JW Library\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'Abram’s family worshipped other gods in Ur of the Chaldeans. Something changed.' Ask: How can someone raised around false worship come to know Jehovah?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\n1. Shem to Abram — ten generations. 2. Terah’s household served other gods (Josh 24:2). 3. Jehovah appeared to Abram in Ur (Acts 7:2). Ask: What does this show about Jehovah’s ability to reach honest hearts?\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Where did Abram’s family live?\nAshelyn: Abram was raised in a family that worshipped idols, yet he became ‘the friend of Jehovah.’ What helps a person choose differently from their family’s religion? Use Acts 7:2 and Heb 11:8.\nKayla: Terah started toward Canaan but stopped at Haran (Gen 11:31). What might have caused him to stop short? What can this teach us about finishing what we begin?\nRyan: Compare the genealogy in Gen 11 with Luke 3:34-36. Why did Luke include these names in Jesus’ line?\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Ur of the Chaldeans.\nAshelyn: Honest heart, Jehovah’s direct appeal, willingness to act.\nKayla: Comfort, attachments, lack of faith; perseverance matters (Luke 9:62).\nRyan: Shows Jesus’ legal and physical descent from Abraham and Shem; Bible records are accurate.", "25": "Week 5 Review\nScripture: Gen 5–11; Job 1–42\nSong: Child’s Choice\n\n\n🎣 HOOK\nSAY: 'This week we met Enoch, Noah, the builders of Babel, and Job.' Ask: Who has Jehovah protected, and who did He correct?\n\n\n🌱 TEACH\nReview each account and have each child present a one-minute summary. Optional: draw a timeline from Adam to Abram. Celebrate with a favorite song.\n\n\n❓ QUESTIONS\nMykah: Share your drawing or favorite part of the week.\nAshelyn: Write 4-5 sentences comparing Noah and Job: How did each stay faithful, and what trial did each face?\nKayla: Create a timeline from Adam to Abram marking five key events with dates. Which period shows the greatest decline?\nRyan: Present a brief defense of the Flood account using three lines of evidence (Bible, Jesus’ testimony, global flood traditions).\n\n\n✅ KEY\nMykah: Celebrate warmly.\nAshelyn: Noah: obeyed building ark, mocked; Job: lost everything, kept integrity.\nKayla: Timeline with accurate dates.\nRyan: Strong use of 2 Pet 2:5; Matt 24:37-39; flood traditions."};

// Keep the day titles & scripture shown in the app in step with the Teacher Lecture scripts.
(function () {
  try {
    if (typeof DKA_CURRICULUM === 'undefined') return;
    Object.keys(DKA_SCRIPTS).forEach(function (k) {
      var row = DKA_CURRICULUM[k - 1]; if (!row || row[4] !== 'See 180-day document') return;
      var L = String(DKA_SCRIPTS[k]).split('\n'); if (!L[0]) return;
      row[3] = L[0]; var sc = /^Scripture:\s*(.*)$/.exec(L[1] || ''); if (sc) row[4] = sc[1];
    });
  } catch (e) {}
})();
